/* Read Anyone · Ask knowledge base. Hand-written study notes in our own words on Vanessa Van Edwards, Vinh Giang and Chase Hughes, with source refs. Built by research/build_ask_kb.py from research/ask-kb/*.txt. */
window.ASK_KB={"v":2,"n":234,"entries":[{"i":"all-sincerity","w":["ra"],"c":"ideas","t":"The sincerity test","a":["sincerity test","sincerity","is this manipulation","is it manipulative","ethics","am i manipulating","ethical"],"s":"Read Anyone's rule for every line: would the other person, knowing your full intent, agree that what you did was in their interest? If not, don't use it. It comes from NCI material about Hughes's work (a secondary source), and it's why covert techniques are left out.","d":"","u":"Before you use a line, ask: would I be happy for them to know exactly why I said it?","e":[],"r":["lo-overview","all-six-needs","all-read-anyone"],"f":["What does Read Anyone leave out?","Are the six needs labels?"],"src":[{"l":"Read Anyone handbook §2"}]},{"i":"all-read-anyone","w":["ra"],"c":"about","t":"About Read Anyone and this Ask section","a":["what is this","about this app","read anyone","how does ask work","sources","is this ai","where do answers come from"],"s":"Read Anyone is a simple line generator built on three teachers: Vanessa Van Edwards, Chase Hughes and Vinh Giang. This Ask section answers from a hand-written set of notes on their books, videos and interviews — in plain English, with sources. It runs on your phone with no AI and no web search, so if something isn't covered, it says so rather than guessing.","d":"","u":"Ask a question, a word or \"what does X mean\", and use the filter to stick to one teacher.","e":[],"r":["all-sincerity","vve-about","vg-about","ch-about"],"f":["Who is Vanessa Van Edwards?","Who is Vinh Giang?"],"src":[{"l":"Read Anyone technique inventory https://github.com/mhccog/read-anyone/blob/main/research/techniques-inventory.md"}]},{"i":"all-follow-ups","w":["all"],"c":"listening","t":"Why follow-up questions work","a":["follow up questions","follow ups","ask more questions","likeable","why questions work","huang"],"s":"All three teachers push follow-up questions, and research agrees: people who ask more questions, especially follow-ups, are liked more (Huang and colleagues, 2017). Vanessa's version is \"Tell me more?\", Hughes's is the Ask step of his small-talk loop, and Vinh asks three follow-ups before talking about himself.","d":"","u":"Ask two follow-ups before you tell your own story.","e":[],"r":["vve-tell-me-more","ch-smalltalk","vg-three-followups"],"f":["What is the small-talk formula?","What does Vinh say about strong emotion?"],"src":[{"l":"Read Anyone handbook §4"},{"l":"Huang et al. 2017 https://www.hbs.edu/faculty/Pages/item.aspx?num=52115"}]},{"i":"all-liking-gap","w":["vve"],"c":"ideas","t":"The liking gap","a":["liking gap","do they like me","they didnt like me","overthinking after conversation"],"s":"After a conversation, people usually liked you more than you think (Boothby and colleagues, 2018). Vanessa uses this to encourage you to signal liking more clearly and to assume people already like you a little.","d":"","u":"If you enjoyed it, say so — \"I really enjoyed that.\"","e":[],"r":["vve-signal-liking","all-assume-liked"],"f":["How do I signal liking?","How do I end a conversation?"],"src":[{"l":"Read Anyone handbook §4"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 1"}]},{"i":"all-assume-liked","w":["vve"],"c":"confidence","t":"Assume they already like you","a":["assume they like me","social anxiety","fear of rejection","power of assumption"],"s":"Vanessa's \"power of assumption\": walk in assuming people already like you a little more than you fear. The liking gap research suggests that's often true.","d":"","u":"Before you walk in, tell yourself \"They're going to like me.\"","e":[],"r":["all-liking-gap","vve-pygmalion"],"f":["What is the liking gap?","What is the Pygmalion effect?"],"src":[{"l":"Read Anyone handbook §5.1"}]},{"i":"all-end-on-purpose","w":["vve"],"c":"exits","t":"End on purpose","a":["when to end a conversation","conversations go on too long","ending","mastroianni"],"s":"A large study found conversations almost never end when both people want them to — only about 2% of the time (Mastroianni and colleagues, 2021). So ending warmly and on purpose is a kindness. Vanessa's exit: something in the future, a specific kindness, then go.","d":"","u":"Leave on a high rather than waiting for it to fizzle.","e":[],"r":["vve-exit","vve-anchor-touch"],"f":["What is the graceful exit?","How do I leave someone who won't stop talking?"],"src":[{"l":"Read Anyone handbook §4"},{"l":"Mastroianni et al. 2021, as cited in the handbook"}]},{"i":"all-warmth-first","w":["vve"],"c":"ideas","t":"Warmth first, then competence","a":["warmth first","first impression","trust","be liked and respected"],"s":"People size you up on two questions: can I trust you, and can I rely on you? Research (Fiske, Cuddy and Glick, 2007) shows warmth is judged first. So lead with interest, their name and a real smile, then make one clear point.","d":"","u":"In the first minute, be warm; in the second, be clear.","e":[],"r":["vve-charisma","vve-permission-warmth"],"f":["What is charisma?","What is permission warmth?"],"src":[{"l":"Read Anyone handbook §4"},{"l":"Fiske, Cuddy & Glick 2007 https://doi.org/10.1016/j.tics.2006.11.005"}]},{"i":"all-label-feeling","w":["vve","vg"],"c":"listening","t":"Naming a feeling calms it","a":["label emotions","name feelings","it seems like","labelling","affect labeling"],"s":"Putting a feeling into words can reduce its intensity (Lieberman and colleagues, 2007). Vanessa uses it to calm people (\"never say calm down\") and Vinh uses it in his undercurrent skill — name the feeling gently, as a question.","d":"","u":"\"Sounds like that was really frustrating?\" — then pause.","e":[],"r":["vve-calm","vg-undercurrent"],"f":["How do I calm someone down?","What is the undercurrent?"],"src":[{"l":"Read Anyone handbook §4"}]},{"i":"all-awkward-silence","w":["all"],"c":"smalltalk","t":"Awkward silences and pauses","a":["awkward silence","awkward pause","conversation dies","run out of things to say","silence","nothing to say","lull"],"s":"Three tools from the teachers. Vinh: a pause isn't a failure — you don't have to fill it, and a calm pause makes you look composed. Vanessa: use context cues around you, or signal a topic switch (\"Totally shifting gears…\"). Hughes: use FORK to pick a new area, or offer four threads in your next answer.","d":"","u":"Take a breath, then ask about something in the room or a FORK topic.","e":[],"r":["vg-pause","vve-context-cues","ch-fork","vve-transition"],"f":["What is FORK?","What are context cues?"],"src":[{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 7"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 1"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual p. 96"}]},{"i":"all-start-conversation","w":["all"],"c":"openers","t":"How do I start a conversation?","a":["start a conversation","opener","first thing to say","talk to a stranger","approach someone","break the ice","icebreaker"],"s":"Vanessa: skip \"Nice to meet you\" and \"What do you do?\" — try \"Working on anything exciting these days?\" or \"What was the highlight of your day?\" Vinh: give a little extra energy to your hello and match their pace. Hughes: a simple open question like \"How did you get started in this?\", then run his small-talk loop.","d":"","u":"Pick one opener and use it three times this week.","e":[],"r":["vve-power-questions","vg-generosity-energy","ch-one-liners","ch-smalltalk"],"f":["What are the nine power questions?","What is the small-talk formula?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 1"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 6"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual p. 96"}]},{"i":"all-body-language","w":["all"],"c":"body","t":"Body language basics","a":["body language","nonverbal","what should my body do","posture","body cues"],"s":"Vanessa: open, visible hands, the Launch Stance, and warmth cues like the head tilt and slow triple nod. Vinh: stillness, the doorway reset and gestures in the Power Sphere. Hughes: move slower than the room and let your face show their story. All three say read changes, not single gestures — and never as a lie detector.","d":"","u":"Start with one: hands visible and still.","e":[],"r":["vve-triple-threat","vve-warmth-cues","vg-power-sphere","ch-move-slower"],"f":["What is the Triple Threat?","What is the Power Sphere?"],"src":[{"l":"Captivate ch. 2"},{"l":"Cues ch. 3–5"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 8–13"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual p. 144"}]},{"i":"all-voice-competence","w":["vg"],"c":"voice","t":"Your voice signals competence","a":["voice matters","sound smart","sound competent","voice research"],"s":"People who heard someone's pitch rated them more competent and thoughtful than people who only read the same words (Schroeder and Epley, 2015). That's why Vinh puts voice first.","d":"","u":"For anything important, call or speak rather than only writing.","e":[],"r":["vg-delivery","vg-instrument"],"f":["Why does delivery matter?","What are the five vocal foundations?"],"src":[{"l":"Read Anyone handbook §4"}]},{"i":"all-myth-93","w":["ra"],"c":"ideas","t":"Myth: 93% of communication is non-verbal","a":["93 percent","93%","mehrabian","non verbal communication percentage","7 38 55"],"s":"A popular myth. It's a misreading of Albert Mehrabian, who said his findings don't apply to everyday conversation. Words matter.","d":"","u":"Get the words right and the delivery right — both count.","e":[],"r":["all-voice-competence","lo-lie-spotting"],"f":["Can you spot liars from body language?","Do power poses work?"],"src":[{"l":"Read Anyone handbook §10"}]},{"i":"all-myth-power-pose","w":["ra"],"c":"ideas","t":"Myth: power poses change your hormones","a":["power pose","power posing","superman pose","hormones","amy cuddy"],"s":"The claim that power poses change hormones didn't replicate (Ranehill and colleagues, 2015). Open posture may still help you feel a little steadier.","d":"","u":"Use open posture for comfort, not as magic.","e":[],"r":["vve-triple-threat","ch-composure"],"f":["What is the Launch Stance?","What is composure?"],"src":[{"l":"Read Anyone handbook §10"}]},{"i":"all-rapport","w":["all"],"c":"ideas","t":"How do I build rapport?","a":["rapport","build rapport","connect with someone","click with people","get along","bond"],"s":"Vinh: Match, Mirror and Lead — get in sync with their pace and volume, then lead gently. Vanessa: find threads in common (\"Same here!\"), use warmth cues and ask follow-ups. Hughes: make them feel heard and seen, and leave them feeling interesting.","d":"","u":"Match their pace for the first minute, then ask a follow-up.","e":[],"r":["vg-match-mirror-lead","vve-thread-theory","ch-heard-seen"],"f":["What is Match, Mirror and Lead?","What is Thread Theory?"],"src":[{"u":"https://vinhgiang.com/blog/how-to-build-rapport-with-absolutely-anyone","l":"Vinh's blog"},{"l":"Captivate ch. 5"},{"l":"DOAC 2026"}]},{"i":"all-disagree","w":["all"],"c":"tough","t":"How do I disagree without a fight?","a":["disagree","disagreement","push back","different opinion","conflict at work"],"s":"Hughes: resonate before you redirect, and leave a golden bridge so no one loses face. Vinh: \"Yes, and\" instead of \"yes, but\", and argue at the situation, not the person. Vanessa: affirm, create safety, and name the mission you share.","d":"","u":"Start with \"I can see why…\" then \"and here's where I land.\"","e":[],"r":["ch-resonate","vg-yes-and","vve-high-stakes","ch-golden-bridge"],"f":["What is the golden bridge?","What is \"yes, and\"?"],"src":[{"l":"DOAC 2026"},{"l":"Instagram Jun 2025"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 1"}]},{"i":"all-confidence","w":["all"],"c":"confidence","t":"How do I feel more confident talking to people?","a":["confidence","confident","shy","social anxiety","nervous talking to people","scared to talk"],"s":"Hughes: work on comfort first (ACSS), reframe nerves as excitement, and avoid collapse or posturing. Vinh: when a new habit feels fake, it's just unfamiliar — practise in tiny reps. Vanessa: go where you thrive, and assume people already like you a little.","d":"","u":"Choose one tiny rep today — a warm \"Good morning!\" with eye contact.","e":[],"r":["ch-acss","ch-nervous-excited","vg-competence-stages","vve-social-game-plan"],"f":["What is ACSS?","Why does practising feel fake?"],"src":[{"l":"DOAC 2024"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 14"},{"l":"Captivate ch. 1"}]},{"i":"vve-about","w":["vve"],"c":"about","t":"Who is Vanessa Van Edwards?","a":["who is vanessa","about vanessa","science of people","vanessa books","captivate cues conversation"],"s":"Vanessa Van Edwards runs Science of People, a behaviour research lab, and writes about the science of connection. On this page she's the teacher for openers, warmth and competence cues, follow-up questions and the nine power questions.","d":"Her three books here are Captivate (2017, fourteen \"hacks\" for first impressions and connection), Cues (2022, the body, voice and word signals behind charisma) and Conversation (2026, the three-level Conversation Blueprint). She curates a lot of research, but a few of her exact numbers are illustrations, so hold those loosely.","u":"Pick Van Edwards on the Teacher chips when you want openers, warmth and practical cues.","e":[],"r":["vve-blueprint","vve-charisma","vve-triple-threat"],"f":["What is the Conversation Blueprint?","What are the nine power questions?"],"src":[{"l":"scienceofpeople.com https://www.scienceofpeople.com/"},{"l":"Read Anyone handbook §3"}]},{"i":"vve-blueprint","w":["vve"],"c":"ideas","t":"The Conversation Blueprint","a":["blueprint","levels of conversation","level 1 level 2 level 3","put people at ease connect deeply create meaning","go deeper in conversation"],"s":"Vanessa's map for a good conversation has three levels, taken in order and at the other person's pace: Level 1 puts people at ease (light facts and fun), Level 2 connects more deeply (goals, values, worries), and Level 3 creates meaning (the story they tell about their life).","d":"Each level has three power questions. You move up only when their answers get longer and warmer. If answers shrink, stay where you are or step back a level. Level 3 borrows from psychologist Dan McAdams's work on life stories.","u":"Start light, listen for energy, then go one level deeper than feels comfortable — but only as fast as they go.","e":["Level 1: \"Working on anything exciting these days?\"","Level 2: \"What's your biggest goal right now?\"","Level 3: \"What would you do if you weren't afraid?\""],"r":["vve-power-questions","vve-go-deeper","vve-treasure"],"f":["What are the nine power questions?","How do I go deeper without being weird?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, \"The Conversation Blueprint\""},{"u":"https://www.scienceofpeople.com/conversation/","l":"Science of People: Conversation"}]},{"i":"vve-power-questions","w":["vve"],"c":"openers","t":"The nine power questions","a":["9 questions","best questions to ask","good questions","question list","conversation questions"],"s":"Vanessa's nine questions, three per level. Ease: exciting work, fun plans, passion project. Connect: biggest goal, what you've always wanted to learn, what you love to talk about. Meaning: which character you're most like, how you feel misunderstood, what you'd do if you weren't afraid.","d":"","u":"Use them in order. Save the Level 3 ones for people who already feel safe with you.","e":["\"Working on anything exciting these days?\"","\"Have any fun plans coming up?\"","\"What's your personal passion project?\"","\"What's your biggest goal right now?\"","\"What have you always wanted to learn?\"","\"What do you love to talk about?\"","\"What book, movie or TV character are you most like?\"","\"How do you feel most misunderstood?\"","\"What would you do if you weren't afraid?\""],"r":["vve-blueprint","vve-pq-exciting","vve-pq-character"],"f":["Why \"working on anything exciting\" instead of \"what do you do\"?","What is the signature question?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Levels 1–3"}]},{"i":"vve-pq-exciting","w":["vve"],"c":"openers","t":"\"Working on anything exciting these days?\"","a":["what do you do alternative","instead of what do you do","exciting work","job question","ask about work"],"s":"Vanessa's swap for \"What do you do?\" It lets people talk about work or anything better, and it doesn't feel like a question about their status or worth.","d":"","u":"Ask it lightly, then follow whatever lights them up, even if it isn't work.","e":[],"r":["vve-power-questions","vve-treasure","vve-logline"],"f":["How do I answer \"What do you do?\" myself?","What is the treasure hunt?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 power question 1"},{"l":"Read Anyone handbook §5.1"}]},{"i":"vve-pq-plans","w":["vve"],"c":"openers","t":"\"Have any fun plans coming up?\"","a":["fun plans","weekend plans","future question"],"s":"The second Level 1 power question. It points the chat at something good in their near future, so it starts on a warm note.","d":"","u":"If they say \"not really\", share a small plan of your own and hand the question back.","e":[],"r":["vve-power-questions","vve-ask-good"],"f":["What other openers does Vanessa suggest?","What is \"ask for good\"?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 power question 2"}]},{"i":"vve-pq-passion","w":["vve"],"c":"openers","t":"\"What's your personal passion project?\"","a":["passion project","hobby question","side project"],"s":"The third Level 1 question. It invites a story instead of a fact, and most people light up when asked about the thing they do just because they love it.","d":"","u":"Follow up on the why: \"What got you into that?\"","e":[],"r":["vve-power-questions","vve-pet-pest"],"f":["What are pet and pest projects?","What is the Conversation Blueprint?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 power question 3"}]},{"i":"vve-pq-goal","w":["vve"],"c":"deeper","t":"\"What's your biggest goal right now?\"","a":["biggest goal","dreams question","ambitions"],"s":"A Level 2 power question that moves from facts to what someone is working towards. When they share it, get genuinely interested — Vanessa calls that dreambuilding.","d":"","u":"Only ask once the light chat is flowing, and be ready to be excited for them.","e":[],"r":["vve-dreambuilding","vve-power-questions"],"f":["What is dreambuilding?","What if I don't like their dream?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 2 \"Uncovering Secret Dreams\""}]},{"i":"vve-pq-learn","w":["vve"],"c":"deeper","t":"\"What have you always wanted to learn?\"","a":["always wanted to learn","how to sound smart","curiosity question"],"s":"A Level 2 question that shows curiosity and makes you look thoughtful too. It reveals what someone values without asking about values directly.","d":"","u":"Follow up with \"What's stopped you so far?\" if the mood is right.","e":[],"r":["vve-power-questions","vve-teach-me"],"f":["What is \"teach me\"?","What is the signature question?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 2 \"How to Sound Smart\""}]},{"i":"vve-signature","w":["vve"],"c":"deeper","t":"The signature question","a":["what do you love to talk about","your own question","unique question","memorable question"],"s":"\"What do you love to talk about?\" is Vanessa's sixth power question, and she encourages you to build your own signature question — one you're known for, that people enjoy answering.","d":"","u":"Pick a question you'd genuinely love to hear answers to, and use it often enough that it becomes yours.","e":[],"r":["vve-power-questions","vve-break-script"],"f":["How do I break the social script?","What are conversation sparkers?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 2 \"Be Known as an Incredible & Unique Conversationalist\""}]},{"i":"vve-pq-character","w":["vve"],"c":"deeper","t":"The character question","a":["what character are you most like","book movie tv character","back door question"],"s":"\"What book, movie or TV character are you most like, and why?\" is a Level 3 question. It's a gentle back door into how someone sees themselves, without asking anything too direct.","d":"","u":"Answer it yourself too, so it feels like play rather than an interview.","e":[],"r":["vve-pq-misunderstood","vve-archetypes"],"f":["What are the five self-story archetypes?","What is Level 3?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 3 \"Get People to Reveal Their True Selves\""}]},{"i":"vve-pq-misunderstood","w":["vve"],"c":"deeper","t":"\"How do you feel most misunderstood?\"","a":["misunderstood question","deep question"],"s":"A Level 3 power question for relationships that can hold it. It shows you want to know the real person, not the polished one.","d":"","u":"Never ask it of a stranger. Ask it when there's trust, and listen without fixing.","e":[],"r":["vve-pq-afraid","vve-fears"],"f":["What would you do if you weren't afraid?","How do I honour someone's fears?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 3"}]},{"i":"vve-pq-afraid","w":["vve"],"c":"deeper","t":"\"What would you do if you weren't afraid?\"","a":["if you werent afraid","fear question","brave question"],"s":"The last of the nine power questions. It opens up hopes people rarely say out loud.","d":"","u":"If they share something big, respond with belief, not a reality check.","e":[],"r":["vve-believe","vve-fears"],"f":["What is \"I believe in you\"?","How do I honour fears and desires?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 3"}]},{"i":"vve-treasure","w":["vve"],"c":"smalltalk","t":"Small talk as a treasure hunt","a":["small talk","hate small talk","boring small talk","make small talk interesting","what do you like to do"],"s":"Vanessa treats small talk as hunting for what lights someone up. \"What do you like to do?\" beats \"What do you do?\" because it points at their interests rather than their job title.","d":"","u":"Watch for the topic that makes them speed up or smile, then dig there.","e":[],"r":["vve-sparkers","vve-stories-not-facts","ch-smalltalk"],"f":["What are conversation sparkers?","What is Hughes's small-talk formula?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 \"Turn Small Talk into a Treasure Hunt\""}]},{"i":"vve-stories-not-facts","w":["vve"],"c":"smalltalk","t":"Ask for stories, not facts","a":["stories not facts","story questions","how was your weekend","positive memory prompt"],"s":"Ask for the story behind a fact. \"Do anything fun this weekend?\" pulls up a good memory, which feels better to share than a list of facts.","d":"","u":"When they give a fact, ask \"How did that happen?\" or \"What was that like?\"","e":[],"r":["vve-treasure","vve-numbers-story"],"f":["How do I get quiet people to open up?","What is \"tell me more\"?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 \"Ask for Stories, Not Facts\""}]},{"i":"vve-magic-phrases","w":["vve"],"c":"warmth","t":"The five magic phrases","a":["magic phrase","best phrases","things to say"],"s":"Vanessa's five short phrases that build warmth: \"I was just thinking of you\", \"Tell me more?\", \"Last time you mentioned…\", \"Same here!\" and \"You're so ___!\" Each one only works when it's true.","d":"","u":"Look for honest moments to use each one this week.","e":[],"r":["vve-thinking-of-you","vve-tell-me-more","vve-last-time","vve-same-here","vve-so-x"],"f":["What does \"last time you mentioned\" do?","Why say \"same here\"?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 \"Five Magic Phrases\""}]},{"i":"vve-thinking-of-you","w":["vve"],"c":"warmth","t":"\"I was just thinking of you\"","a":["thinking of you","reach out","text a friend"],"s":"The first magic phrase. When someone genuinely crosses your mind, tell them. It's a small, true signal that they matter to you.","d":"","u":"Send it the moment it happens — a text is enough.","e":[],"r":["vve-magic-phrases","vve-pebbling"],"f":["What is pebbling?","What are the other magic phrases?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 magic phrase 1"}]},{"i":"vve-tell-me-more","w":["vve"],"c":"listening","t":"\"Tell me more?\"","a":["tell me more","keep them talking","follow up question","follow-up questions"],"s":"Both Vanessa and Vinh rate this phrase highly. It invites more of what the person cares about, makes them feel heard, and buys you time. Research on follow-up questions backs it: people who ask more follow-ups are liked more.","d":"","u":"Say it with a slight lift and real curiosity, then stay quiet.","e":[],"r":["vve-magic-phrases","vg-tell-me-more","all-follow-ups"],"f":["Why do follow-up questions work?","What does Vinh say about \"tell me more\"?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 magic phrase 2"},{"l":"Huang et al. 2017, as cited in the handbook"}]},{"i":"vve-last-time","w":["vve"],"c":"warmth","t":"\"Last time you mentioned…\"","a":["callback","remember details","last time you mentioned","follow up later"],"s":"The third magic phrase. Listen to remember, then call back a detail next time. It proves you were really listening.","d":"","u":"Jot down one detail after a conversation so you can bring it up later.","e":[],"r":["vve-magic-phrases","ch-names-stories"],"f":["How do I remember names?","What is pebbling?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 magic phrase 3"}]},{"i":"vve-same-here","w":["vve"],"c":"warmth","t":"\"Same here!\"","a":["same here","me too","in common","commonality"],"s":"The fourth magic phrase. When you spot a shared fact or feeling, say so. Similarity is one of the oldest findings in liking research.","d":"","u":"Only when it's true — fake common ground backfires and fails the sincerity test.","e":[],"r":["vve-thread-theory","vve-magic-phrases"],"f":["What is Thread Theory?","What if we have nothing in common?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 magic phrase 4"}]},{"i":"vve-so-x","w":["vve"],"c":"warmth","t":"\"You're so ___!\"","a":["youre so","be easily impressed","awe out loud","compliment"],"s":"The fifth magic phrase: be easily impressed and say it out loud. \"You're so organised!\" tells someone what you noticed and admire.","d":"","u":"Make it specific to something they just did.","e":[],"r":["vve-compliments","vve-highlighter"],"f":["How do I give a good compliment?","What is the Highlighter?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 magic phrase 5"}]},{"i":"vve-priming","w":["vve"],"c":"openers","t":"Priming words and first impressions","a":["first impression","first 10 seconds","nice to meet you alternative","positive primer","wonderful to connect"],"s":"Vanessa suggests opening with a short positive primer like \"Wonderful to connect\" instead of the forgettable \"Nice to meet you\", and choosing warm, team words like \"partner\" and \"collaborate\".","d":"","u":"Plan your first sentence before you walk in so it's warm and specific.","e":[],"r":["vve-permission-warmth","vve-triple-threat"],"f":["What is permission warmth?","What is the Triple Threat?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 \"Ten Seconds to a Memorable First Impression\""}]},{"i":"vve-break-script","w":["vve"],"c":"openers","t":"Break the social script","a":["autopilot","how are you alternative","boring questions","script"],"s":"Most small talk runs on autopilot (\"How are you?\" \"Good, you?\"). Vanessa says swap in a line that needs a real answer, like \"What was the highlight of your day?\"","d":"","u":"Pick one autopilot line you use and replace it this week.","e":[],"r":["vve-how-are-you","vve-ask-good","vve-sparkers"],"f":["How do I answer \"How are you?\"","What are conversation sparkers?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 \"The Weirdest, Best Party\""},{"l":"Read Anyone handbook §5.1"}]},{"i":"vve-high-stakes","w":["vve"],"c":"tough","t":"High-stakes conversations: affirm, safety, shared mission","a":["high stakes","important meeting","nervous meeting","difficult conversation","job interview"],"s":"In a high-stakes talk, Vanessa says appreciate the other person, create a small pocket of safety, and name the mission you share. Asking for advice can also make you look more competent, not less.","d":"","u":"Open with one honest appreciation and \"I think we both want…\".","e":[],"r":["vve-franklin","ch-golden-bridge","vg-yes-and"],"f":["What is the Franklin Effect?","How do I disagree nicely?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 \"How to Handle High-Stakes Conversations\""}]},{"i":"vve-context-cues","w":["vve"],"c":"smalltalk","t":"Context cues","a":["run out of things to say","nothing to say","what to talk about","how do you know the host"],"s":"When you run dry, use what's around you: the setting, the host, the event. \"How do you know the host?\" works almost anywhere.","d":"","u":"Scan the room for one shared thing before you start.","e":[],"r":["vve-treasure","all-awkward-silence"],"f":["What do I do in an awkward silence?","What is Hughes's FORK?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 \"Never Run Out of Things to Say\""}]},{"i":"vve-how-are-you","w":["vve"],"c":"smalltalk","t":"Answering \"How are you?\"","a":["how are you","answer how are you","good thanks"],"s":"Instead of \"Good, you?\", have a small story or a fun answer ready, then turn it back with something better than the script, like \"What's been good with you?\"","d":"","u":"Keep one light, true story from your week ready to share.","e":[],"r":["vve-break-script","vve-ask-good"],"f":["What is \"ask for good\"?","How do I answer \"What do you do?\""],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 \"How to Answer 'How Are You?'\""}]},{"i":"vve-logline","w":["vve"],"c":"smalltalk","t":"A logline for \"What do you do?\"","a":["what do you do","answer what do you do","elevator pitch","describe my job"],"s":"Have a short, hooky one-liner about your work, and end with a hook back to them so the conversation continues.","d":"","u":"Write one sentence that a ten-year-old would find interesting, then add \"How about you?\"","e":[],"r":["vve-pq-exciting","vve-verbal-catch","ch-four-threads"],"f":["What is Hughes's Four Threads formula?","What is verbal catch?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 \"How to Answer 'What Do You Do?'\""}]},{"i":"vve-signal-liking","w":["vve"],"c":"warmth","t":"Signal that you like them","a":["i like you","liking gap","show interest","they dont like me"],"s":"We like people who like us, and your signals are weaker than you think. Research on the \"liking gap\" shows people usually liked you more than you guessed, so amplify your warmth a little.","d":"","u":"Say what you enjoyed — \"I really enjoyed that\" — when it's true.","e":[],"r":["vve-attunement","all-liking-gap"],"f":["What is the liking gap?","How do I end a conversation?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 \"How to Create a Magnetic Presence\""},{"l":"Boothby et al. 2018, as cited in the handbook"}]},{"i":"vve-prepare-topics","w":["vve"],"c":"smalltalk","t":"Prepare your topics","a":["be interesting","prepare for event","what to say at a party"],"s":"Interesting people arrive prepared: a few fresh questions and a couple of stories they're ready to tell.","d":"","u":"Before an event, note two questions and one recent story.","e":[],"r":["vve-story-stack","vg-story-bank"],"f":["What is the Story Stack?","What is Vinh's Story Bank?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 \"How to Be Interesting\""}]},{"i":"vve-can-i-guess","w":["vve"],"c":"smalltalk","t":"\"Can I guess?\" for quieter people","a":["introverts","shy person","quiet person","talk to introverts"],"s":"Playful guessing (\"Can I guess what you do?\") invites quieter people in without putting them on the spot.","d":"","u":"Keep it light and let them correct you.","e":[],"r":["vve-numbers-story","vg-match-mirror-lead"],"f":["How do I get people to open up?","How do I match a quiet person?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 \"How to Talk to Introverts\""}]},{"i":"vve-numbers-story","w":["vve"],"c":"smalltalk","t":"Numbers, then story","a":["open up","get people to talk","easy questions","how long have you lived here"],"s":"Start with easy number questions (\"How long have you lived here?\"), then build towards a story. Small, easy answers warm people up for bigger ones.","d":"","u":"Follow a number answer with \"What brought you here?\"","e":[],"r":["vve-stories-not-facts","vve-can-i-guess"],"f":["Why ask for stories, not facts?","What is \"tell me more\"?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 \"How to Encourage People to Open Up\""}]},{"i":"vve-yums","w":["vve"],"c":"warmth","t":"Don't yuck my yums","a":["dont yuck","enthusiasm","they love something weird","respond to excitement"],"s":"When someone is excited about something, meet it with curiosity instead of a wince, even if it's not your thing.","d":"","u":"Ask \"What do you love about it?\"","e":[],"r":["vve-dream-dont-love","vve-dreambuilding"],"f":["What if I don't like their dream?","What is dreambuilding?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 \"Don't Yuck My Yums\""}]},{"i":"vve-transition","w":["vve"],"c":"smalltalk","t":"Graceful topic transitions","a":["change topic","change the subject","shift gears","transition"],"s":"Signal the switch openly: \"Totally shifting gears for a second, can I ask about…?\" It feels smooth instead of abrupt.","d":"","u":"Use it when a topic has run dry rather than forcing it.","e":[],"r":["vve-context-cues","all-awkward-silence"],"f":["What do I do when the conversation dies?","What is conversational confetti?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 \"Talk About Shark Attacks\""}]},{"i":"vve-weather","w":["vve"],"c":"smalltalk","t":"Weather only when it's good","a":["weather","talk about weather"],"s":"Vanessa says mention the weather only when it's good, and build on it. Bad-weather talk tends to drag the mood down.","d":"","u":"\"Isn't this sunshine great? Got anything fun planned for it?\"","e":[],"r":["vve-ask-good"],"f":["What is \"ask for good\"?","What should I never ask?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 \"Should I Ask About the Weather?\""}]},{"i":"vve-been-busy","w":["vve"],"c":"smalltalk","t":"Never ask \"Been busy?\"","a":["been busy","busy","bad questions","dont ask"],"s":"\"Been busy?\" primes people to think about stress. Ask about something good instead.","d":"","u":"Swap it for \"What's been the best part of your week?\"","e":[],"r":["vve-ask-good","vve-break-script"],"f":["What is \"ask for good\"?","How do I answer \"How are you?\""],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 \"Don't (Ever) Ask This\""}]},{"i":"vve-ask-good","w":["vve"],"c":"openers","t":"Ask for good","a":["whats something good","icebreaker","meeting opener","dinner question"],"s":"Open meetings and dinners with \"What's something good?\" Emotions are contagious, so starting on a good note lifts the whole room.","d":"","u":"Go first with a small, real good thing.","e":[],"r":["vve-been-busy","vve-weather"],"f":["What is conversational confetti?","What are good openers?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 \"Use This Icebreaker…\""}]},{"i":"vve-confetti","w":["vve"],"c":"smalltalk","t":"Conversational confetti","a":["tease a story","hook","can i tell you something weird"],"s":"Tease something interesting before you share it: \"Can I tell you something really weird?\" It sparks curiosity and gives the chat energy.","d":"","u":"Only tease what you can deliver — it must be genuinely interesting.","e":[],"r":["vve-story-stack","vg-spark-point-link"],"f":["What is the Story Stack?","What is Spark–Point–Link?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 \"A Trick from Master Conversationalists\""}]},{"i":"vve-mirror","w":["vve"],"c":"body","t":"Be a mirror (mirroring)","a":["mirroring","mirror","mimic","copy their body language","chameleon"],"s":"Mirroring means subtly reflecting someone's positive gestures, posture or words. Vanessa says mirror the positive, never the negative, and keep it subtle — research on the \"chameleon effect\" found light mimicry raises liking, while obvious copying backfires.","d":"Vinh teaches a related idea, Match, Mirror and Lead: match their pace and volume first, then gently lead. Hughes's verbal mirror is the spoken version — repeat their last few words.","u":"Let it happen naturally; if you notice yourself copying, stop.","e":[],"r":["vg-match-mirror-lead","ch-verbal-mirror","vve-warmth-cues"],"f":["What is Match, Mirror and Lead?","What is the verbal mirror?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 \"Be a Mirror\""},{"l":"Cues ch. 4"},{"l":"Chartrand & Bargh 1999, as cited in the handbook"}]},{"i":"vve-at-ease","w":["vve"],"c":"confidence","t":"Be at ease, not impressive","a":["trying to impress","impress people","be cool","show off"],"s":"Drop the clever line. Vanessa says people connect with someone relaxed who lets them shine, not with someone performing.","d":"","u":"Make your goal \"help them feel at ease\" instead of \"impress them\".","e":[],"r":["ch-performance-mode","vve-anti-perfect"],"f":["What is performance mode?","Should I show flaws?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 \"Stop Trying to Impress People\""}]},{"i":"vve-exit","w":["vve"],"c":"exits","t":"The graceful exit","a":["end a conversation","leave a conversation","exit","wrap up","say goodbye","get away"],"s":"Vanessa's three-step exit: mention something in the future, give a specific compliment plus your next action, then wave and go. \"Good luck with the launch next week — I loved hearing about it. I'm going to grab a drink. Talk soon!\"","d":"A big study found conversations almost never end when both people want them to, so ending on purpose is a kindness, not a rejection.","u":"Leave on a high, not when the chat has fizzled.","e":["\"I'm so glad we met. Good luck with the move! I'm going to say hi to the host.\""],"r":["all-end-on-purpose","ch-barkeep","vve-anchor-touch"],"f":["Why end on purpose?","How do I leave someone who won't stop talking?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 \"How to Gracefully End a Conversation\""},{"l":"Mastroianni et al. 2021, as cited in the handbook"}]},{"i":"vve-dreambuilding","w":["vve"],"c":"deeper","t":"Dreambuilding","a":["support someones goal","react to good news","capitalisation","be excited for them"],"s":"When someone shares a goal or good news, get excited, bring ideas, and check back later. Research on responding to good news shows enthusiastic, active responses build relationships.","d":"","u":"Ask a follow-up and offer one idea or contact that might help.","e":[],"r":["vve-pq-goal","vve-pebbling"],"f":["What if I don't like their dream?","What is pebbling?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 2 \"Uncovering Secret Dreams\""}]},{"i":"vve-dream-dont-love","w":["vve"],"c":"deeper","t":"When you don't love their dream","a":["dont like their dream","disagree with their plan","skeptical of their idea"],"s":"Instead of doubting, get curious: ask what got them into it and where they see it going.","d":"","u":"Curiosity first; give your view only if they ask.","e":[],"r":["vve-yums","vve-curious-critical"],"f":["What is \"curious before critical\"?","What is dreambuilding?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 2 \"What to Do When You Don't Like Their Dream\""}]},{"i":"vve-assertive","w":["vve"],"c":"tough","t":"Assertive curiosity and a clear ask","a":["be assertive","ask for what i want","speak up","make a request"],"s":"Ask what, where, who and why to understand, then claim a goal and an action: \"I need X. Can you do Y?\"","d":"","u":"Keep the request short and specific, then stop talking.","e":[],"r":["vve-own-it","ch-plain-speech"],"f":["How do I speak plainly?","How do I give a reason with a request?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 2 \"Be More Assertive\""}]},{"i":"vve-feedback","w":["vve"],"c":"tough","t":"Tough feedback, warmly","a":["give feedback","criticism","tell someone they did badly","constructive feedback"],"s":"Lead with your belief in them, be specific about what happened, then ask how you can help it not happen again.","d":"","u":"One issue at a time, said calmly, then listen.","e":[],"r":["vve-nut-job","vg-situation-not-person"],"f":["How do I argue about the situation, not the person?","What is the NUT Job?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 2 \"How to Deliver Tough Feedback\""}]},{"i":"vve-calm","w":["vve"],"c":"tough","t":"Calm someone down by naming, not suppressing","a":["calm down","angry person","upset person","someone is upset","de-escalate"],"s":"Never say \"calm down\". Vanessa says acknowledge the feeling, then ask \"Can you tell me more about that?\" Research shows naming a feeling can lower its intensity, while suppressing it backfires.","d":"","u":"Name it as a question (\"Sounds frustrating?\") and slow your own voice.","e":[],"r":["vg-undercurrent","vg-match-mirror-lead","all-label-feeling"],"f":["How do I name someone's feelings?","How does Vinh calm someone?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 2 \"How to Calm Someone Down\""},{"l":"Lieberman et al. 2007, as cited in the handbook"}]},{"i":"vve-vips","w":["vve"],"c":"tough","t":"Talking to your boss or VIPs","a":["boss","vip","important people","famous person","senior person","manager"],"s":"Skip the fan compliment. Talk to VIPs at Level 2: ask for their take on something real.","d":"","u":"\"What's your read on where this is heading?\" — then listen properly.","e":[],"r":["vve-blueprint","ch-teach-me"],"f":["What is Level 2?","What is \"teach me\"?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 2 \"How to Talk to Your Boss (or VIPs)\""}]},{"i":"vve-pebbling","w":["vve"],"c":"warmth","t":"Pebbling (be a penguin)","a":["penguin","keep in touch","stay in touch","maintain friendships","send a link"],"s":"Penguins give pebbles as small gifts. Vanessa says send small \"this made me think of you\" pebbles — a link, a photo, a quick follow-up on something they told you.","d":"","u":"Send one pebble this week to someone you've been meaning to contact.","e":[],"r":["vve-thinking-of-you","vve-last-time"],"f":["What are the magic phrases?","How do I make friends as an adult?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 2 \"Be a Penguin\""}]},{"i":"vve-pet-pest","w":["vve"],"c":"deeper","t":"Pet and pest projects","a":["pet project","pest project","hassles","motivation","complain"],"s":"Ask about the project they love (pet) and the hassle they're living with (pest). For a pest, ask whether they want support or just a good rant.","d":"","u":"\"Do you want ideas, or do you just want to vent?\"","e":[],"r":["vve-pq-passion","vg-traps"],"f":["What are the listening traps?","What is the passion project question?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 2 \"Discover Hidden Motivations\" and \"Bond Over Pest Projects\""}]},{"i":"vve-humour","w":["vve"],"c":"warmth","t":"Laughter, being your own butt and inside jokes","a":["be funny","humour","humor","self deprecation","inside jokes","laugh"],"s":"Shared laughs are relationship glue. Vanessa says the easiest way to be funny is to be your own butt — gently — and to call back a shared joke later.","d":"","u":"Laugh at yourself lightly; never at them.","e":[],"r":["vg-humour","vve-anti-perfect"],"f":["What does Vinh say about humour?","Should I show my flaws?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 2 \"The Easiest Way to Be Funny\", \"Be Your Own Butt\""}]},{"i":"vve-confident-language","w":["vve"],"c":"confidence","t":"Simple, confident language","a":["sound confident","simple words","speak with confidence","im confident that"],"s":"Short, simple words make you sound smarter, not less. Vanessa also suggests \"I'm confident that…\" and framing what you want rather than what's wrong.","d":"","u":"Cut long words when a short one will do.","e":[],"r":["ch-plain-speech","vve-vocal-power"],"f":["Which hedging words should I drop?","How do I sound confident?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 2 \"Speak with Confidence\""}]},{"i":"vve-verbal-catch","w":["vve"],"c":"listening","t":"Verbal catch: \"How about you?\"","a":["talk too much","i talk too much","take turns","how about you","dominate conversation"],"s":"Treat conversation like a game of catch. After your bit, hand the ball back with \"How about you?\"","d":"","u":"If you've talked for over a minute, toss the ball back.","e":[],"r":["vve-logline","ch-smalltalk"],"f":["How do I stop talking too much?","What is the small-talk formula?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 2 \"Make Sure You Don't Talk Too Much\""}]},{"i":"vve-connector","w":["vve"],"c":"groups","t":"Be a connector","a":["introduce people","introductions","networking","connect people"],"s":"Introduce people around a shared keyword: \"You both love climbing!\" It makes you memorable and makes them feel looked after.","d":"","u":"Give each person one interesting fact about the other.","e":[],"r":["vve-highlighter","ch-barkeep"],"f":["What is the Highlighter?","What is Hughes's Barkeep?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 2 \"How to Be a Connector\""},{"l":"Captivate ch. 4"}]},{"i":"vve-friends","w":["vve"],"c":"groups","t":"How to make friends","a":["make friends","new friends","adult friendships","lonely"],"s":"Vanessa's approach: schedule regular social hours, build shared contexts (a class, a club, a regular spot), and try small \"friendship tests\" like a low-key invitation.","d":"","u":"Pick one regular thing and show up every week.","e":[],"r":["vve-pebbling","vve-social-game-plan"],"f":["What is pebbling?","What is the Social Game Plan?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 2 \"How to Make Friends\""}]},{"i":"vve-disliked","w":["vve"],"c":"confidence","t":"The courage to be disliked","a":["not everyone likes me","people dont like me","rejection"],"s":"Not everyone will click with you, and that's fine. Accepting it frees you to be yourself with the people who do.","d":"","u":"Treat a flat conversation as information, not a verdict.","e":[],"r":["all-liking-gap","ch-rules-confidence"],"f":["What is the liking gap?","What are Hughes's rules of confidence?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 2"}]},{"i":"vve-narrative-hints","w":["vve"],"c":"listening","t":"Follow narrative hints","a":["their story","how they see their life","what did that teach you"],"s":"When someone hints at how they see their life (\"I've always been the responsible one\"), follow it: \"What did that teach you?\"","d":"","u":"Listen for \"always\", \"never\" and \"I'm the kind of person who…\".","e":[],"r":["vve-archetypes","vve-change-story"],"f":["What are the five self-story archetypes?","How do I help someone change their story?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 3 \"What's Your Story?\" and \"How to Listen\""}]},{"i":"vve-archetypes","w":["vve"],"c":"deeper","t":"Five self-story archetypes","a":["ambitious hero","contented pragmatist","selfless helper","creative disruptor","unlucky struggler","personality types"],"s":"Vanessa describes five ways people tell their life story: the ambitious hero, contented pragmatist, selfless helper, creative disruptor and unlucky struggler. Read Anyone uses them only as colour for how to respond, never as a label.","d":"","u":"Never name the type out loud; just respond to the story they're telling.","e":[],"r":["vve-narrative-hints","all-six-needs"],"f":["Are the six needs labels?","What is Level 3?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 3 \"Know Who You're Dealing With\""}]},{"i":"vve-lucky","w":["vve"],"c":"deeper","t":"\"Are you lucky?\"","a":["are you lucky","luck question","optimism"],"s":"One question that reveals optimism and how much control someone feels over their life.","d":"","u":"Ask it playfully, then follow up on their reason.","e":[],"r":["vve-money","vve-archetypes"],"f":["What is the money question?","What are the self-story archetypes?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 3 \"The One Question…\""}]},{"i":"vve-money","w":["vve"],"c":"deeper","t":"The money question","a":["when do you feel rich","money narrative","money talk"],"s":"\"When do you feel rich?\" opens up how someone thinks about money. Only for people close enough to hold it.","d":"","u":"Share your own answer first.","e":[],"r":["vve-lucky","vve-pq-misunderstood"],"f":["What is \"are you lucky?\"","What is Level 3?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 3 \"A Question That Might Lead to Divorce\""}]},{"i":"vve-awe","w":["vve"],"c":"deeper","t":"Awe","a":["awe","be amazed","connection ingredient"],"s":"Vanessa calls awe the most important connection ingredient: be ready to be amazed by someone's story.","d":"","u":"Listen as if their story could surprise you — it often will.","e":[],"r":["vve-so-x","vve-believe"],"f":["What is \"I believe in you\"?","What is dreambuilding?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 3 \"The Single Most Important Connection Ingredient\""}]},{"i":"vve-believe","w":["vve"],"c":"warmth","t":"\"I believe in you\"","a":["i believe in you","encourage","support someone","courage"],"s":"Four words that fuel courage. Said with conviction, they can help someone take a step they're scared of.","d":"","u":"Say it slowly and mean it; don't add \"but\".","e":[],"r":["vve-pq-afraid","vve-fears"],"f":["How do I honour someone's fears?","What is awe?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 3 \"Four Words That Fuel Courage\""}]},{"i":"vve-fears","w":["vve"],"c":"deeper","t":"Honour fears and desires","a":["someones fears","secret desires","scared","vulnerable conversation"],"s":"Make it safe for people to say what scares them and what they secretly want, then offer support rather than judgment.","d":"","u":"Thank them for telling you before you say anything else.","e":[],"r":["vve-believe","vg-vulnerability"],"f":["What does Vinh say about vulnerability?","How do I help someone change their story?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 3 \"Hidden Fears and Secret Desires\""}]},{"i":"vve-change-story","w":["vve"],"c":"deeper","t":"Help change the story","a":["reframe","change the story","negative story","they think theyre a failure"],"s":"Help someone see the story they tell about themselves and find a kinder, truer version. Vinh's \"second arrow\" idea is a close cousin.","d":"","u":"Ask \"Is there another way to read that?\" rather than arguing.","e":[],"r":["vg-relinking","vve-narrative-hints"],"f":["What is the second arrow?","What are narrative hints?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 3 \"How to Change the Story\""}]},{"i":"vve-be-known","w":["vve"],"c":"deeper","t":"Allow yourself to be known","a":["open up","share myself","be vulnerable","autopilot answers"],"s":"Connection runs both ways. Answer deep questions honestly instead of on autopilot.","d":"","u":"Next time someone asks a real question, give a real answer.","e":[],"r":["vg-vulnerability","ch-go-first"],"f":["What does \"go first\" mean?","What does Vinh say about vulnerability?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 3 \"Allow Yourself to Be Known\""}]},{"i":"vve-social-game-plan","w":["vve"],"c":"groups","t":"The Social Game Plan","a":["social game plan","winger","riser","thrive spots","networking event","party strategy","where to stand"],"s":"Captivate's first hack: go where you thrive, skip what you dread, and plan your position in the room. Bring a Winger (a friend on your social adventure) and pick a Riser (someone you want to level up with).","d":"Vanessa sorts places into thrive, neutral and survive spots, and the room into the start zone (entrance), side zone (corners) and social zone (where people come off the bar or food). The challenge: say no to one event you dread.","u":"At your next event, find the social zone instead of hovering by the door.","e":[],"r":["vve-triple-threat","vve-friends"],"f":["What is the Triple Threat?","How do I join a group?"],"src":[{"l":"Captivate ch. 1"}]},{"i":"vve-triple-threat","w":["vve"],"c":"body","t":"The Triple Threat","a":["first impression body language","launch stance","handshake","visible hands","posture first impression"],"s":"Vanessa's three first-impression cues: visible hands, the Launch Stance (shoulders down and back, chin and chest up, space between arms and body) and good eye contact.","d":"The \"60–70% eye contact\" figure she uses is best treated as a rule of thumb, not a lab number. Her challenge for this chapter is a handshake audit.","u":"Before you walk in, check: hands out of pockets, shoulders down, chin up.","e":[],"r":["vve-charisma-cues","vg-posture-doorway"],"f":["What is Vinh's doorway reset?","What are charisma cues?"],"src":[{"l":"Captivate ch. 2"}]},{"i":"vve-sparkers","w":["vve"],"c":"openers","t":"Conversation sparkers and hot buttons","a":["sparkers","conversation sparks","spark","hot button","novel questions","interesting questions"],"s":"Conversation sparkers are fresh questions that get people out of autopilot. Hot buttons are the topics that make someone lean in — when you spot one, dig there.","d":"","u":"Replace one stale question (\"Where are you from?\") with a sparker (\"Working on anything exciting?\").","e":[],"r":["vve-treasure","vve-power-questions","vve-break-script"],"f":["What are the nine power questions?","What is the treasure hunt?"],"src":[{"l":"Captivate ch. 3"}]},{"i":"vve-name-game","w":["vve"],"c":"names","t":"The Name Game","a":["remember names","forget names","names","name memory","bad with names"],"s":"Vanessa's three steps to remember a name: hear it (really listen), repeat it back (\"Mike, nice to meet you\"), and anchor it to someone you already know with the same name, picturing them together.","d":"She calls it a fun way to gamify names and suggests teaching it to friends so you can help each other find anchors. Practise on a TV show: try the steps every time a new character appears.","u":"Say the name back once, clearly, in the first sentence.","e":[],"r":["ch-names-stories","ch-use-names"],"f":["What if I forget a name?","What does Hughes say about names?"],"src":[{"l":"Captivate ch. 3 (pp. 68–69)"}]},{"i":"vve-highlighter","w":["vve"],"c":"groups","t":"The Highlighter (raving introductions)","a":["introduce someone","raving introduction","introductions","tell people what impresses you"],"s":"Introduce people by their best qualities (\"This is Sam — she just ran her first marathon\"). Vanessa says be a highlighter: point out what's great in others.","d":"","u":"Next time you introduce someone, add one thing that impresses you about them.","e":[],"r":["vve-connector","vve-pygmalion"],"f":["What is a connector?","What is the Pygmalion effect?"],"src":[{"l":"Captivate ch. 4"}]},{"i":"vve-pygmalion","w":["vve"],"c":"ideas","t":"Expect the best (Pygmalion)","a":["pygmalion","expectations","expect the best","vow of silence"],"s":"Your expectations of people shape how they behave around you. Listen for the good and you'll tend to bring it out.","d":"","u":"Before a meeting, decide one good thing you expect from the other person.","e":[],"r":["vve-highlighter","all-assume-liked"],"f":["What is the Highlighter?","Do people like me more than I think?"],"src":[{"l":"Captivate ch. 4"}]},{"i":"vve-thread-theory","w":["vve"],"c":"smalltalk","t":"Thread Theory","a":["thread theory","me too","commonalities","five whys","common ground","things in common"],"s":"Look for three things in common in the first three minutes, ask \"why?\" in different ways to go deeper (the five whys), and offer help where you can. Shared threads tie people together.","d":"","u":"When you hit a \"me too\", ask \"What got you into it?\"","e":[],"r":["vve-same-here","vve-teach-me","vg-threads"],"f":["What if we have nothing in common?","What are Vinh's conversational threads?"],"src":[{"l":"Captivate ch. 5"}]},{"i":"vve-teach-me","w":["vve"],"c":"smalltalk","t":"\"Teach me?\"","a":["teach me","nothing in common","no common ground","dont know anything about it"],"s":"When there's no \"me too\", Vanessa says ask them to teach you. People enjoy explaining what they know, and you learn something.","d":"","u":"\"I know nothing about that — teach me?\"","e":[],"r":["vve-thread-theory","ch-teach-me"],"f":["What does Hughes say about \"teach me\"?","What is Thread Theory?"],"src":[{"l":"Captivate ch. 5"}]},{"i":"vve-decoder","w":["vve"],"c":"body","t":"The Decoder: seven microexpressions","a":["microexpression","facial expressions","read faces","seven emotions","contempt","fear face"],"s":"Captivate's Decoder teaches the seven universal expressions (anger, contempt, happiness, fear, surprise, disgust, sadness). Read Anyone uses this only to notice a feeling and respond kindly — never to decide someone is lying.","d":"","u":"If you see a flash of a feeling, respond to it gently: \"Did that land oddly?\"","e":[],"r":["lo-lie-spotting","vg-visual-radar"],"f":["Can you spot liars from body language?","What is visual radar?"],"src":[{"l":"Captivate ch. 6"}]},{"i":"vve-speed-read","w":["vve"],"c":"ideas","t":"Speed-Reading personality (Big Five)","a":["personality","big five","openness","conscientiousness","extraversion","agreeableness","neuroticism","read personality"],"s":"Vanessa uses the Big Five traits to adapt how you talk: openness, conscientiousness, extraversion, agreeableness and neuroticism. It's a guide for adapting, not a way to label people.","d":"","u":"Notice, adapt a little, and stay open to being wrong.","e":[],"r":["all-six-needs","vve-archetypes"],"f":["Are the six needs labels?","What is the Appreciation Matrix?"],"src":[{"l":"Captivate ch. 7"}]},{"i":"vve-appreciation","w":["vve"],"c":"warmth","t":"The Appreciation Matrix","a":["thank someone","appreciation","love languages","show appreciation","gratitude"],"s":"People feel appreciated in different ways: words, time, acts of service, gifts and (where appropriate) touch. Vanessa says thank people their way, not yours.","d":"","u":"Notice how someone thanks others — that's often how they like to be thanked.","e":[],"r":["ch-gratitude","vve-primary-value"],"f":["What is Primary Value?","What does Hughes say about gratitude?"],"src":[{"l":"Captivate ch. 8"}]},{"i":"vve-primary-value","w":["vve"],"c":"ideas","t":"Primary Value","a":["what people value","resources","love service status money goods information"],"s":"Based on Foa's resource theory, people trade six kinds of value: love, service, status, money, goods and information. Knowing what someone values helps you give in a way that means something to them.","d":"","u":"Ask yourself what this person seems to value most, then offer that sincerely.","e":[],"r":["vve-appreciation","ch-values-map"],"f":["What is Hughes's Values Map?","What is the Appreciation Matrix?"],"src":[{"l":"Captivate ch. 9"}]},{"i":"vve-story-stack","w":["vve"],"c":"story","t":"Story Stack and the Boomerang","a":["story stack","stories to tell","boomerang","what story to tell"],"s":"Prepare stories for common topics, each with a hook, a struggle and vivid words. After you tell one, boomerang the topic back: \"Has that ever happened to you?\"","d":"","u":"Build a stack of three stories for the topics that come up most.","e":[],"r":["vg-story-bank","vg-spark-point-link"],"f":["What is Vinh's Spark–Point–Link?","What is the Story Bank?"],"src":[{"l":"Captivate ch. 10"}]},{"i":"vve-own-it","w":["vve"],"c":"tough","t":"Own It! (buy-in and \"because\")","a":["buy in","get buy-in","because","give a reason","delegate","ownership"],"s":"Share the mission, hand over real ownership, and give a reason when you ask for something. The word \"because\" with a real reason makes requests feel fair.","d":"","u":"\"Could you take this on, because you know the client best?\"","e":[],"r":["vve-assertive","ch-decision-style"],"f":["How do I make a clear ask?","What are decision styles?"],"src":[{"l":"Captivate ch. 11"}]},{"i":"vve-franklin","w":["vve"],"c":"warmth","t":"The Franklin Effect and being anti-perfect","a":["ask for advice","franklin effect","show weakness","flaws","pratfall"],"s":"Asking someone for advice tends to make them like you more, and admitting a small weakness makes you easier to warm to. Vanessa calls it being anti-perfect.","d":"","u":"Ask for genuine advice on something you actually want help with.","e":[],"r":["vve-anti-perfect","ch-insecurities"],"f":["Should I share my insecurities?","What is \"teach me\"?"],"src":[{"l":"Captivate ch. 12"}]},{"i":"vve-anti-perfect","w":["vve"],"c":"warmth","t":"Show a small imperfection","a":["imperfect","be human","perfect","flaws early"],"s":"Showing a small human imperfection early helps people relax, because they stop waiting for the flaw.","d":"","u":"Admit something small and true, lightly.","e":[],"r":["vve-franklin","ch-insecurities","vg-vulnerability"],"f":["What does Vinh say about vulnerability?","What is the Franklin Effect?"],"src":[{"l":"Captivate ch. 12"},{"l":"Read Anyone handbook §5.1"}]},{"i":"vve-nut-job","w":["vve"],"c":"tough","t":"The NUT Job","a":["nut","name understand transform","fear","worried person","objection"],"s":"For someone who's worried or resistant: Name the fear, Understand it, Transform it into a solution together.","d":"","u":"\"Sounds like you're worried about the cost. Tell me more?\" — then work on it together.","e":[],"r":["ch-objections","vve-calm"],"f":["How does Hughes handle objections honestly?","How do I calm someone down?"],"src":[{"l":"Captivate ch. 13"}]},{"i":"vve-say-no","w":["vve"],"c":"tough","t":"Counter-offers and saying no","a":["say no","decline","counter offer","turn down"],"s":"Say no to the thing, yes to the person: \"I can't do dinner, but let's grab a coffee.\"","d":"","u":"Offer the version you can genuinely do.","e":[],"r":["vve-assertive","ch-plain-speech"],"f":["How do I make a clear ask?","How do I speak plainly?"],"src":[{"l":"Captivate ch. 13"}]},{"i":"vve-attunement","w":["vve"],"c":"warmth","t":"Attunement","a":["attunement","belonging","curiosity","enjoy their company"],"s":"Vanessa's final hack: reciprocal liking, belonging and curiosity. Her challenge: tell one person you enjoy their company.","d":"","u":"Say it today to someone it's true about.","e":[],"r":["vve-signal-liking","all-liking-gap"],"f":["What is the liking gap?","How do I signal liking?"],"src":[{"l":"Captivate ch. 14"}]},{"i":"vve-charisma","w":["vve"],"c":"ideas","t":"Charisma = warmth + competence","a":["charisma","warm","competent","charismatic","likeable and smart","danger zone","charisma dial"],"s":"Vanessa defines charisma as a balance of warmth (can I trust you?) and competence (can I rely on you?). Her Charisma Dial means turning one up or down to suit the moment. Low on both is her \"Danger Zone\".","d":"This rests on well-supported research on warmth and competence (Fiske, Cuddy and Glick, 2007). Competence without warmth reads as cold; warmth without competence can read as nice but not credible.","u":"In most first meetings, lead with warmth, then show one clear point of competence.","e":[],"r":["vve-warmth-cues","vve-power-cues","all-warmth-first"],"f":["What are warmth cues?","What are power cues?"],"src":[{"l":"Cues ch. 1"},{"l":"Fiske, Cuddy & Glick 2007, as cited in the handbook"}]},{"i":"vve-cue-cycle","w":["vve"],"c":"ideas","t":"The Cue Cycle","a":["cues are contagious","decode encode","cue cycle"],"s":"Vanessa says cues spread between people: we decode others' signals, encode our own, and internalise them. Your calm or tension can rub off on others.","d":"","u":"Change your own cues first — they set the tone.","e":[],"r":["vve-charisma","vve-danger-cues"],"f":["What are danger-zone cues?","What is charisma?"],"src":[{"l":"Cues ch. 2"}]},{"i":"vve-charisma-cues","w":["vve"],"c":"body","t":"Charisma cues: lean, no blocks, face them","a":["lean in","blocking","face them","fronting","personal space","body language interest"],"s":"Show interest with your body: lean in a little, remove barriers between you (laptops, crossed arms, bags), point toes, torso and head towards them, respect personal space and use purposeful eye contact.","d":"","u":"Clear the table between you and turn your whole body their way.","e":[],"r":["vve-warmth-cues","vve-triple-threat"],"f":["What are warmth cues?","What is the Triple Threat?"],"src":[{"l":"Cues ch. 3"}]},{"i":"vve-warmth-cues","w":["vve"],"c":"body","t":"Warmth cues","a":["look warm","friendly body language","approachable","head tilt","eyebrow raise"],"s":"Vanessa's warmth cues: a well-timed head tilt, a slow triple nod, a quick eyebrow raise for interest, a real smile, light appropriate touch, and mirroring the positive.","d":"","u":"Pick one — the head tilt while listening is easiest — and practise it this week.","e":[],"r":["vve-triple-nod","vve-head-tilt","vve-mirror"],"f":["What is the triple nod?","What does a head tilt do?"],"src":[{"l":"Cues ch. 4"}]},{"i":"vve-triple-nod","w":["vve"],"c":"body","t":"The slow triple nod","a":["nod","nodding","triple nod","nod three times","get people to talk more"],"s":"A slow nod in threes while someone talks encourages them to keep going and shows you're with them. Speed matters: slow nods feel thoughtful, fast nods look like \"hurry up\".","d":"Vanessa's exercise: say \"I hear you… I hear you… I hear you\" slowly, about one per second, nodding with each. The often-quoted \"67% longer\" figure is best held loosely. To show quiet disagreement, she says simply withhold your nods.","u":"Use it when you want someone to open up more.","e":[],"r":["vve-warmth-cues","vve-head-tilt","vve-vocal-invitations"],"f":["What does a head tilt do?","What are vocal invitations?"],"src":[{"l":"Cues ch. 4"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 1"}]},{"i":"vve-head-tilt","w":["vve"],"c":"body","t":"The head tilt","a":["head tilt","tilt head","tilt"],"s":"A slight head tilt while someone talks says \"I'm listening, tell me more\". Vanessa says time your tilts for the moments that matter rather than holding one all the time.","d":"","u":"Tilt when they get to the important part.","e":[],"r":["vve-triple-nod","vve-warmth-cues"],"f":["What is the triple nod?","What are warmth cues?"],"src":[{"l":"Cues ch. 4"}]},{"i":"vve-power-cues","w":["vve"],"c":"body","t":"Power cues","a":["look confident","competent body language","steeple","lower lid","explanatory gestures","palms down","take up space"],"s":"Vanessa's competence cues: confident posture that takes up space, a focused lower-lid flex when listening hard, the steeple (fingertips touching), explanatory gestures that show your point, and palms down for a firm point.","d":"","u":"Use gestures that show the size, shape or number you're describing.","e":[],"r":["vve-charisma","vg-power-sphere"],"f":["What is Vinh's Power Sphere?","How do I stop interruptions?"],"src":[{"l":"Cues ch. 5"}]},{"i":"vve-interruptions","w":["vve"],"c":"tough","t":"Stop interruptions nicely: Fish, Bookmark, Anchor Touch, Preview","a":["interrupted","interrupt","interrupter","someone keeps interrupting","talks too much","droner","won't stop talking","get a word in"],"s":"Vanessa's gentle toolkit for interrupters and talkers: the Fish (open your mouth slightly, as if about to speak), the Bookmark (raise a hand, palm out, to hold your place), the Anchor Touch (a light touch on the arm to bring them back, only where touch is welcome), and the Preview (say \"I've got three points\" up front).","d":"","u":"Start with the Preview before you speak, and the Bookmark if they jump in.","e":[],"r":["ch-interruptions","vve-exit"],"f":["What does Hughes say about interruptions?","How do I end a conversation?"],"src":[{"l":"Cues ch. 5"}]},{"i":"vve-anchor-touch","w":["vve"],"c":"exits","t":"Leaving a monologue","a":["someone wont stop talking","trapped in conversation","escape conversation","long story"],"s":"When someone's lost in a monologue, Vanessa suggests the Fish, then the Bookmark, then, if touch is appropriate, a light Anchor Touch on the arm. Use the pause to add your bit — or to say a warm goodbye.","d":"","u":"Pair the pause with a graceful exit line.","e":[],"r":["vve-interruptions","vve-exit"],"f":["What is the graceful exit?","How do I stop interruptions?"],"src":[{"l":"Cues ch. 5"}]},{"i":"vve-danger-cues","w":["vve"],"c":"body","t":"Danger-zone cues","a":["distancing","self soothing","blocking","lip purse","shame","resting face","incongruence","bad body language"],"s":"Cues that lower trust: distancing, self-comforting (rubbing, fidgeting), blocking, signs of shame, lip purses and a cold resting face. Notice them in yourself first. When your words and body don't match, people trust the body less.","d":"","u":"Check your resting face in a video call — soften it a little.","e":[],"r":["vve-cue-cycle","vg-expression"],"f":["What does Vinh say about facial expression?","What are warmth cues?"],"src":[{"l":"Cues ch. 6"}]},{"i":"vve-vocal-power","w":["vve"],"c":"voice","t":"Vocal power","a":["sound confident","uptalk","vocal fry","voice confidence","end sentences down","um"],"s":"Vanessa's vocal power basics: speak at your natural low pitch, end statements going down rather than up (no uptalk), avoid vocal fry, control your volume, and use a power pause instead of \"um\".","d":"","u":"Record yourself and check the last word of each sentence goes down.","e":[],"r":["vg-melody","vg-pause","vve-vocal-variety"],"f":["How does Vinh use pauses?","What is vocal variety?"],"src":[{"l":"Cues ch. 7"}]},{"i":"vve-permission-warmth","w":["vve"],"c":"openers","t":"Permission warmth (the vocal hug)","a":["warm greeting","greet someone","so good to see you","vocal hug","say hi"],"s":"Open with genuine warmth in your voice: \"So good to see you!\" It gives the other person permission to be warm back.","d":"","u":"Lift your voice a little on the hello.","e":[],"r":["vg-generosity-energy","vve-priming"],"f":["What is generosity of energy?","What are priming words?"],"src":[{"l":"Cues ch. 8"}]},{"i":"vve-vocal-invitations","w":["vve"],"c":"listening","t":"Vocal invitations","a":["listening sounds","mm hm","uh huh","and then","encourage them to talk"],"s":"Little listening sounds and nudges — \"Mm-hm… and then?\" — invite people to keep going.","d":"","u":"Pair them with a slow nod.","e":[],"r":["vve-triple-nod","vve-tell-me-more"],"f":["What is the triple nod?","What is \"tell me more\"?"],"src":[{"l":"Cues ch. 8"}]},{"i":"vve-vocal-variety","w":["vve"],"c":"voice","t":"Vocal variety and a nonverbal script","a":["vocal variety","monotone","boring voice","script my delivery","presentation voice"],"s":"Vary your voice so people stay with you. For important talks, Vanessa suggests marking your script with where to smile, pause and emphasise.","d":"","u":"Mark three words to stress and one place to pause in your next talk.","e":[],"r":["vg-melody","vg-rate","vve-vocal-power"],"f":["What is Vinh's siren technique?","How does Vinh think about rate?"],"src":[{"l":"Cues ch. 8"}]},{"i":"vve-verbal-chameleon","w":["vve"],"c":"voice","t":"Warm vs competent words (verbal chameleon)","a":["word choice","warm words","competent words","email tone","match their style"],"s":"Count the warm words (thanks, excited, together) and competent words (results, plan, data) in what you write and say. Then shift the mix to match the other person's style — a verbal chameleon.","d":"","u":"Before sending an email, check you've got at least one warm word.","e":[],"r":["vve-charisma","ch-their-words"],"f":["What does Hughes say about using their words?","What is charisma?"],"src":[{"l":"Cues ch. 9"}]},{"i":"vve-imagery","w":["vve"],"c":"ideas","t":"Imagery cues","a":["profile photo","background","colours","clothes","video background"],"s":"Colours, props, backgrounds and photos also send warmth or competence signals — think about your profile picture or video background.","d":"","u":"Check your profile photo shows your hands or a real smile.","e":[],"r":["vve-charisma","vg-video"],"f":["What does Vinh say about video calls?","What is charisma?"],"src":[{"l":"Cues ch. 10"}]},{"i":"vve-curious-critical","w":["vve"],"c":"tough","t":"Curious before critical","a":["curious before critical","frustrated with someone","annoyed","judge someone"],"s":"When someone frustrates you, ask what explanation you'd give if you'd done the same thing. It turns judgment into curiosity.","d":"","u":"Ask \"What might be going on for them?\" before you react.","e":[],"r":["ch-people-are-reasons","vg-undercurrent"],"f":["What does Hughes mean by \"people are reasons\"?","What is the undercurrent?"],"src":[{"l":"Vanessa on TikTok, via Read Anyone handbook §5.1"}]},{"i":"vve-compliments","w":["vve"],"c":"warmth","t":"Memorable compliments","a":["compliment","compliments","give a compliment","praise","if you think something nice say it"],"s":"If you think something nice, say it. Make it specific, skip the obvious, and praise what they worked on rather than what they were born with.","d":"","u":"\"The way you handled that question was so calm\" beats \"You're great\".","e":[],"r":["vve-so-x","ch-compliments"],"f":["What does Hughes say about compliments?","What is \"You're so ___!\"?"],"src":[{"l":"Vanessa on X, Sep 2026 https://x.com/vvanedwards/status/2096268031156478239"},{"l":"Read Anyone handbook §5.1"}]},{"i":"vve-go-deeper","w":["vve"],"c":"deeper","t":"Go one level deeper","a":["go deeper","deep conversation","meaningful conversation","shallow conversation","move past small talk"],"s":"Research cited by Vanessa shows people expect deep talk to be more awkward than it is, and enjoy it more than they predict. Go one level deeper than feels comfortable — at their pace.","d":"","u":"Swap \"What do you do?\" for \"What's something you're working on that you actually care about?\"","e":[],"r":["vve-blueprint","vve-power-questions"],"f":["What is the Conversation Blueprint?","What are the nine power questions?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation research guide https://www.scienceofpeople.com/conversation-guide/"},{"l":"Kardas, Kumar & Epley 2022, as cited in the handbook"}]},{"i":"vg-about","w":["vg"],"c":"about","t":"Who is Vinh Giang?","a":["who is vinh","about vinh","vinh book","before we forget how to connect","askvinh"],"s":"Vinh Giang is a communication coach (and former magician) who teaches voice, body language, storytelling and listening. His book is Before We Forget How to Connect (2026). On this page he's the teacher for how you say things.","d":"The book has four parts: vocal skills (tonality, rate, melody, volume, pause), body language (presence, posture, gestures, expression, movement, eye contact), storytelling, and active listening. Every chapter ends with a \"Practical Magic\" exercise: record a Look Back, a Stretch, then Compare, and finish the sentence \"The one thing I need to work on is…\". Most \"How to say it\" cues on the Generate tab come from his book.","u":"Pick Giang on the Teacher chips when you want delivery-first lines.","e":[],"r":["vg-delivery","vg-instrument","vg-practical-magic"],"f":["Why does delivery matter so much?","What is the voice as an instrument?"],"src":[{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect, contents"},{"u":"https://vinhgiang.com/before-we-forget-how-to-connect","l":"Vinh's book page"}]},{"i":"vg-delivery","w":["vg"],"c":"ideas","t":"Delivery is influence","a":["how you say it","delivery","ideas ignored","nobody listens to me","influence"],"s":"Vinh's starting point: good ideas lose to poor delivery. How you say something decides whether it lands. Research backs the voice part — people who heard a pitch rated the speaker more competent than people who read the same words.","d":"","u":"Before an important line, decide how you'll say it, not just what you'll say.","e":[],"r":["vg-instrument","all-voice-competence"],"f":["What is the voice as an instrument?","What are the five vocal foundations?"],"src":[{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 1"},{"l":"Schroeder & Epley 2015, as cited in the handbook"}]},{"i":"vg-instrument","w":["vg"],"c":"voice","t":"Your voice is an instrument","a":["voice instrument","natural voice","record myself","communication mirror","voice baseline","vocal foundations","five foundations"],"s":"Vinh treats your voice as an instrument with five foundations: tonality, rate, melody, volume and pause. Most of us play only a few keys. He has you record a \"before\" video and review it three ways — audio only, muted video, and the transcript — then ask whether your habitual voice serves you.","d":"When something feels strange, he says don't call it fake. Your \"natural\" voice is mostly habits picked up from the people around you growing up.","u":"Record five minutes of yourself talking, wait a day, then review it three ways.","e":[],"r":["vg-tonality","vg-rate","vg-melody","vg-volume","vg-pause"],"f":["What is Vinh's tip for tonality?","How do I use pauses?"],"src":[{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 2"}]},{"i":"vg-tonality","w":["vg"],"c":"voice","t":"Tonality: your face drives your tone","a":["tone of voice","tonality","sound cold","sound angry","resting face","feel the weird","emotion in voice"],"s":"Your face drives your tone. Put an emotion on your face and you'll hear it in your voice. Vinh's \"Feel the weird\" lesson: hiding emotion makes you harder to connect with, so practise showing the seven core expressions out loud.","d":"He describes people reading his neutral face as unhappy, and fixing it by lifting the corners of his mouth slightly. His exercise has you record yourself making the faces for each emotion while speaking.","u":"Before a warm line, let your face warm up first.","e":[],"r":["vg-expression","vve-danger-cues"],"f":["What does Vinh say about facial expression?","What are the five vocal signals?"],"src":[{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 3"}]},{"i":"vg-rate","w":["vg"],"c":"voice","t":"Rate of speech: find your home base","a":["speak too fast","talk fast","slow down","speed","rate","words per minute","home base","rambling"],"s":"Vinh suggests a home base of about 150 words a minute — a 5 on his 1–10 speed scale. From there, play: speed up for energy, slow right down for the line that matters. If you feel you're rambling, you're probably going too fast.","d":"To calm someone who's upset, he says first match their pace, then gradually slow down and they'll tend to slow with you. The real magic is variety; a single speed loses people.","u":"Slow down on the one sentence you most want remembered.","e":[],"r":["vg-calm-pace","vg-pause","vg-melody"],"f":["How do I calm an upset person with my voice?","How does Vinh use pauses?"],"src":[{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 4"}]},{"i":"vg-calm-pace","w":["vg"],"c":"tough","t":"Calm someone by matching, then slowing","a":["calm someone down","upset person","panicking","anxious friend","slow them down","soothe"],"s":"When someone's upset and talking fast, Vinh's approach is to match their rate first, then slow down step by step. They tend to follow your pace down.","d":"","u":"Match, then slow, then lower your volume a touch.","e":[],"r":["vg-rate","vve-calm","vg-match-mirror-lead"],"f":["What is Match, Mirror and Lead?","What does Vanessa say about calming someone?"],"src":[{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 4"}]},{"i":"vg-melody","w":["vg"],"c":"voice","t":"Melody: break the monotone (siren technique)","a":["monotone","boring voice","flat voice","pitch","siren","range","uptalk","end sentences lower"],"s":"Most people use only about 20% of their pitch range. Vinh's siren technique: read a passage starting at your lowest note and slowly climb to your highest, then back down. It wakes up your range. Also: drop your pitch at the end of a statement so it sounds sure, not like a question.","d":"","u":"Try the siren for one minute, then read the same passage normally and hear the difference.","e":[],"r":["vve-vocal-power","vg-instrument"],"f":["What does Vanessa say about uptalk?","How do I find my volume?"],"src":[{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 5"}]},{"i":"vg-volume","w":["vg"],"c":"voice","t":"Volume: calibrate and match","a":["too quiet","too loud","speak up","volume","project my voice","whisper zone","leader zone"],"s":"Vinh maps volume on a 1–10 scale: Whisper Zone (1–2), Close Conversation Zone (3–4), Everyday Leader Zone (5–6), Projection Zone (7–8) and Emergency Zone (9–10). Ask three people where your normal volume sits, then practise moving between zones.","d":"With a quiet person, match their volume first so they relax. For a greeting, add a notch more than usual. It's not about being loud — it's about having the whole range available.","u":"Ask a friend: \"Where does my voice sit on a 1–10 scale?\"","e":[],"r":["vg-generosity-energy","vg-match-mirror-lead"],"f":["What is generosity of energy?","What is Match, Mirror and Lead?"],"src":[{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 6"}]},{"i":"vg-pause","w":["vg"],"c":"voice","t":"The pause: replace the filler","a":["pause","pauses","um","uh","ah","filler words","like","silence","stop saying um","vinh pauses","pause guy"],"s":"Vinh — who calls himself \"the Pause Guy\" — says don't fight your \"um\", replace it. When you feel a filler coming, pause instead: breathe, find your next thought, then carry on. A pause adds suspense, kills fillers, calms your body and lets people take in what you said.","d":"Where to pause: before you answer a question (you sound more considered), before an opinion (more thoughtful), before a punchline (bigger laugh), and before and after a key word. In a disagreement, an extra second of pause can stop you saying something you regret. His exercise: count your pauses and fillers in a recording, then re-record with a pause wherever an \"um\" was.","u":"Next time you're asked a question, pause for one breath before answering.","e":["\"Here is what I think. / We should wait a week.\" (pause at the slash, where an \"um\" would have gone)"],"r":["vve-vocal-power","vg-rate","vg-presence"],"f":["What is Vinh's home base speed?","What does Vanessa say about vocal power?"],"src":[{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 7"}]},{"i":"vg-presence","w":["vg"],"c":"body","t":"Presence: stillness","a":["fidget","fidgeting","nervous movement","sway","presence","stand still","be seen"],"s":"Stillness reads as presence. Vinh says stand or sit still and contained so your body stops leaking nerves — no swaying, tapping or fiddling.","d":"","u":"Plant your feet before you start speaking.","e":[],"r":["vg-posture-doorway","vg-movement","ch-move-slower"],"f":["What is the doorway reset?","What does Hughes say about moving slower?"],"src":[{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 8"}]},{"i":"vg-posture-doorway","w":["vg"],"c":"body","t":"Posture: the doorway reset","a":["posture","slouch","doorway","reset posture","first impression body","stand tall"],"s":"Every time you walk through a doorway, let it trigger a posture reset: drop your shoulders, lift your chest, ground yourself. Doorways are everywhere, and each one is a chance at a first impression. It takes two seconds.","d":"","u":"Try it on every doorway today until it becomes automatic.","e":[],"r":["vve-triple-threat","vg-presence"],"f":["What is Vanessa's Launch Stance?","What is the Power Sphere?"],"src":[{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 9"}]},{"i":"vg-power-sphere","w":["vg"],"c":"body","t":"Gestures: the Power Sphere and five gestures","a":["gestures","hands","what to do with my hands","power sphere","palms up","palms down","pointing","placater","leveler","blamer","thinker","distracter"],"s":"Keep your gestures in the Power Sphere — roughly between your navel and your eyes, elbows off your sides. Vinh teaches five gestures: Placater (palms up, to invite and build rapport), Leveler (palms down, firm and certain), Blamer (pointing — use rarely), Thinker (hand to chin, to show you're taking it in) and Distracter (a clap and wave to reset energy).","d":"Palms up versus palms down even changes your voice: say the same firm sentence with palms up and it sounds softer; with palms down it lands. Research suggests gestures help listeners understand.","u":"Palms up when you open a conversation, palms down when you make your key point.","e":[],"r":["vve-power-cues","vg-movement"],"f":["What does Vanessa say about power cues?","How should I move when I speak?"],"src":[{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 10"}]},{"i":"vg-expression","w":["vg"],"c":"body","t":"Expression: let your face speak","a":["blank face","facial expression","resting face","look bored","look unfriendly","unfreeze your face"],"s":"A blank face reads as \"doesn't care\". Vinh says let your face show what you feel, so people can read you and connect.","d":"","u":"Watch a muted recording of yourself — does your face match your words?","e":[],"r":["vg-tonality","vve-danger-cues"],"f":["How does your face change your tone?","What are danger-zone cues?"],"src":[{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 11"}]},{"i":"vg-movement","w":["vg"],"c":"body","t":"Move with purpose","a":["pacing","walking around","move on stage","presentation movement"],"s":"Only move to mark a new point; otherwise stay still. Vinh's \"three-zone story\" exercise has you tell a story in three parts, moving to a new spot for each one.","d":"","u":"Stand still while you make a point, then move as you change topics.","e":[],"r":["vg-presence","vg-power-sphere"],"f":["What is the Power Sphere?","How do I make eye contact in a group?"],"src":[{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 12"}]},{"i":"vg-eye-contact","w":["vg"],"c":"body","t":"Eye contact: triangle and one thought, one person","a":["eye contact","look people in the eye","staring","awkward eye contact","group eye contact","triangle technique"],"s":"If eye contact feels intense, Vinh's triangle technique helps: look at the left eye, the right eye, then the bridge of the nose, and repeat. In a group, finish a whole thought with one person before moving to the next — and avoid a robotic left-to-right sweep.","d":"","u":"Speaking to a group, say \"you\" to one person at a time while you look at them.","e":[],"r":["vve-triple-threat","vg-presence"],"f":["What is the Triple Threat?","What does Vinh say about video calls?"],"src":[{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 13 (p. 122)"}]},{"i":"vg-practice","w":["vg"],"c":"practice","t":"Practice: replace, don't just remove","a":["practice","habits","change a habit","good morning","daily practice","feels fake"],"s":"You can't just delete a habit; you replace it with a better one. Vinh suggests tiny daily reps, like a \"Good morning!\" with eye contact and a smile. He also says start with a pause, not an apology.","d":"","u":"Pick one habit and one replacement this week — not five.","e":[],"r":["vg-competence-stages","vg-practical-magic","vg-low-status"],"f":["Why does it feel fake?","What are low-status habits?"],"src":[{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 14"}]},{"i":"vg-competence-stages","w":["vg"],"c":"practice","t":"Four stages of competence","a":["feels fake","feels unnatural","awkward practising","fake","clunky","inauthentic"],"s":"Learning goes from not knowing you're bad, to knowing you're bad, to clunky and deliberate, to natural. The clunky third stage is where people quit. Vinh says when it feels fake, it's just unfamiliar — keep going.","d":"","u":"When a new habit feels weird, tell yourself \"unfamiliar, not fake\".","e":[],"r":["vg-practice","vg-instrument"],"f":["How should I practise?","What is Practical Magic?"],"src":[{"l":"@askvinh on YouTube https://www.youtube.com/@askvinh"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 14"}]},{"i":"vg-practical-magic","w":["vg"],"c":"practice","t":"Practical Magic: Look Back, Stretch, Compare","a":["practical magic","look back stretch compare","exercises","one thing","record yourself"],"s":"Every chapter in Vinh's book ends with a recorded exercise: Look Back at a baseline video, Stretch by exaggerating the skill, then Compare the two. You finish by writing \"The one thing I need to work on is…\".","d":"","u":"Do one Practical Magic a week, focused on a single skill.","e":[],"r":["vg-instrument","vg-practice"],"f":["What is the siren technique?","How do I count my filler words?"],"src":[{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect, all chapters"}]},{"i":"vg-generosity-energy","w":["vg"],"c":"openers","t":"Generosity of energy","a":["energy","greeting","say hello","enthusiasm","lift the room","flat greeting"],"s":"Give a little more life to your hello than feels necessary. Vinh calls it generosity of energy — it lifts the other person and the room.","d":"","u":"Add one notch of volume and a real smile to your next greeting.","e":[],"r":["vve-permission-warmth","vg-volume"],"f":["What is permission warmth?","How do I calibrate my volume?"],"src":[{"l":"Audible interview and On Purpose, via the Read Anyone inventory (G15)"}]},{"i":"vg-storytelling","w":["vg"],"c":"story","t":"Storytelling: the human superpower","a":["tell stories","storytelling","boring stories","story bank","collect stories"],"s":"Vinh calls storytelling the human superpower. Start a \"Story Bank\" note on your phone: whenever something moves you — frustration, surprise, pride, embarrassment, joy — write down what happened. Then fill in the 4Ws: Who, What, Where, When.","d":"","u":"Add one entry to your Story Bank this week.","e":[],"r":["vg-spark-point-link","vve-story-stack"],"f":["What is Spark–Point–Link?","What makes a story boring?"],"src":[{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 15"}]},{"i":"vg-story-bank","w":["vg"],"c":"story","t":"Building your Story Bank","a":["story bank","what stories should i have","stories to tell"],"s":"Collect stories in categories: high-stakes moments, vulnerable moments and funny moments. Pick ones where you still remember exactly how you felt.","d":"","u":"Write the working title and the 4Ws before you write the story itself.","e":[],"r":["vg-storytelling","vg-stakes","vg-vulnerability","vg-humour"],"f":["What are stakes?","How do I tell a vulnerable story?"],"src":[{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 15–18"}]},{"i":"vg-stakes","w":["vg"],"c":"story","t":"Stakes: make people lean in","a":["stakes","make story interesting","hook","tension"],"s":"Say what could be lost. When people know what's on the line, they lean in.","d":"","u":"In your next story, add one sentence about what you stood to lose.","e":[],"r":["vg-spark-point-link","vg-story-bank"],"f":["What is Spark–Point–Link?","What does Vinh say about humour?"],"src":[{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 16"}]},{"i":"vg-vulnerability","w":["vg"],"c":"story","t":"Vulnerability builds trust","a":["vulnerable","vulnerability","share struggles","open up","be real","trust"],"s":"Share a real struggle, not a polished highlight. Vinh says the emotions you hide to protect people are often the ones that would connect you.","d":"","u":"Share something you've learned the hard way — but only what you've processed enough to share calmly.","e":[],"r":["vve-anti-perfect","ch-insecurities","vve-be-known"],"f":["Should I share my insecurities?","What is the second arrow?"],"src":[{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 17"},{"l":"ch. 3 \"Feel the Weird\""}]},{"i":"vg-humour","w":["vg"],"c":"story","t":"Humour: make them like you","a":["funny","humour","humor","jokes","be funny"],"s":"Self-aware, true, small stories are the easiest humour. You don't need jokes — you need honest moments told with timing (pause before the punchline).","d":"","u":"Add a \"Humorous Stories\" section to your Story Bank.","e":[],"r":["vve-humour","vg-pause"],"f":["Where should I pause?","What does Vanessa say about humour?"],"src":[{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 18"}]},{"i":"vg-boring","w":["vg"],"c":"story","t":"Why you lose them (don't be boring)","a":["boring","ramble","rambling","long stories","lose attention","people zone out"],"s":"Stories lose people through detours, corrections (\"No wait, it was Tuesday\") and starting too early. Cut anything that doesn't serve the point. Vinh names two traps: the story that never takes off, and the story that never lands.","d":"","u":"Start closer to the action and know your point before you begin.","e":[],"r":["vg-spark-point-link","vg-321"],"f":["What is Spark–Point–Link?","What is the 3-2-1 method?"],"src":[{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 19–20"}]},{"i":"vg-spark-point-link","w":["vg"],"c":"story","t":"Spark, Point, Link","a":["story formula","spark point link","how to tell a story","structure a story"],"s":"Vinh's story formula. Spark: start at a peak moment of emotion, action or insight, then fill in the 4Ws. Point: land one clear point. Link: connect it to the person listening, so it's about them too.","d":"","u":"Ask \"So what?\" until you find your point, then ask \"Why does this matter to them?\" for the link.","e":[],"r":["vg-link","vg-storytelling","vg-boring"],"f":["What is the Link?","What is relinking?"],"src":[{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 20"}]},{"i":"vg-link","w":["vg"],"c":"story","t":"The Link: make the story relevant","a":["link","make it relevant","why should they care"],"s":"The Link ties your story to this particular listener's life, so it isn't just about you.","d":"","u":"End with one sentence that starts \"And I think that's true for you too, because…\".","e":[],"r":["vg-spark-point-link","vg-relinking"],"f":["What is relinking?","What is Spark–Point–Link?"],"src":[{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 21"}]},{"i":"vg-relinking","w":["vg"],"c":"story","t":"Relinking and the second arrow","a":["second arrow","relink","painful story","reframe","self blame","failure story"],"s":"Vinh's two arrows: the first arrow is what happened; the second is the meaning you add afterwards (\"I'm a failure\"). Relinking means finding a kinder, truer meaning for an old painful story, so you stop firing second arrows at yourself.","d":"","u":"Write the first arrow (just the events), then the second (what you told yourself), then a new link.","e":[],"r":["vve-change-story","vg-link"],"f":["What does Vanessa say about changing the story?","What is the Link?"],"src":[{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 22 (Two Arrows, pp. 211–212)"}]},{"i":"vg-high-low-buffalo","w":["vg"],"c":"openers","t":"High, Low, Buffalo (fill the shelf)","a":["high low buffalo","buffalo","icebreaker","group icebreaker","fill the shelf","get to know people fast"],"s":"Each person shares a high (something good), a low (something hard) and a buffalo (something random about them). One turn gives three threads, so a group of four has a dozen places to go. Vinh says go first and give a rich answer — your depth gives others permission.","d":"His two-shelf exercise: try it with someone you barely know, and with someone you've known for years.","u":"Use it at the start of a team meeting or a dinner.","e":[],"r":["vg-threads","ch-go-first","vve-ask-good"],"f":["What are conversational threads?","What does \"go first\" mean?"],"src":[{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 23"},{"l":"On Purpose with Jay Shetty https://podcasts.happyscribe.com/on-purpose-with-jay-shetty/vinh-giang-how-to-communicate-so-people-actually-listen-to-you"}]},{"i":"vg-threads","w":["vg"],"c":"listening","t":"Conversational threads","a":["threads","thread pulling","what to ask next","follow the conversation"],"s":"Every answer offers threads — a detail, a flash of emotion, a shift in tone, folded arms. Vinh says offer threads of your own and gently pull on theirs, in words, voice and body.","d":"","u":"Pick the thread with the most feeling in it and ask about that.","e":[],"r":["vg-vocal-radar","vve-thread-theory","ch-four-threads"],"f":["What is the vocal radar?","What is Hughes's Four Threads?"],"src":[{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 23, 26"},{"l":"Vinh on TikTok"}]},{"i":"vg-listening","w":["vg"],"c":"listening","t":"Listening: the bigger half","a":["listening","be a better listener","how to listen","listen better","active listening"],"s":"Vinh calls listening the bigger half of connection. At the end of a conversation, ask yourself \"What seemed to matter most to them?\" — and ask a friend how you could listen better.","d":"","u":"After your next conversation, answer that question in one line.","e":[],"r":["vg-traps","vg-reflection","ch-heard-seen"],"f":["What are the five listening traps?","How do I reflect what I heard?"],"src":[{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 24"}]},{"i":"vg-traps","w":["vg"],"c":"listening","t":"Name your trap: five passive listening traps","a":["listening traps","judging","giving advice","one upping","mind reading","daydreaming","bad listener","fix things"],"s":"Vinh's five passive listening traps: judging, giving advice, one-upping, mind reading and daydreaming. For a week, just notice which one you fall into most — you have to catch a habit before you can change it.","d":"","u":"Finish the sentence: \"My number one listening trap is…\"","e":[],"r":["vg-last-word","vg-listening"],"f":["What is Last Word, Last Letter?","How do I reflect what I heard?"],"src":[{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 25"}]},{"i":"vg-reflection","w":["vg"],"c":"listening","t":"Reflection: notice, reflect, clarify","a":["reflect","reflection","repeat back","paraphrase","active listening","reflect their word"],"s":"Notice a strong word they used, reflect it back (\"Trapped?\"), pause, then clarify. Reflective listening has good research behind it and makes people feel genuinely understood.","d":"","u":"Repeat one key word with a gentle rise, then wait.","e":[],"r":["ch-verbal-mirror","vg-undercurrent"],"f":["What is Hughes's verbal mirror?","What is the undercurrent?"],"src":[{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 26"}]},{"i":"vg-vocal-radar","w":["vg"],"c":"listening","t":"Vocal radar: the five vocal signals","a":["vocal signals","tone change","they sound different","voice change","hear what changed"],"s":"The five vocal foundations, heard in someone else: a change in rate, volume, melody, tonality (warm to cold) or an unexpected pause. Don't analyse every sentence — just notice when their voice doesn't match the rest of the conversation, then gently ask.","d":"","u":"\"You slowed down when you mentioned your sister — what's going on there?\"","e":[],"r":["vg-visual-radar","vg-threads","ch-baseline"],"f":["What is visual radar?","What does Hughes mean by baseline?"],"src":[{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 27 (Thread Pulling, pp. 260–262)"}]},{"i":"vg-visual-radar","w":["vg"],"c":"listening","t":"Visual radar: see what they're not saying","a":["visual radar","body changes","read body language","notice body language"],"s":"Notice what changes in their body — posture, hands, face — and ask about it rather than assuming what it means.","d":"","u":"Name what you saw as a question: \"You went quiet there — all good?\"","e":[],"r":["vg-vocal-radar","ch-baseline","lo-lie-spotting"],"f":["Can you tell if someone is lying from body language?","What is baseline?"],"src":[{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 28"}]},{"i":"vg-undercurrent","w":["vg"],"c":"listening","t":"The undercurrent: hear what they mean","a":["undercurrent","emotional radar","feelings","what they really mean","name the feeling","empathy"],"s":"Ask yourself \"What might they be feeling or needing?\" Then name the feeling as a question. Labelling a feeling, research shows, can turn its intensity down.","d":"","u":"\"Sounds like that left you feeling a bit unseen?\"","e":[],"r":["vg-reflection","vve-calm","all-label-feeling"],"f":["How does Vanessa calm someone down?","What is reflection?"],"src":[{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 29"}]},{"i":"vg-last-word","w":["vg"],"c":"listening","t":"Last Word, Last Letter","a":["last word last letter","planning my reply","stop thinking about what to say","listening game"],"s":"A theatre game Vinh used to stop himself planning his reply while someone was still talking: start your response with a word beginning with the last letter of the last word they said. It forces you to listen to the end.","d":"","u":"Play it with a friend for five minutes.","e":[],"r":["vg-traps","vg-listening"],"f":["What are the listening traps?","What is the undercurrent?"],"src":[{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 29 (p. 274)"},{"l":"On Purpose with Jay Shetty"}]},{"i":"vg-three-followups","w":["vg"],"c":"listening","t":"Three follow-ups on strong emotion","a":["three follow ups","someone is emotional","dont make it about me","strong feelings"],"s":"When someone shows strong feeling, ask three follow-up questions before you say anything about yourself.","d":"","u":"Count them on your fingers if you need to.","e":[],"r":["all-follow-ups","vg-traps"],"f":["Why do follow-up questions work?","What is one-upping?"],"src":[{"l":"On Purpose with Jay Shetty https://podcasts.happyscribe.com/on-purpose-with-jay-shetty/vinh-giang-how-to-communicate-so-people-actually-listen-to-you"}]},{"i":"vg-situation-not-person","w":["vg"],"c":"tough","t":"Argue at the situation, not the person","a":["argument","arguing","fight with partner","conflict","disagreement","argue"],"s":"In a conflict, reflect their emotion first, then say what upset you about the situation, not what's wrong with them.","d":"","u":"\"I can hear you're exhausted. What got to me was the plan changing last minute.\"","e":[],"r":["vg-yes-and","ch-resonate","vve-feedback"],"f":["What is \"yes, and\"?","What does Hughes say about resonating first?"],"src":[{"l":"On Purpose with Jay Shetty"}]},{"i":"vg-wins","w":["vg"],"c":"confidence","t":"Share wins without arrogance","a":["share good news","brag","humble brag","arrogant","talk about success"],"s":"Share a win by crediting the people who helped and what you learned along the way.","d":"","u":"\"We got it — and honestly, Sam's idea made the difference.\"","e":[],"r":["vve-at-ease"],"f":["How do I be at ease, not impressive?","What is generosity of energy?"],"src":[{"l":"On Purpose with Jay Shetty"}]},{"i":"vg-hard-to-interrupt","w":["vg"],"c":"voice","t":"Be hard to interrupt","a":["people interrupt me","get interrupted","talked over"],"s":"Vinh's take: finish your sentence, keep your volume steady, and keep your body still and present. People interrupt less when your voice and body show you're mid-thought.","d":"","u":"Don't speed up or drop volume when someone tries to cut in.","e":[],"r":["vve-interruptions","ch-interruptions"],"f":["What is the Bookmark?","How does Hughes handle interruptions?"],"src":[{"l":"On Purpose with Jay Shetty"}]},{"i":"vg-match-mirror-lead","w":["vg"],"c":"listening","t":"Match, Mirror and Lead","a":["match mirror lead","rapport","build rapport","match their energy","quiet person","mirroring","pacing and leading"],"s":"Vinh's rapport method. Match and mirror their vocal foundations and body language first — if they're quiet and slow, lower and slow down — so they relax. Once you're in sync, lead gently with a question, a lighter tone or a decision. Don't yank a nervous person's energy up.","d":"","u":"Start near their pace and volume, then lead a notch at a time. Obvious copying backfires.","e":[],"r":["vve-mirror","vg-calm-pace","vg-what-they-want"],"f":["What does Vanessa say about mirroring?","What do people want from a conversation?"],"src":[{"l":"Vinh's blog https://vinhgiang.com/blog/how-to-build-rapport-with-absolutely-anyone"}]},{"i":"vg-what-they-want","w":["vg"],"c":"ideas","t":"What they want from this chat","a":["what do people want","conversation goal","information connection harmony fun","read the room"],"s":"Vinh says most people want one of four things from a conversation: information (be clear and short), connection (warmth), harmony (keep the peace) or fun. Spot it, then tailor how you talk.","d":"","u":"Ask yourself in the first minute: which of the four is this?","e":[],"r":["vg-match-mirror-lead","all-six-needs"],"f":["What are the six needs?","What is Match, Mirror and Lead?"],"src":[{"l":"Vinh's blog https://vinhgiang.com/blog/how-to-build-rapport-with-absolutely-anyone"},{"l":"DOAC 2025 https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oIiv_335yus"}]},{"i":"vg-yes-and","w":["vg"],"c":"tough","t":"\"Yes, and…\"","a":["yes and","yes but","build on ideas","improv","disagree politely"],"s":"Borrowed from improv: \"Yes, and…\" builds on what they said; \"yes, but…\" wipes it out.","d":"","u":"Swap one \"but\" for \"and\" in your next disagreement.","e":[],"r":["vg-situation-not-person","ch-resonate"],"f":["How do I argue at the situation, not the person?","What is resonate before redirect?"],"src":[{"l":"Vinh on Instagram, Jun 2025, via the Read Anyone handbook"}]},{"i":"vg-321","w":["vg"],"c":"confidence","t":"3-2-1: when you're put on the spot","a":["put on the spot","impromptu","answer a question","mind goes blank","the one thing is"],"s":"When you're asked something on the spot, give structure: three steps, two types, or one thing (\"The one thing I'd say is…\").","d":"","u":"Pick the structure first, then fill it in.","e":[],"r":["vg-pause","vg-boring"],"f":["Where should I pause?","What makes a story boring?"],"src":[{"l":"@askvinh on YouTube https://www.youtube.com/@askvinh"}]},{"i":"vg-ford","w":["vg"],"c":"smalltalk","t":"FORD topics","a":["ford","family occupation recreation dreams","what to talk about","small talk topics"],"s":"Family, Occupation, Recreation, Dreams — four safe areas for small talk. Vinh's favourite question: \"What do you do in your free time?\"","d":"","u":"If you're stuck, pick the next letter.","e":[],"r":["ch-fork","vve-treasure"],"f":["What is Hughes's FORK?","What is the treasure hunt?"],"src":[{"l":"@askvinh on YouTube https://www.youtube.com/@askvinh"}]},{"i":"vg-tell-me-more","w":["vg"],"c":"listening","t":"\"Tell me more\" (Vinh)","a":["tell me more vinh","buy time","dont know what to say"],"s":"Vinh says \"Tell me more\" does two jobs: it makes the person feel heard and it buys you time to think.","d":"","u":"Use it when you're not sure what to say next.","e":[],"r":["vve-tell-me-more","vg-reflection"],"f":["What is reflection?","What does Vanessa say about \"tell me more\"?"],"src":[{"l":"DOAC 2025 https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oIiv_335yus"}]},{"i":"vg-low-status","w":["vg"],"c":"confidence","t":"Low-status habits","a":["low status","apologise too much","sorry","shrinking","rushing","sound weak"],"s":"Vinh points out habits that lower how people see you: over-apologising, shrinking your body, and rushing. Notice them, then replace them — start with a pause, not an apology.","d":"","u":"Replace your opening \"Sorry, quick question\" with a pause and the question.","e":[],"r":["vg-practice","ch-plain-speech"],"f":["Which words should I drop?","How do I replace a habit?"],"src":[{"l":"@askvinh on YouTube"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 14"}]},{"i":"vg-video","w":["vg"],"c":"body","t":"Video presence","a":["video call","zoom","teams call","on camera","online meeting"],"s":"Camera on at hello, torso and hands in shot, good light and sound, and a bit more energy than in person.","d":"","u":"Raise your camera to eye level and sit back enough that your hands show.","e":[],"r":["vg-generosity-energy","vve-imagery"],"f":["What are imagery cues?","What is generosity of energy?"],"src":[{"l":"@askvinh on YouTube"},{"l":"On Purpose with Jay Shetty"}]},{"i":"vg-entering-group","w":["vg"],"c":"groups","t":"Entering a group conversation","a":["join a group","join conversation","walk up to group","networking group","break into a group"],"s":"Hover near the group, catch someone's eye, wait for a natural gap, then add to the topic they're already on rather than changing it.","d":"","u":"Your first line should build on what they're discussing.","e":[],"r":["vve-social-game-plan","ch-barkeep"],"f":["What is the Social Game Plan?","What is Hughes's Barkeep?"],"src":[{"l":"@askvinh \"How to enter conversations\" https://www.youtube.com/@askvinh"},{"l":"On Purpose with Jay Shetty"}]},{"i":"ch-about","w":["ch"],"c":"about","t":"Who is Chase Hughes?","a":["who is chase","about chase","chase book","behavior ops manual","nci","ellipsis manual","six minute x-ray"],"s":"Chase Hughes is a former US Navy behaviour trainer who teaches influence, body language and interrogation, mainly through The Behavior Ops Manual (2023) and his NCI University courses. On this page he's the teacher for the small-talk loop, composure, plain speech and the six needs as listening prompts.","d":"Much of his manual is written for influence and interrogation, so Read Anyone leaves far more of his work out than the other two teachers' — 40 of his 83 listed techniques. His credentials and claims are disputed by critics and none of his models has published validation, so treat his ideas as a coach's view: useful, untested.","u":"Pick Hughes on the Teacher chips for small talk, composure and plain, steady lines.","e":[],"r":["ch-smalltalk","all-six-needs","lo-overview"],"f":["What does Read Anyone leave out from Hughes?","What is the small-talk formula?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual"},{"u":"https://nci.university/","l":"NCI University"},{"l":"Read Anyone handbook §3, §5.3"}]},{"i":"ch-smalltalk","w":["ch"],"c":"smalltalk","t":"The small-talk formula: Listen, Display, React, Ask, Offer","a":["small talk formula","small talk","listen display react ask offer","how to do small talk","small talk loop","keep a conversation going"],"s":"Hughes's five-step loop: Listen properly; Display it on your face (react visibly to the high points); React with a few words; Ask one small follow-up question; then Offer a related story of your own. It matches research showing follow-up questions make people like you more.","d":"His stated aim for small talk is that the other person leaves feeling interesting — not that you seem interesting.","u":"Run the loop once in your next chat: listen, show it, react, ask, then offer.","e":["They: \"We just got back from Lisbon.\" You (face lights up): \"Oh, nice!\" (react) \"What was the highlight?\" (ask) …then later, \"I went years ago and got lost for a day — best day of the trip.\" (offer)"],"r":["ch-interesting","vve-treasure","all-follow-ups"],"f":["What does \"interesting, not interested\" mean?","What is FORK?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 91–93, 448–450"}]},{"i":"ch-interesting","w":["ch"],"c":"smalltalk","t":"Make them feel interesting, not interested","a":["interesting not interested","make them feel interesting","be interesting"],"s":"Hughes's goal for small talk: the other person should leave feeling interesting. Your curiosity does that — not your own clever stories.","d":"","u":"Ask yourself afterwards: \"Did they leave feeling interesting?\"","e":[],"r":["ch-smalltalk","ch-role"],"f":["What is the small-talk formula?","What is role application?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual p. 91"}]},{"i":"ch-one-new-thing","w":["ch"],"c":"smalltalk","t":"Learn one new thing every conversation","a":["learn something new","one new thing","old friends","curiosity"],"s":"Set yourself a small goal: learn one new thing about the person in every conversation, even with people you've known for years. It keeps you curious and makes them feel interesting. Vanessa shares a similar habit.","d":"","u":"\"What's something you've got into lately that I don't know about?\"","e":[],"r":["ch-smalltalk","ch-interesting"],"f":["How do I remember names and stories?","What is the small-talk formula?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 92, 449"},{"l":"Vanessa on X https://x.com/vvanedwards/status/2095555287851913413"}]},{"i":"ch-names-stories","w":["ch"],"c":"names","t":"Remember names and stories","a":["remember names","forget names","remember details","bad memory","names"],"s":"Hughes says remembering names and stories is about motivation, not memory. Decide it matters, and keep notes on what people tell you.","d":"","u":"After a conversation, jot down their name and one detail in your phone.","e":[],"r":["vve-name-game","ch-use-names","vve-last-time"],"f":["What is the Name Game?","How should I use names?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 92, 448"}]},{"i":"ch-one-liners","w":["ch"],"c":"openers","t":"Opening one-liners","a":["opening lines","openers","how to start a conversation","conversation starters","first line"],"s":"Hughes suggests simple, open starters like \"How did you get started in the industry?\" or \"What's your take on all this?\" — questions that invite a story or opinion.","d":"","u":"Use the setting: \"What's your take on the talk?\"","e":[],"r":["vve-power-questions","ch-fork"],"f":["What is FORK?","What are Vanessa's power questions?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual p. 96"}]},{"i":"ch-fork","w":["ch"],"c":"smalltalk","t":"FORK topics","a":["fork","family occupation recreation knowledge","small talk topics","what to talk about"],"s":"A military acronym Hughes uses for small talk: Family (people close to them), Occupation (what they do and like most about it), Recreation (what they do for fun) and Knowledge (what they know a lot about). Vinh's FORD is a close cousin, with Dreams instead of Knowledge.","d":"","u":"When you run dry, pick the next letter.","e":[],"r":["vg-ford","ch-four-threads"],"f":["What is Vinh's FORD?","What is the Four Threads formula?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual p. 96"}]},{"i":"ch-four-threads","w":["ch"],"c":"smalltalk","t":"The Four Threads formula","a":["four threads","answer questions better","where are you from","short answers","give them something to grab"],"s":"When someone asks you a basic question, answer with four hooks they can grab instead of one word. \"Where are you from?\" \"Houston\" ends the chat; \"Originally Houston, but I left at eighteen for the navy and spent years in Hawaii\" gives them four threads.","d":"","u":"Practise a four-thread answer to \"Where are you from?\" and \"What do you do?\"","e":[],"r":["vg-threads","vve-logline"],"f":["What are Vinh's conversational threads?","How do I answer \"What do you do?\""],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 96–97, 450"}]},{"i":"ch-barkeep","w":["ch"],"c":"groups","t":"Barkeep: meet, then introduce","a":["barkeep","networking","introduce groups","work the room","party","meet people at an event"],"s":"Hughes's social challenge from military training: meet a small group and learn their names, then meet a second group, then introduce the second group to the first. Keep connecting people. Soon you're the thing everyone has in common. You can stop at any phase.","d":"","u":"At your next event, aim to introduce just two people who haven't met.","e":[],"r":["vve-connector","vve-highlighter","ch-smalltalk"],"f":["What is a connector?","What is the Highlighter?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 451–452"}]},{"i":"ch-compliments","w":["ch"],"c":"warmth","t":"Sincere compliments","a":["compliment","compliments","flattery","suck up"],"s":"Compliment freely, Hughes says, but never suck up. A compliment must be true and not tied to a request.","d":"","u":"Give the compliment and ask for nothing afterwards.","e":[],"r":["vve-compliments","lo-brain-pharmacist"],"f":["What does Vanessa say about compliments?","Why is compliment-then-ask left out?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual p. 131"}]},{"i":"ch-insecurities","w":["ch"],"c":"warmth","t":"Share your insecurities","a":["insecurities","imperfect","vulnerable","flaws","share weakness"],"s":"Openness invites openness. Hughes notes people like imperfect people, so sharing a real insecurity can make you easier to connect with.","d":"","u":"Share something small and true, not a dramatic confession.","e":[],"r":["vve-anti-perfect","vg-vulnerability"],"f":["What does Vinh say about vulnerability?","What is the Franklin Effect?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual p. 132"}]},{"i":"ch-reaction","w":["ch"],"c":"listening","t":"Sincere reaction statements","a":["reaction statement","you must be shattered","empathy statement","show understanding"],"s":"The honest core of what Hughes calls a provocative statement: reflect what their day probably felt like — \"You must be shattered\" — to show understanding. Read Anyone keeps only this sincere use, never using statements to fish for information.","d":"","u":"Say it, then stop and let them respond.","e":[],"r":["lo-elicitation","vg-undercurrent"],"f":["Why is elicitation left out?","What is the undercurrent?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 348–349"},{"l":"DOAC 2024 https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RvjR9GM2kX8"}]},{"i":"ch-verbal-mirror","w":["ch"],"c":"listening","t":"The verbal mirror","a":["verbal mirror","repeat their words","repeat last words","echo","mirroring words"],"s":"Repeat the last few words someone said, or the theme, back to them. It shows you're listening and invites them to say more. Hughes draws it from FBI practice; Chris Voss teaches a similar mirror.","d":"","u":"They: \"It's been a strange month.\" You: \"A strange month?\" Then wait.","e":[],"r":["vg-reflection","vve-mirror"],"f":["What is Vinh's reflection?","What does mirroring mean?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 354–356"}]},{"i":"ch-go-first","w":["ch"],"c":"warmth","t":"Go first (sincere self-disclosure)","a":["go first","share first","self disclosure","open up first"],"s":"Share something of your own to make it easier for them to share. Read Anyone keeps only the sincere version of what Hughes calls \"informational altruism\" — sharing to connect, not to obligate them.","d":"","u":"Share at the depth you'd like them to match, then ask.","e":[],"r":["vg-high-low-buffalo","lo-elicitation"],"f":["What is High, Low, Buffalo?","Why is elicitation left out?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual p. 350"}]},{"i":"ch-teach-me","w":["ch"],"c":"smalltalk","t":"Sincere \"teach me\"","a":["teach me hughes","ask their expertise","expert","dont know"],"s":"Show real interest in their expertise when you genuinely don't know. Pretending not to know to draw information out (feigned naivety) is left out.","d":"","u":"Only ask what you really want to learn.","e":[],"r":["vve-teach-me","lo-elicitation"],"f":["What does Vanessa say about \"teach me\"?","What is elicitation?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual p. 356"}]},{"i":"all-six-needs","w":["ch"],"c":"ideas","t":"The six needs (listening prompts)","a":["six needs","needs map","social needs","significance","approval","acceptance","intelligence","pity","hardship","power","strength","lean chips","what is lean","human needs"],"s":"Hughes describes six social needs: significance, approval, acceptance, intelligence, pity (shown here as Hardship) and strength or power. On Read Anyone they're the Lean chips — listening prompts for the quiet question someone might be asking, never labels for people. Most people show a mix.","d":"The quiet questions: Significance — do people see my impact? Approval — am I doing this right? Acceptance — do I belong? Intelligence — do people see I'm sharp? Hardship — does anyone see how hard this has been? Power — do I get the final say? Answer the quiet question sincerely. Never say \"you're a significance person\", and never use needs to steer or sell — that use is left out.","u":"Listen for what they're showing right now; if you're unsure, pick Anyone and ask a follow-up.","e":[],"r":["lo-needs-steering","all-sincerity","vg-what-they-want"],"f":["Why don't you use needs to persuade?","What is the sincerity test?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 366–372"},{"u":"https://www.youtube.com/shorts/YzJI8GTHteQ","l":"Chase's needs Short"},{"l":"Read Anyone handbook §6"}]},{"i":"ch-values-map","w":["ch"],"c":"listening","t":"Values Map: \"so I can finally…\"","a":["values","values map","what they want","end goal","so i can finally"],"s":"Listen for the end goal behind what people want — often heard as \"so I can finally…\". Hughes lists six values: connection, information, recognition, experience, freedom and growth. Read Anyone uses this only to ask honest questions about what matters to them.","d":"","u":"\"And once that's sorted, what will it let you do?\"","e":[],"r":["all-six-needs","vve-primary-value"],"f":["What are the six needs?","What is Primary Value?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 383–386"}]},{"i":"ch-decision-style","w":["ch"],"c":"ideas","t":"Decision styles, spoken honestly","a":["decision style","how people decide","give reasons","deviance novelty social conformity necessity investment"],"s":"Hughes describes six decision styles: deviance, novelty, social, conformity, necessity and investment (people often use two neighbouring ones). Read Anyone uses them only to give the honest reason that matters to how someone decides — practical, value, new, or what others do — never to pressure.","d":"","u":"If they care about practicality, lead with the practical reason, truthfully.","e":[],"r":["lo-persuasion-scripts","all-sincerity"],"f":["Why are persuasion scripts left out?","What is the sincerity test?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 378–382"}]},{"i":"ch-their-words","w":["ch"],"c":"listening","t":"Use their words","a":["use their words","their language","pronouns","i we they","sensory words"],"s":"Notice the words and pronouns people use (I, we, they; see, hear, feel) and speak in their terms — sincerely, to be understood. Using their words to steer a pitch is left out, and research doesn't support \"visual/auditory/kinaesthetic\" matching.","d":"","u":"If they say \"the team\", talk about \"the team\", not \"you\".","e":[],"r":["vve-verbal-chameleon","lo-vak"],"f":["Why is sensory-preference persuasion left out?","What is a verbal chameleon?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 393–400"}]},{"i":"ch-heard-seen","w":["ch"],"c":"ideas","t":"Make people feel heard and seen","a":["heard and seen","most important people skill","feel heard","without judgment","ai"],"s":"In his 2026 Diary of a CEO interview, Hughes said the most important people skill is making people feel heard and seen — resonating with them without judging. He thinks it will matter more as AI grows.","d":"","u":"Before responding, check: have I shown I heard them?","e":[],"r":["ch-resonate","vg-listening"],"f":["What is resonate before redirect?","What is listening the bigger half?"],"src":[{"l":"DOAC 2026 https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9uSXOr-AdAU"}]},{"i":"ch-resonate","w":["ch"],"c":"tough","t":"Resonate before you redirect","a":["resonate","acknowledge first","disagree","change their mind","their river","opposing view"],"s":"Acknowledge someone's view first, genuinely, before you offer yours. Hughes calls it getting into their river before trying to steer the boat.","d":"","u":"\"I can see why that matters to you. Here's where I land…\"","e":[],"r":["vg-yes-and","ch-golden-bridge","vg-situation-not-person"],"f":["What is the golden bridge?","What is \"yes, and\"?"],"src":[{"l":"DOAC 2026 https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9uSXOr-AdAU"}]},{"i":"ch-people-are-reasons","w":["ch"],"c":"ideas","t":"People are reasons","a":["difficult people","annoying people","why people behave","everyone is suffering","mask","four ways of seeing people"],"s":"Hughes asks you to see everyone as carrying something and wearing a mask: behaviour has reasons, so people aren't broken — something is driving them.","d":"","u":"When someone's difficult, ask \"What might be behind this?\"","e":[],"r":["vve-curious-critical","vg-undercurrent"],"f":["What is curious before critical?","What is the undercurrent?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 297–302"}]},{"i":"ch-golden-bridge","w":["ch"],"c":"tough","t":"The golden bridge","a":["golden bridge","save face","back down","let them win","climb down","argument exit"],"s":"From Sun Tzu: build your opponent a golden bridge to retreat across. In everyday terms, let the other person back down without losing face.","d":"","u":"Give them an easy, dignified way out: \"With what you knew then, that made sense.\"","e":[],"r":["ch-resonate","vve-high-stakes"],"f":["What is resonate before redirect?","How do I handle a high-stakes conversation?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual p. 51"}]},{"i":"ch-interruptions","w":["ch"],"c":"tough","t":"Handling interruptions calmly","a":["interrupted","interruption","someone cuts me off","let me finish"],"s":"Stay calm and use their name: \"One sec, James — let me finish this thought.\" Then finish it.","d":"","u":"Keep your voice level and don't speed up.","e":[],"r":["vve-interruptions","vg-hard-to-interrupt"],"f":["What is the Bookmark?","How do I become hard to interrupt?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 454–455"}]},{"i":"ch-plain-speech","w":["ch"],"c":"voice","t":"Speak plainly: drop hedges","a":["hedging","hedge words","just","honestly","ill try","maybe","sound weak","tag questions","filler words"],"s":"Cut words that weaken what you say: \"just\", \"honestly\", \"I'll try\", \"maybe\" and needless tag questions (\"…, right?\"). Say a price, opinion or request once, calmly, then stop.","d":"","u":"Re-read your next email and delete every \"just\".","e":[],"r":["vve-confident-language","vg-low-status"],"f":["What are low-status habits?","How do I sound confident?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 453, 462–463"}]},{"i":"ch-objections","w":["ch"],"c":"tough","t":"Honest objection handling (five S's)","a":["objection","objections","pushback","they say no","skeptical","worried customer","handle objection"],"s":"Hughes's five steps, used here only with true statements: Sympathise (\"I felt the same\"), Socialise (\"many people do\"), Select (\"which part worries you most?\"), Show (the real picture) and Secure (\"does that help?\").","d":"","u":"Only say \"I felt the same\" if you did.","e":[],"r":["vve-nut-job","all-sincerity"],"f":["What is the NUT Job?","What is the sincerity test?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 538–539"}]},{"i":"ch-use-names","w":["ch"],"c":"names","t":"Use names and answer the implication","a":["use their name","names","hidden question","implication","tough question"],"s":"Use people's names, and when you're asked a question, answer both the question and its hidden implication — calmly.","d":"","u":"If asked \"Is this on schedule?\", answer that and the worry behind it.","e":[],"r":["ch-names-stories","vve-name-game"],"f":["What is the Name Game?","How do I remember names?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 887–889"}]},{"i":"ch-gratitude","w":["ch"],"c":"confidence","t":"Gratitude and enjoyment","a":["gratitude","enjoyment","magnetic","enjoy the moment","grateful"],"s":"Two of Hughes's authority traits are gratitude towards people and visible enjoyment of the moment. He calls enjoyment highly magnetic. Gratitude has good research support.","d":"","u":"Thank one person specifically today and let yourself enjoy the conversation.","e":[],"r":["ch-authority","vve-appreciation"],"f":["What is the authority triangle?","What is the Appreciation Matrix?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 148–153"}]},{"i":"ch-acss","w":["ch"],"c":"confidence","t":"ACSS: Authority, Comfort, Social skill, Skills","a":["acss","comfort","be comfortable","most comfortable person in the room"],"s":"Hughes's order of work: Authority and Comfort come before Social skills and Skills. Fix your comfort first — aim to be the most comfortable person in the room — and scripts matter much less.","d":"","u":"Before an event, work on calm rather than lines.","e":[],"r":["ch-composure","ch-authority"],"f":["What is composure?","What is the authority triangle?"],"src":[{"l":"DOAC 2024 https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RvjR9GM2kX8"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 938–943"}]},{"i":"ch-authority","w":["ch"],"c":"confidence","t":"Authority Triangle and five authority traits","a":["authority","presence","authority triangle","confidence discipline leadership gratitude enjoyment","life order"],"s":"Hughes sees authority as coming from behaviour, effect and habits, and from five traits: confidence, discipline, leadership, gratitude and enjoyment. He links it to order in your life (environment, time, appearance, social life, finances). It's self-work, not a tactic, and it isn't validated by research.","d":"","u":"Pick one area of life order to tidy this week.","e":[],"r":["ch-acss","ch-gratitude","ch-lifestyle"],"f":["What are the lifestyle traits?","What is ACSS?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 32, 122–198"}]},{"i":"ch-composure","w":["ch"],"c":"confidence","t":"Composure: avoid collapse and posturing","a":["composure","nervous","calm","collapse","posturing","shrink","puff up","pendulum","stay calm"],"s":"Hughes describes a pendulum with two traps at either end: collapse (shrinking, apologising, going small) and posturing (puffing up, acting big). Composure sits in the middle. When things go wrong, tell yourself \"this is part of life\".","d":"","u":"Notice which way you swing under pressure, and aim for the middle.","e":[],"r":["ch-move-slower","ch-nervous-excited","vg-presence"],"f":["Why move slower than the room?","Can I reframe nerves?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 154–158, 164"}]},{"i":"ch-performance-mode","w":["ch"],"c":"confidence","t":"Performance mode vs connection mode","a":["performance","performing","self conscious","overthinking","camera view","nervous talking"],"s":"Talking to people isn't a performance. Hughes says shift your focus onto them instead of on how you're coming across — like switching from a camera pointed at you to one pointed at them.","d":"","u":"When you feel self-conscious, ask them a question.","e":[],"r":["vve-at-ease","ch-role"],"f":["What is role application?","How do I be at ease?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual p. 164"}]},{"i":"ch-nervous-excited","w":["ch"],"c":"confidence","t":"Nervous = excited","a":["nervous","anxiety","anxious","butterflies","reframe nerves","im nervous"],"s":"Nerves and excitement feel almost the same in the body. Hughes suggests reframing nerves as excitement — research by Alison Wood Brooks supports saying \"I'm excited\" over \"calm down\".","d":"","u":"Before you walk in, say \"I'm excited\" to yourself.","e":[],"r":["ch-composure","ch-stop-worrying"],"f":["What is composure?","How do I stop worrying?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 164–165"}]},{"i":"ch-role","w":["ch"],"c":"confidence","t":"Role application","a":["role","give yourself a role","my job is","host mindset"],"s":"Give yourself a role before you walk in: \"My job is to make them feel interesting.\" A role takes the focus off you.","d":"","u":"Pick a role for your next event — host, connector or question-asker.","e":[],"r":["ch-interesting","ch-performance-mode"],"f":["What is performance mode?","What does \"interesting, not interested\" mean?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual p. 130"}]},{"i":"ch-rules-confidence","w":["ch"],"c":"confidence","t":"Rules of confidence","a":["confidence","confident","self doubt","comparing myself","fake it"],"s":"Hughes's rules: everyone's faking it to some degree; expect good outcomes; the worst case is usually small; don't compare yourself with others.","d":"","u":"Before something scary, name the realistic worst case — it's usually small.","e":[],"r":["ch-limiting-beliefs","ch-nervous-excited"],"f":["How do I change limiting beliefs?","Can I reframe nerves?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 129, 134"}]},{"i":"ch-limiting-beliefs","w":["ch"],"c":"confidence","t":"Limiting beliefs and self-talk","a":["limiting beliefs","self talk","growth mindset","negative thoughts","im not good at"],"s":"Spot the beliefs that hold you back (\"I'm bad with people\"), question them and rewrite them. Hughes pairs this with a growth mindset.","d":"","u":"Write one limiting belief and a truer rewrite.","e":[],"r":["ch-rules-confidence","vg-relinking"],"f":["What is the second arrow?","What are the rules of confidence?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 166–178"}]},{"i":"ch-lifestyle","w":["ch"],"c":"confidence","t":"Lifestyle traits","a":["lifestyle","life order","appearance","finances","environment","time management"],"s":"Hughes believes order in five areas — environment, time, appearance, social life and finances — shows up in how you carry yourself.","d":"","u":"Pick the area that feels messiest and make one small fix.","e":[],"r":["ch-authority"],"f":["What is the authority triangle?","What is ACSS?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 179–183"}]},{"i":"ch-move-slower","w":["ch"],"c":"body","t":"Move slower than the room","a":["move slowly","slow movements","calm energy","swimming pool","calm assertive","jittery"],"s":"Fear speeds the body up. Hughes's rule of thumb: never move faster than you would in a swimming pool. Slow, deliberate movement reads as calm and trustworthy.","d":"","u":"Walk in at pool speed.","e":[],"r":["vg-presence","ch-composure"],"f":["What does Vinh say about stillness?","What is composure?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 144, 880"}]},{"i":"ch-display","w":["ch"],"c":"body","t":"Display: let your face show their story","a":["display","react visibly","facial reaction","show interest"],"s":"React visibly to the high points of their story. Your face tells them you're with them — the \"Display\" step of the small-talk formula.","d":"","u":"Let your eyebrows and smile move when they hit the good part.","e":[],"r":["ch-smalltalk","vg-expression"],"f":["What is the small-talk formula?","What does Vinh say about expression?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 92, 129"}]},{"i":"ch-tonality","w":["ch"],"c":"voice","t":"Tonality and pauses (Hughes)","a":["emphasis","which word to stress","tone hughes","stress words"],"s":"Where you put the emphasis changes the meaning of a sentence, so choose the word you stress, and pause around the key words.","d":"","u":"Say \"I didn't say she took it\" stressing a different word each time.","e":[],"r":["vg-pause","vg-tonality"],"f":["How does Vinh use pauses?","How does your face change your tone?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 498–499"}]},{"i":"ch-mammal","w":["ch"],"c":"story","t":"Speak to the mammal: descriptive language","a":["descriptive language","concrete","vivid","story impact","reveal at the end"],"s":"Use concrete pictures people can see, and move the emotional reveal to the end of the sentence or story so it lands. Hughes outlines a Journey–Connection–Reveal shape.","d":"","u":"Replace one abstract word with a picture in your next story.","e":[],"r":["vg-spark-point-link","vve-story-stack"],"f":["What is Spark–Point–Link?","What is the Story Stack?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 452, 456–461, 473–484"}]},{"i":"ch-baseline","w":["ch"],"c":"body","t":"Baseline and change","a":["baseline","body language change","read body language","clusters","context","five cs","what does it mean when"],"s":"Notice changes from someone's normal (their baseline), read clusters of signals in context and culture rather than one gesture — then ask, never conclude. A shift usually means stress or discomfort, not a lie.","d":"","u":"Gently ask: \"Is this a comfortable thing to talk about?\"","e":[],"r":["lo-lie-spotting","vg-visual-radar","vg-vocal-radar"],"f":["Can you tell if someone is lying?","What is visual radar?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 216–217, 302"},{"l":"Read Anyone handbook §5.3"}]},{"i":"ch-stop-worrying","w":["ch"],"c":"confidence","t":"Stop-worrying questions and day-tight compartments","a":["worry","worrying","overthinking","anxious thoughts","day tight compartments"],"s":"Two questions: what's worrying me, and what can I do about it? Then live in \"day-tight compartments\" — one day at a time.","d":"","u":"Write both answers down, do the first step, let the rest wait.","e":[],"r":["ch-nervous-excited","ch-composure"],"f":["Can I reframe nerves?","What is composure?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 165–166"}]},{"i":"ch-coaching","w":["ch"],"c":"practice","t":"Self-coaching checklist","a":["review my conversations","self coaching","checklist","improve"],"s":"Hughes ends with a short self-coaching checklist for reviewing your own interactions afterwards.","d":"","u":"After an important chat, ask: did I listen, did they feel interesting, did I stay composed?","e":[],"r":["vg-practical-magic","ch-smalltalk"],"f":["What is Practical Magic?","What is the small-talk formula?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual p. 946"}]},{"i":"lo-overview","w":["ch"],"c":"leftout","t":"What Read Anyone leaves out","a":["excluded","left out","what is excluded","manipulation","manipulative techniques","dark psychology","covert","why exclude hughes"],"s":"Read Anyone leaves out 41 techniques — 40 from Hughes and 1 from Van Edwards — because they don't pass the sincerity test. They include covert influence models, embedded commands, hypnosis, identity hacking, interrogation methods, information extraction with false statements, pressure through fears or needs, and lie-spotting from body language.","d":"They're listed in the technique inventory so you can see what they are, but they never become lines. Where a technique has an honest core, the honest version is kept instead — for example, a sincere reaction statement instead of fishing statements, or a real \"teach me\" instead of pretending not to know.","u":"Ask about any one of them and I'll say briefly what it is and point you to an honest alternative.","e":[],"r":["all-sincerity","lo-embedded","lo-lie-spotting"],"f":["What is the sincerity test?","What are embedded commands?"],"src":[{"l":"Read Anyone technique inventory §3c https://github.com/mhccog/read-anyone/blob/main/research/techniques-inventory.md"},{"l":"Read Anyone handbook §2"}]},{"i":"lo-embedded","w":["ch"],"c":"leftout","t":"Embedded commands (left out)","a":["embedded commands","embedded command","hidden commands","nlp commands","deliberate ambiguity","covert suggestion"],"s":"An embedded command hides an instruction inside a normal sentence, often marked with a change of tone, hoping the listener acts on it without noticing. Hughes teaches them in the manual. Read Anyone leaves them out because they work by going around someone's awareness, so they fail the sincerity test.","d":"","u":"Make a clear, open request and give a real reason — \"Could you look at this by Friday, because the client needs it?\"","e":[],"r":["vve-assertive","vve-own-it","all-sincerity"],"f":["How do I make a clear ask?","What is the sincerity test?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 523–531, 819–823 (excluded, H60)"}]},{"i":"lo-hypnosis","w":["ch"],"c":"leftout","t":"Hypnosis and hypnotic language (left out)","a":["hypnosis","hypnotic language","covert hypnosis","trance","fractionation","inductions","double binds","presuppositions","implied directives"],"s":"Hughes's manual covers hypnotic language patterns, inductions, fractionation, double binds and presuppositions designed to guide someone's state without their full awareness. Read Anyone leaves all of it out because it's covert and fails the sincerity test.","d":"","u":"Tell a real story well with Vinh's Spark–Point–Link, and use pauses.","e":[],"r":["vg-spark-point-link","vg-pause","all-sincerity"],"f":["What is Spark–Point–Link?","What is the sincerity test?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 499–522, 566–581 (excluded, H59, H63)"}]},{"i":"lo-pcp","w":["ch"],"c":"leftout","t":"The PCP model (left out)","a":["pcp","perception context permission","get people to do things","behaviour change model"],"s":"Hughes's PCP model (Perception, Context, Permission) is a planning tool for getting someone to act out of character. Read Anyone leaves it out because it's built for covert influence rather than connection.","d":"","u":"Say what you want openly and make the other person's choice easy — Vanessa's \"Own It!\" and a clear ask.","e":[],"r":["vve-own-it","vve-assertive","lo-overview"],"f":["What is Own It?","What else is left out?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 29–31 (excluded, H44)"}]},{"i":"lo-models","w":["ch"],"c":"leftout","t":"Six-Axis, FATE and the Hierarchy of Influence (left out)","a":["six axis","six-axis model","fate model","hierarchy of influence","taxonomy","skills map","influence models"],"s":"These are Hughes's frameworks for planning compliance — which levers to pull to move someone. Read Anyone leaves them out because they're influence engineering, not connection.","d":"","u":"Vinh's Match, Mirror and Lead — get in sync first, then lead gently and openly.","e":[],"r":["vg-match-mirror-lead","lo-overview"],"f":["What is Match, Mirror and Lead?","What is the sincerity test?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 16–28, 33–37 (excluded, H45)"}]},{"i":"lo-frame","w":["ch"],"c":"leftout","t":"Frame control tactics (left out)","a":["frame control","frames","warning shot","pace their reality","those types","status games","yes set"],"s":"Tactics for winning status and agreement — calling out \"those types\", warning shots, pacing someone's reality to build a yes-set. They're left out because they pressure people through identity and status.","d":"","u":"Resonate before you redirect, and give them a golden bridge.","e":[],"r":["ch-resonate","ch-golden-bridge"],"f":["What is resonate before redirect?","What is the golden bridge?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 40–56 (excluded, H46)"}]},{"i":"lo-lie-spotting","w":["ch","vve"],"c":"leftout","t":"Lie-spotting from body language (left out)","a":["lie","lying","liar","spot a liar","tell if someone is lying","deception","detect lies","behavioral table of elements","deception rating scale","microexpressions lie"],"s":"Both Hughes (the Behavioral Table of Elements and a deception rating scale) and Van Edwards (microexpressions) present ways to read lies from the body. Read Anyone leaves lie-spotting out: research finds people are about 54% accurate at spotting lies — near chance — and microexpression training didn't reliably help.","d":"A change in someone's behaviour usually means stress or discomfort, not dishonesty. Treating a gesture as proof can damage trust and be plain wrong.","u":"Notice a change from their normal, then ask kindly — \"Is this a comfortable thing to talk about?\"","e":[],"r":["ch-baseline","vg-visual-radar","vve-decoder"],"f":["What is baseline?","What is visual radar?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 205–278 (excluded, H48)"},{"l":"Captivate ch. 6 (excluded, V91)"},{"l":"Bond & DePaulo 2006, as cited in the handbook"}]},{"i":"lo-xray","w":["ch"],"c":"leftout","t":"Six-Minute X-Ray and rapid profiling (left out)","a":["six minute x-ray","6mx","profiling","profile someone","behavior compass","eye accessing","blink rate","read anyone in minutes"],"s":"Hughes's Six-Minute X-Ray and related tools (Behavior Compass, eye-accessing cues, blink rates) claim to profile someone's needs, fears and decision style in minutes. Read Anyone leaves them out: there's no published validation, and brief impressions pick up only a little, not a full profile.","d":"","u":"Use the six needs only as listening prompts, and ask follow-up questions.","e":[],"r":["all-six-needs","all-follow-ups"],"f":["Are the six needs labels?","Why do follow-up questions work?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 279–422 (excluded, H49)"},{"l":"Ambady & Rosenthal 1992, as cited in the handbook"}]},{"i":"lo-elicitation","w":["ch"],"c":"leftout","t":"Elicitation by false statements (left out)","a":["elicitation","elicit information","false statements","bracketing","disbelief","criticism","complaint baiting","feigned naivety","hourglass","loaded questions","get information out of someone","interrogation style questions"],"s":"Elicitation gets people to reveal information without asking directly — saying something deliberately wrong so they correct you, bracketing numbers, feigning disbelief or naivety, or using an \"hourglass\" of questions. It was designed for intelligence work. Read Anyone leaves it out because it relies on deception and covert extraction.","d":"","u":"Ask genuine questions and follow-ups. Research shows people who ask more follow-up questions are liked more.","e":[],"r":["all-follow-ups","ch-reaction","ch-teach-me"],"f":["What is a sincere reaction statement?","Why do follow-up questions work?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 346–363 (excluded, H51, H52, H80, H81)"}]},{"i":"lo-needs-steering","w":["ch"],"c":"leftout","t":"Using needs or fears to steer people (left out)","a":["use needs to persuade","hidden fears","needs persuasion","feed their need","exploit needs","fears map"],"s":"Hughes also teaches using someone's dominant need or hidden fear as a lever — feeding it so they'll comply. Read Anyone leaves that use out. The six needs stay only as listening prompts.","d":"","u":"Answer the quiet question sincerely, with nothing asked in return.","e":[],"r":["all-six-needs","lo-brain-pharmacist"],"f":["What are the six needs?","Why is compliment-then-ask left out?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 373–377 (excluded, H50)"}]},{"i":"lo-brain-pharmacist","w":["ch"],"c":"leftout","t":"Compliment, then ask (left out)","a":["compliment then ask","brain pharmacist","flatter before asking","compliment before favour","butter up"],"s":"Hughes describes complimenting someone's core need right before making a request (\"become the brain pharmacist\"). Read Anyone leaves out compliment-then-ask sequencing because the compliment becomes a tool for the favour.","d":"","u":"Give compliments with nothing attached, and make requests separately and openly.","e":[],"r":["ch-compliments","vve-compliments","vve-assertive"],"f":["How do I give a good compliment?","How do I make a clear ask?"],"src":[{"l":"DOAC 2024; The Behavior Ops Manual p. 376 (excluded, H83)"}]},{"i":"lo-persuasion-scripts","w":["ch"],"c":"leftout","t":"Persuasion scripts and decision-map drills (left out)","a":["persuasion scripts","decision map scripts","sales scripts","persuasion","how to persuade","convince someone","influence someone"],"s":"The manual includes scripts that weave someone's decision style and values into pitches with embedded pointers. Read Anyone leaves them out because they use embedded commands and negative dissociation.","d":"","u":"Give the true reason that matters to how they decide, once, and let them choose.","e":[],"r":["ch-decision-style","vve-own-it"],"f":["What are decision styles?","What is Own It?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 601, 806–817 (excluded, H53)"}]},{"i":"lo-identity","w":["ch"],"c":"leftout","t":"Identity hacking and pre-commitment (left out)","a":["identity hacking","identity hack","consistency hack","pre commitment","get them to commit","foot in the door"],"s":"Getting someone to state a trait about themselves (\"I'm someone who helps\"), then using it to make a request they'd feel inconsistent refusing. Read Anyone leaves it out because it pressures people through their identity.","d":"","u":"Ask plainly and make \"no\" easy to say.","e":[],"r":["vve-assertive","vve-say-no"],"f":["How do I make a clear ask?","How do I say no?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 423–430, 489–493 (excluded, H55, H78)"}]},{"i":"lo-dissociation","w":["ch"],"c":"leftout","t":"Negative dissociation (left out)","a":["negative dissociation","positive association","pair with disliked group"],"s":"Pairing a behaviour with a group someone dislikes (or likes) to shift what they do. Left out as covert influence.","d":"","u":"Talk about the behaviour itself and why it matters to you.","e":[],"r":["vg-situation-not-person"],"f":["How do I argue at the situation, not the person?","What else is left out?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 493–497, 520–522 (excluded, H56)"}]},{"i":"lo-entrainment","w":["ch"],"c":"leftout","t":"Behavioural entrainment and body steering (left out)","a":["entrainment","behavioural entrainment","eyebrow flash steering","compliance wedge","social vacuum","nod inducing","get people to nod"],"s":"Techniques for leading someone's body to lead their mind — getting them to nod, steering with eyebrow flashes, using silence or objects to push compliance. Left out as covert compliance.","d":"","u":"Vanessa's warmth cues and the slow triple nod to show you're listening — not to steer.","e":[],"r":["vve-warmth-cues","vve-triple-nod"],"f":["What is the triple nod?","What are warmth cues?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 431–446 (excluded, H57)"}]},{"i":"lo-confusion","w":["ch"],"c":"leftout","t":"The confusion technique (left out)","a":["confusion technique","confuse then command","kelly method","confusion weapon"],"s":"Deliberately confusing someone, then giving a command while they're looking for clarity. Left out as covert.","d":"","u":"Be clear — 3-2-1 structure and a single clear point.","e":[],"r":["vg-321","ch-plain-speech"],"f":["What is the 3-2-1 method?","How do I speak plainly?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 540–566 (excluded, H62)"}]},{"i":"lo-scarcity","w":["ch"],"c":"leftout","t":"Scarcity, fear and strategic absence (left out)","a":["scarcity","fomo","fear of missing out","strategic absence","play hard to get","pressure tactics","grief process method"],"s":"Using scarcity, regret or fear of missing out — or withdrawing on purpose — to drive someone to act. Left out because it pressures through fear.","d":"","u":"Say what's true about timing once, calmly, and let them decide.","e":[],"r":["ch-plain-speech","ch-objections"],"f":["How do I handle objections honestly?","How do I speak plainly?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 531–538, 582–585 (excluded, H61, H64)"}]},{"i":"lo-touch","w":["ch"],"c":"leftout","t":"Timed touch as a tactic (left out)","a":["touch","when to touch someone","physical contact","nine best times to touch"],"s":"Hughes lists \"best times\" to make physical contact to deepen connection. Read Anyone leaves timed touch as a tactic out because touch needs consent, not strategy.","d":"","u":"Only light, welcome touch that you'd use anyway, and never as a technique.","e":[],"r":["vve-warmth-cues","all-sincerity"],"f":["What are warmth cues?","What is the sincerity test?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 585–586 (excluded, H65)"}]},{"i":"lo-bias-hacking","w":["ch"],"c":"leftout","t":"Hacking cognitive biases (left out)","a":["cognitive biases","anchoring","bias hacking","exploit biases","zeigarnik","reciprocity trick"],"s":"The manual walks through about 40 cognitive biases and how to exploit them. Knowing biases can protect you, but using them on people is left out as manipulation.","d":"","u":"Say the full, true picture and give people time.","e":[],"r":["ch-objections","lo-overview"],"f":["What else is left out?","How do I handle objections honestly?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 615–634 (excluded, H67)"}]},{"i":"lo-interrogation","w":["ch"],"c":"leftout","t":"Interrogation protocols (left out)","a":["interrogation","interview techniques","hbi","police interview","make someone confess","question someone"],"s":"Hughes's interrogation and interview protocols are built to get admissions under pressure. Left out: they're interrogation, not conversation.","d":"","u":"Ask open questions, reflect, and make it safe to talk — Vinh's reflection and Vanessa's \"tell me more\".","e":[],"r":["vg-reflection","vve-tell-me-more"],"f":["What is reflection?","What is \"tell me more\"?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 683–714, 836–842 (excluded, H70, H82)"}]},{"i":"lo-recruiter","w":["ch"],"c":"leftout","t":"Recruiter, cult and coercive formulas (left out)","a":["cult","cult formula","love bombing","recruiter","escalating deviance","isolation","free coffee script","kaptor","lilac","grace","sigma","avery"],"s":"The manual describes recruiter and cult formulas (love-bombing, isolation, escalating deviance), tactical protocols such as KAPTOR, LILAC, GRACE and SIGMA, and the Avery process for installing a persona. Read Anyone leaves all of these out as harmful. They're useful only to recognise if someone uses them on you.","d":"","u":"If someone's showering you with attention and pulling you away from others, slow down and talk it over with people you trust.","e":[],"r":["lo-overview","all-sincerity"],"f":["What is the sincerity test?","What else is left out?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 587–614, 659–683, 732–796 (excluded, H66, H69, H72, H73)"}]},{"i":"lo-vak","w":["ch"],"c":"leftout","t":"Sensory-preference persuasion (left out)","a":["vak","visual auditory kinesthetic","sensory words persuasion","nlp","representational systems"],"s":"Matching someone's \"visual, auditory or kinaesthetic\" words to make a pitch resonate. Left out: it's a persuasion use, and research doesn't support VAK matching.","d":"","u":"Simply use their words so you're understood.","e":[],"r":["ch-their-words"],"f":["How do I use their words?","What else is left out?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 393–397 (excluded, H76)"}]},{"i":"lo-memory","w":["ch"],"c":"leftout","t":"Memory, regression and state induction (left out)","a":["false memory","regression","past event recall","brainwave entrainment","sleep deprivation","borrowing emotion"],"s":"Techniques for triggering memories or states to steer someone, plus darker material on false memories and sleep deprivation. Left out as covert or harmful.","d":"","u":"Vinh's storytelling — share your own memories openly and invite theirs.","e":[],"r":["vg-storytelling","vg-high-low-buffalo"],"f":["What is High, Low, Buffalo?","What is the Story Bank?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 455–457, 484–489, 636–656 (excluded, H58, H68)"}]}],"glossary":[["sincerity test","all-sincerity"],["follow-up questions","all-follow-ups"],["liking gap","all-liking-gap"],["affect labelling","all-label-feeling"],["Conversation Blueprint","vve-blueprint"],["three levels","vve-blueprint"],["power questions","vve-power-questions"],["nine power questions","vve-power-questions"],["signature question","vve-signature"],["character question","vve-pq-character"],["treasure hunt","vve-treasure"],["magic phrases","vve-magic-phrases"],["five magic phrases","vve-magic-phrases"],["priming words","vve-priming"],["social script","vve-break-script"],["logline","vve-logline"],["yuck my yums","vve-yums"],["conversational confetti","vve-confetti"],["mirroring","vve-mirror"],["mirror","vve-mirror"],["graceful exit","vve-exit"],["dreambuilding","vve-dreambuilding"],["pebbling","vve-pebbling"],["pet project","vve-pet-pest"],["pest project","vve-pet-pest"],["verbal catch","vve-verbal-catch"],["connector","vve-connector"],["self-story archetypes","vve-archetypes"],["archetypes","vve-archetypes"],["Social Game Plan","vve-social-game-plan"],["Winger","vve-social-game-plan"],["Riser","vve-social-game-plan"],["Triple Threat","vve-triple-threat"],["Launch Stance","vve-triple-threat"],["conversation sparkers","vve-sparkers"],["sparkers","vve-sparkers"],["hot buttons","vve-sparkers"],["Name Game","vve-name-game"],["Highlighter","vve-highlighter"],["Pygmalion effect","vve-pygmalion"],["Thread Theory","vve-thread-theory"],["five whys","vve-thread-theory"],["microexpressions","vve-decoder"],["Decoder","vve-decoder"],["Big Five","vve-speed-read"],["Speed-Read","vve-speed-read"],["Appreciation Matrix","vve-appreciation"],["appreciation languages","vve-appreciation"],["Primary Value","vve-primary-value"],["Story Stack","vve-story-stack"],["Boomerang","vve-story-stack"],["Own It","vve-own-it"],["Franklin Effect","vve-franklin"],["anti-perfect","vve-franklin"],["NUT Job","vve-nut-job"],["attunement","vve-attunement"],["charisma","vve-charisma"],["Charisma Dial","vve-charisma"],["warmth and competence","vve-charisma"],["Danger Zone","vve-charisma"],["Cue Cycle","vve-cue-cycle"],["charisma cues","vve-charisma-cues"],["anti-blocking","vve-charisma-cues"],["fronting","vve-charisma-cues"],["warmth cues","vve-warmth-cues"],["triple nod","vve-triple-nod"],["slow triple nod","vve-triple-nod"],["head tilt","vve-head-tilt"],["power cues","vve-power-cues"],["steeple","vve-power-cues"],["lower-lid flex","vve-power-cues"],["the Fish","vve-interruptions"],["the Bookmark","vve-interruptions"],["Anchor Touch","vve-interruptions"],["Preview technique","vve-interruptions"],["danger-zone cues","vve-danger-cues"],["resting face","vve-danger-cues"],["uptalk","vve-vocal-power"],["vocal fry","vve-vocal-power"],["power pause","vve-vocal-power"],["permission warmth","vve-permission-warmth"],["vocal hug","vve-permission-warmth"],["vocal invitations","vve-vocal-invitations"],["vocal variety","vve-vocal-variety"],["nonverbal script","vve-vocal-variety"],["verbal chameleon","vve-verbal-chameleon"],["warm words","vve-verbal-chameleon"],["competent words","vve-verbal-chameleon"],["imagery cues","vve-imagery"],["communication mirror","vg-instrument"],["voice baseline","vg-instrument"],["vocal foundations","vg-instrument"],["tonality","vg-tonality"],["feel the weird","vg-tonality"],["home base","vg-rate"],["rate of speech","vg-rate"],["melody","vg-melody"],["siren technique","vg-melody"],["monotone","vg-melody"],["volume zones","vg-volume"],["pause","vg-pause"],["filler words","vg-pause"],["stillness","vg-presence"],["presence","vg-presence"],["doorway reset","vg-posture-doorway"],["Power Sphere","vg-power-sphere"],["Placater","vg-power-sphere"],["Leveler","vg-power-sphere"],["Blamer","vg-power-sphere"],["Thinker","vg-power-sphere"],["Distracter","vg-power-sphere"],["three-zone story","vg-movement"],["triangle technique","vg-eye-contact"],["one thought one person","vg-eye-contact"],["four stages of competence","vg-competence-stages"],["Practical Magic","vg-practical-magic"],["The One Thing","vg-practical-magic"],["generosity of energy","vg-generosity-energy"],["Story Bank","vg-storytelling"],["stakes","vg-stakes"],["Spark–Point–Link","vg-spark-point-link"],["the Spark","vg-spark-point-link"],["the Point","vg-spark-point-link"],["the Link","vg-spark-point-link"],["second arrow","vg-relinking"],["relinking","vg-relinking"],["two arrows","vg-relinking"],["High Low Buffalo","vg-high-low-buffalo"],["fill the shelf","vg-high-low-buffalo"],["two-shelf exercise","vg-high-low-buffalo"],["conversational threads","vg-threads"],["threads","vg-threads"],["listening traps","vg-traps"],["reflection","vg-reflection"],["reflective listening","vg-reflection"],["vocal radar","vg-vocal-radar"],["vocal signals","vg-vocal-radar"],["undercurrent","vg-undercurrent"],["emotional radar","vg-undercurrent"],["Last Word Last Letter","vg-last-word"],["Match Mirror and Lead","vg-match-mirror-lead"],["MML","vg-match-mirror-lead"],["yes and","vg-yes-and"],["3-2-1","vg-321"],["FORD","vg-ford"],["small-talk formula","ch-smalltalk"],["small talk loop","ch-smalltalk"],["LDRAO","ch-smalltalk"],["FORK","ch-fork"],["Four Threads","ch-four-threads"],["Barkeep","ch-barkeep"],["verbal mirror","ch-verbal-mirror"],["verbal reflection","ch-verbal-mirror"],["six needs","all-six-needs"],["Needs Map","all-six-needs"],["significance","all-six-needs"],["approval","all-six-needs"],["acceptance","all-six-needs"],["intelligence","all-six-needs"],["hardship","all-six-needs"],["power","all-six-needs"],["pity","all-six-needs"],["lean","all-six-needs"],["Values Map","ch-values-map"],["decision styles","ch-decision-style"],["resonate before redirect","ch-resonate"],["get into their river","ch-resonate"],["golden bridge","ch-golden-bridge"],["Sympathise Socialise Select Show Secure","ch-objections"],["ACSS","ch-acss"],["Authority Triangle","ch-authority"],["authority traits","ch-authority"],["composure","ch-composure"],["collapse","ch-composure"],["posturing","ch-composure"],["performance mode","ch-performance-mode"],["connection mode","ch-performance-mode"],["swimming pool rule","ch-move-slower"],["baseline","ch-baseline"],["five Cs","ch-baseline"],["embedded commands","lo-embedded"],["covert hypnosis","lo-hypnosis"],["PCP model","lo-pcp"],["Six-Axis Model","lo-models"],["FATE model","lo-models"],["Hierarchy of Influence","lo-models"],["frame control","lo-frame"],["lie detection","lo-lie-spotting"],["Behavioral Table of Elements","lo-lie-spotting"],["deception rating","lo-lie-spotting"],["Six-Minute X-Ray","lo-xray"],["Behavior Compass","lo-xray"],["elicitation","lo-elicitation"],["brain pharmacist","lo-needs-steering"],["identity hacking","lo-identity"],["interrogation","lo-interrogation"]],"topics":[{"i":"t-nervous","c":"confidence","t":"I get nervous meeting people","q":["nervous","i get nervous","nervous meeting people","anxious","anxiety","social anxiety","scared to talk to people","butterflies","nerves","shaky voice","heart racing","scared"],"n":["ch-nervous-excited","ch-composure","ch-acss","ch-move-slower","vg-presence","all-assume-liked","ch-role","ch-performance-mode","all-label-feeling"],"b":"Nerves aren't a sign you're bad at this — they're just energy looking for a job. Give it one. First, name it to yourself (\"nerves, noted\") and then call it excitement; Chase leans on this, and research on reappraising anxiety backs it. Second, slow your body down: Chase's rule is to move no faster than you would in a swimming pool, and Vinh adds that stillness stops your body leaking the jitters. Third, take the spotlight off yourself. Give yourself a job, like \"help them feel interesting\", and go in assuming they already like you a little — Vanessa's point is that they usually do.","say":"\"Hi, I’m [your name] — I don't know anyone here yet. How do you know the host?\"","tip":"Breathe out slowly before you walk up, then say your first sentence a notch louder than feels natural. Volume steadies a shaky voice.","short":"For nerves, name them, slow your body down and put your attention on the other person.","f":["How do I come across more confident?","I'm shy at parties","What do I say when I go blank?"],"src":[{"u":"https://doi.org/10.1037/a0035325","l":"Brooks 2014"},{"u":"https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9280.2007.01916.x","l":"Lieberman et al. 2007"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 164–165"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 154–158, 164"},{"l":"DOAC 2024 https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RvjR9GM2kX8"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 938–943"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 144, 880"}],"w":["ch","vg","vve"]},{"i":"t-shy-party","c":"confidence","t":"I'm shy at parties","q":["shy","shy at parties","introvert","introverted","party","parties","social events","dont know anyone","standing alone","awkward at parties","hate parties"],"n":["vve-social-game-plan","vg-entering-group","vve-context-cues","ch-barkeep","ch-role","vve-can-i-guess"],"b":"Shy is mostly a set of habits, not a personality, so plan the party instead of hoping it goes well. Vanessa's move is to pick your spot: skip the doorway and stand where people arrive from the drinks or food, because that's where conversations start. Bring a friend if you can. Vinh's way in to a group is to hover, catch an eye, wait for a natural gap and add to what they're already saying. Chase gives you a role so you're not just \"being at a party\": be the host-in-disguise who learns names and introduces people. And give yourself permission to leave after one good conversation.","say":"\"Mind if I join you? I heard 'Lisbon' and couldn't walk past.\"","tip":"Smile before you speak and keep your first line short. A small, easy opener makes the next one easier.","short":"At parties, pick a good spot, join groups by adding to their topic, and give yourself a role.","f":["How do I join a group conversation?","How do I start a conversation?","I get nervous meeting people"],"src":[{"l":"Captivate ch. 1"},{"l":"@askvinh \"How to enter conversations\" https://www.youtube.com/@askvinh"},{"l":"On Purpose with Jay Shetty"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 \"Never Run Out of Things to Say\""},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 451–452"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual p. 130"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 \"How to Talk to Introverts\""}],"w":["vve","vg","ch"]},{"i":"t-confidence","c":"confidence","t":"How do I come across more confident?","q":["confident","confidence","more confident","look confident","sound confident","seem confident","self assured","presence","authority","low confidence","insecure"],"n":["vve-triple-threat","vg-posture-doorway","ch-composure","ch-plain-speech","vg-low-status","vve-vocal-power","ch-rules-confidence","ch-acss","vg-pause"],"b":"Don't wait to feel confident — start with what people can see and hear, and the feeling tends to follow. Vinh splits it into look, sound and feel, and says fix the first two. Look: shoulders down and back, hands visible, chin level; his doorway reset makes it automatic. Sound: end statements going down, not up, and swap fillers for pauses — Vanessa and Vinh agree on both. Then Chase's piece: composure sits between collapse (shrinking, over-apologising) and posturing (puffing up). Say your point once, calmly, then stop talking. Confidence is mostly not rushing.","say":"\"Here's what I'd suggest.\" (then pause, and give the one point)","tip":"Drop your pitch on the last word of the sentence and hold the silence after it for a full breath.","short":"Confidence starts with how you look and sound — open posture, statements that end low, and not rushing.","f":["How do I sound more confident?","I get nervous meeting people","New habits feel fake"],"src":[{"l":"Captivate ch. 2"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 9"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 154–158, 164"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 453, 462–463"},{"l":"@askvinh on YouTube"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 14"},{"l":"Cues ch. 7"}],"w":["vve","vg","ch"]},{"i":"t-fake","c":"confidence","t":"New habits feel fake","q":["feels fake","fake","unnatural","inauthentic","not me","awkward practising","feels weird","cringe","trying too hard","forced"],"n":["vg-competence-stages","vg-practice","vg-instrument","vg-tonality"],"b":"Feeling fake is exactly what learning feels like, so treat it as a sign of progress. Vinh's line is that a new habit feels fake only because it's unfamiliar — your \"natural\" voice and manner are just habits you picked up growing up. He uses the four stages of competence: the clunky, thinking-about-it stage is where most people quit, right before it turns automatic. Two things make it easier. Practise on \"neutral ears\" first — a barista, a shop assistant — before people who know your old style. And decide who you're becoming (\"I'm someone who speaks calmly\") so each attempt counts as a vote for it.","say":"\"I'm working on speaking up more — if you see me try, give me a nod.\"","tip":"When it feels strange, say \"unfamiliar, not fake\" in your head and finish the sentence anyway.","short":"Feeling fake just means it's unfamiliar — keep going and practise on neutral ears first.","f":["How do I get better at talking?","How do I come across more confident?","What's a good daily practice?"],"src":[{"l":"@askvinh on YouTube https://www.youtube.com/@askvinh"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 14"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 2"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 3"}],"w":["vg"]},{"i":"t-overthinking","c":"confidence","t":"I overthink conversations afterwards","q":["overthinking","overthink","replay conversations","did they like me","said something stupid","cringe afterwards","awkward afterwards","rumination","they dont like me"],"n":["all-liking-gap","vve-disliked","ch-limiting-beliefs","vg-relinking","vve-signal-liking"],"b":"You're almost certainly being harder on yourself than they are. Research on the \"liking gap\" found people consistently underestimate how much the other person liked them after a chat — Vanessa builds on this a lot. So when you replay a moment, use Vinh's two arrows: the first arrow is what happened (\"I stumbled on a word\"); the second is the meaning you add (\"I'm awkward\"). Stop firing the second one. Chase says spot the belief behind it (\"I'm bad with people\"), question it and rewrite it. And if one person didn't click with you, Vanessa's reminder is that not everyone has to.","say":"\"I really enjoyed that — let's do it again.\"","tip":"Send that message the same day, in a warm, plain voice. It replaces the replay with something kind and true.","short":"People usually liked you more than you think, so drop the second arrow of self-criticism.","f":["How do I be more likeable?","I get nervous meeting people","New habits feel fake"],"src":[{"u":"https://doi.org/10.1177/0956797618783714","l":"Boothby et al. 2018"},{"l":"Read Anyone handbook §4"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 1"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 2"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 166–178"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 22 (Two Arrows, pp. 211–212)"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 \"How to Create a Magnetic Presence\""}],"w":["vve","ch","vg"]},{"i":"t-big-moment","c":"confidence","t":"Nerves before a big moment","q":["presentation nerves","big meeting","speech nerves","before a presentation","stage fright","public speaking nerves","interview nerves","pitch nerves","before a talk"],"n":["ch-nervous-excited","vg-pause","vg-rate","vg-presence","ch-stop-worrying","all-label-feeling"],"b":"Treat the hour before like an athlete would. Burn off the adrenaline first — Vinh says a shaky voice is your body shaking, so a brisk walk or a few press-ups genuinely helps. Then breathe out slowly and name the feeling. Chase suggests calling it excitement and asking two questions: what's actually worrying me, and what can I do about it right now? Then move your focus to the audience: you can only be self-conscious if you're thinking about yourself, so ask \"how can I help them?\" Once you start, pause before your first sentence and speak slower than you think you need to.","say":"\"Before I start, the one thing I want you to leave with is this…\"","tip":"Plant your feet, take one breath, and let the first word come out lower and slower than normal.","short":"Before a big moment, burn off adrenaline, name the nerves and focus on helping the audience.","f":["How do I speak to a group?","I talk too fast","How do I stop saying um?"],"src":[{"u":"https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9280.2007.01916.x","l":"Lieberman et al. 2007"},{"u":"https://doi.org/10.1037/a0035325","l":"Brooks 2014"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 164–165"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 7"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 4"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 8"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 165–166"}],"w":["ch","vg","vve"]},{"i":"t-start","c":"openers","t":"How do I start a conversation?","q":["start a conversation","opener","openers","first thing to say","talk to a stranger","approach someone","break the ice","icebreaker","conversation starters","how to begin","first line","nice to meet you"],"n":["vve-pq-exciting","vve-break-script","vve-priming","vve-permission-warmth","vg-generosity-energy","ch-one-liners","vve-context-cues","vve-sparkers","all-start-conversation"],"b":"Open warm, then ask something that needs a real answer. Vanessa's research says autopilot lines like \"Nice to meet you\" and \"What do you do?\" are forgettable, so swap in a sparker: \"Working on anything exciting these days?\" or \"What was the highlight of your day?\" If you're somewhere specific, use it — \"How do you know the host?\" works almost anywhere. Vinh's piece is energy: give your hello a little more life than feels necessary. Chase's is to keep it open and simple — \"How did you get started in this?\" — and then actually listen to the answer.","say":"\"So good to meet you. Working on anything exciting these days?\"","tip":"Smile first, then speak. Lift your voice slightly on the hello and let the question end with a gentle rise.","short":"Start warm and swap autopilot questions for one that needs a real answer.","f":["What do I say when I go blank?","How do I keep a conversation going?","What are good questions to ask?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 power question 1"},{"l":"Read Anyone handbook §5.1"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 \"The Weirdest, Best Party\""},{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 \"Ten Seconds to a Memorable First Impression\""},{"l":"Cues ch. 8"},{"l":"Audible interview and On Purpose, via the Read Anyone inventory (G15)"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual p. 96"}],"w":["vve","vg","ch"]},{"i":"t-what-say","c":"openers","t":"What do I say? (I go blank)","q":["what do i say","what should i say","i go blank","mind goes blank","dont know what to say","nothing to say","freeze up","run out of things to say","stuck"],"n":["vve-context-cues","ch-fork","vg-ford","vg-tell-me-more","vve-tell-me-more","vve-prepare-topics"],"b":"When your mind goes blank, you don't need something clever — you need something easy. Three fallbacks. Look around: the place, the event, the food — Vanessa's context cues. Pick a FORK topic, Chase's shortcut: Family, Occupation, Recreation, Knowledge (Vinh uses FORD, with Dreams instead). Or go back to what they last said and ask for more; Vinh points out that \"tell me more\" buys you thinking time and makes them feel heard. And before events, arrive with two questions and one short story ready, so you're never starting from zero.","say":"\"Wait, go back — tell me more about that.\"","tip":"Pause for a breath before you speak. A calm pause reads as thoughtful, not stuck.","short":"When you go blank, use the setting, a FORK topic, or ask them to tell you more.","f":["What do I do in an awkward silence?","How do I keep a conversation going?","How do I start a conversation?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 \"Never Run Out of Things to Say\""},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual p. 96"},{"l":"@askvinh on YouTube https://www.youtube.com/@askvinh"},{"l":"DOAC 2025 https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oIiv_335yus"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 magic phrase 2"},{"l":"Huang et al. 2017, as cited in the handbook"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 \"How to Be Interesting\""}],"w":["vve","ch","vg"]},{"i":"t-small-talk","c":"smalltalk","t":"How do I make small talk less boring?","q":["small talk","boring small talk","hate small talk","small talk tips","better small talk","shallow conversation","surface level"],"n":["ch-smalltalk","vve-treasure","vve-stories-not-facts","ch-interesting","vve-been-busy","vve-weather","vve-ask-good","vve-sparkers"],"b":"Small talk gets boring when it's a swap of facts, so turn it into a hunt for what lights them up. Vanessa calls it a treasure hunt: ask for stories, not facts, and dig wherever their voice speeds up. Chase gives you a simple loop to run: listen, let your face show you heard it, react in a few words, ask one small follow-up, then offer a related bit of your own. His aim is that they walk away feeling interesting. Skip \"Been busy?\" and bad-weather moaning — they drag the mood down. Ask for something good instead.","say":"\"What's been the best part of your week so far?\"","tip":"Let your face react before your words do — eyebrows up, a real smile — then ask your follow-up.","short":"Make small talk a treasure hunt — ask for stories and follow what lights them up.","f":["How do I keep a conversation going?","How do I go deeper?","What are good questions to ask?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 91–93, 448–450"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 \"Turn Small Talk into a Treasure Hunt\""},{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 \"Ask for Stories, Not Facts\""},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual p. 91"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 \"Don't (Ever) Ask This\""},{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 \"Should I Ask About the Weather?\""},{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 \"Use This Icebreaker…\""}],"w":["ch","vve"]},{"i":"t-intros","c":"smalltalk","t":"How do I answer \"How are you?\" and \"What do you do?\"","q":["how are you","what do you do","answer what do you do","describe my job","elevator pitch","introduce myself","where are you from","tell me about yourself"],"n":["vve-how-are-you","vve-logline","ch-four-threads","vve-verbal-catch"],"b":"Answer with something they can grab, then hand it back. For \"How are you?\", Vanessa suggests a tiny real story instead of \"Good, you?\" — something from your week — then turn it back with a better question. For \"What do you do?\", have a short, plain one-liner about your work. Chase's Four Threads trick works for any basic question: give the answer plus three or four hooks, so they can pick whichever interests them. Then finish with \"How about you?\" so it doesn't become a monologue.","say":"\"Honestly, good — I finally fixed my bike this weekend. What's been good with you?\"","tip":"Keep it under fifteen seconds, smile on the hook, and end with your voice rising gently on the question back.","short":"Answer basic questions with a few hooks they can grab, then hand the question back.","f":["How do I keep a conversation going?","How do I start a conversation?","I talk too much"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 \"How to Answer 'How Are You?'\""},{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 \"How to Answer 'What Do You Do?'\""},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 96–97, 450"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 2 \"Make Sure You Don't Talk Too Much\""}],"w":["vve","ch"]},{"i":"t-silence","c":"smalltalk","t":"What do I do in an awkward silence?","q":["awkward silence","awkward pause","silence","conversation dies","conversation died","lull","dead air","it went quiet","uncomfortable silence"],"n":["all-awkward-silence","vg-pause","vve-transition","vve-context-cues","ch-fork"],"b":"First, don't panic — a short silence feels far longer to you than to them. Vinh, the self-described \"Pause Guy\", says you don't have to fill a pause at all — a calm one makes you look composed. If it stretches, change lanes openly; Vanessa's line is \"Totally shifting gears for a second…\", which feels smooth rather than abrupt. Or pick up an earlier thread — something they mentioned in passing — or a FORK topic from Chase. Calling back to something they said earlier also shows you were really listening.","say":"\"Going back to something you said earlier — how did the move go?\"","tip":"Let the silence sit for one easy breath, smile, then speak a little slower than before.","short":"A short silence is fine — let it breathe, then pick up an earlier thread or shift gears openly.","f":["What do I say when I go blank?","How do I keep a conversation going?","They seem bored"],"src":[{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 7"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 1"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual p. 96"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 \"Talk About Shark Attacks\""},{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 \"Never Run Out of Things to Say\""}],"w":["vg","vve","ch"]},{"i":"t-keep-going","c":"smalltalk","t":"How do I keep a conversation going?","q":["keep a conversation going","keep it going","conversation flow","follow up","follow-up questions","what to ask next","conversation dies quickly","one word answers"],"n":["all-follow-ups","vg-threads","ch-four-threads","vve-thread-theory","ch-smalltalk","vve-tell-me-more","vg-three-followups"],"b":"The secret is follow-ups, not new topics. Research by Huang and colleagues found people who ask more follow-up questions are liked more, and all three teachers build on this. Listen for threads, as Vinh calls them — a detail, a feeling, a change in their voice — and pull on the one with the most life in it. Vanessa's \"Tell me more?\" and Chase's small-talk loop do the same job. Give threads back too: when it's your turn, answer with a few hooks they can grab. If someone shows strong feeling, Vinh's rule is three follow-ups before you say anything about yourself.","say":"\"Wait, how did that happen?\"","tip":"Ask it with real curiosity and a slight rise, then stay quiet and let them run with it.","short":"Keep it going with follow-up questions on the thread with the most life in it.","f":["What do I do in an awkward silence?","How do I go deeper?","I talk too much"],"src":[{"u":"https://doi.org/10.1037/pspi0000097","l":"Huang et al. 2017"},{"l":"Read Anyone handbook §4"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 23, 26"},{"l":"Vinh on TikTok"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 96–97, 450"},{"l":"Captivate ch. 5"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 91–93, 448–450"}],"w":["vg","ch","vve"]},{"i":"t-deeper","c":"deeper","t":"How do I go deeper and have meaningful conversations?","q":["go deeper","deep conversation","meaningful conversation","real conversation","beyond small talk","connect deeply","deep questions","intimate conversation","get to know someone"],"n":["vve-blueprint","vve-go-deeper","vve-power-questions","vg-high-low-buffalo","ch-go-first","vve-pq-goal","vve-pq-afraid"],"b":"Go one level deeper than feels comfortable, at their pace. Vanessa's Conversation Blueprint moves from easy facts, to goals and values, to the story someone tells about their life — and research shows people enjoy deep talk far more than they expect. Move up a level only when their answers get longer and warmer. Vinh's way in is to go first: share a high, a low and something random about yourself (his High, Low, Buffalo), with a real answer, and they'll usually match your depth. Chase agrees — openness invites openness.","say":"\"What's something you're working towards right now that you actually care about?\"","tip":"Slow down and soften your voice on the question, then give them a beat of silence to think.","short":"To go deeper, move one level at a time — from facts to goals to their story — and go first.","f":["What are good questions to ask?","How do I make friends?","How do I make someone feel heard?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, \"The Conversation Blueprint\""},{"u":"https://www.scienceofpeople.com/conversation/","l":"Science of People: Conversation"},{"l":"Conversation research guide https://www.scienceofpeople.com/conversation-guide/"},{"l":"Kardas, Kumar & Epley 2022, as cited in the handbook"},{"l":"Conversation, Levels 1–3"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 23"},{"l":"On Purpose with Jay Shetty https://podcasts.happyscribe.com/on-purpose-with-jay-shetty/vinh-giang-how-to-communicate-so-people-actually-listen-to-you"}],"w":["vve","vg","ch"]},{"i":"t-nothing-common","c":"smalltalk","t":"We have nothing in common","q":["nothing in common","no common ground","different from me","dont relate","cant relate","different interests","old people","young people"],"n":["vve-teach-me","vve-thread-theory","vve-same-here","ch-teach-me","vve-yums"],"b":"Nothing in common is actually a gift — it means they can teach you something. Vanessa's move when there's no \"me too\" is to ask them to teach you, and Chase agrees as long as you genuinely want to learn. People love explaining what they know. Meanwhile keep listening for small overlaps — a place, a worry, a love of bad coffee — and when you find one, say \"Same here!\" Similarity is one of the oldest findings in liking research. And if they're into something you'd never choose, meet it with curiosity, not a wince.","say":"\"I know nothing about that — teach me? What got you into it?\"","tip":"Lean in a little and keep your eyebrows up while they explain. Your face shows you mean it.","short":"When you've nothing in common, ask them to teach you — and keep listening for small overlaps.","f":["How do I keep a conversation going?","What are good questions to ask?","How do I be more likeable?"],"src":[{"l":"Captivate ch. 5"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 magic phrase 4"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual p. 356"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 \"Don't Yuck My Yums\""}],"w":["vve","ch"]},{"i":"t-questions","c":"openers","t":"What are good questions to ask?","q":["good questions","questions to ask","best questions","interesting questions","question list","power questions","what to ask"],"n":["vve-power-questions","vve-signature","vve-pq-learn","ch-one-liners","vg-ford","vve-lucky"],"b":"The best questions invite a story and are easy to answer. Vanessa's nine power questions work in order. Light: \"Working on anything exciting these days?\", \"Any fun plans coming up?\" Deeper: \"What's your biggest goal right now?\", \"What have you always wanted to learn?\" Meaningful (only with trust): \"What would you do if you weren't afraid?\" Chase likes open, opinion-style questions such as \"What's your take on all this?\" Vinh's favourite easy one is \"What do you do in your free time?\" Pick one you'd genuinely love to hear answered and make it your signature question.","say":"\"What do you love to talk about?\"","tip":"Ask one question, then stop. Don't stack a second question on top while they're thinking.","short":"Good questions invite a story — start light, go deeper as trust grows, and ask one at a time.","f":["How do I go deeper?","How do I start a conversation?","How do I keep a conversation going?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Levels 1–3"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 2 \"Be Known as an Incredible & Unique Conversationalist\""},{"l":"Conversation, Level 2 \"How to Sound Smart\""},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual p. 96"},{"l":"@askvinh on YouTube https://www.youtube.com/@askvinh"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 3 \"The One Question…\""}],"w":["vve","ch","vg"]},{"i":"t-interesting","c":"story","t":"How do I be more interesting?","q":["be interesting","more interesting","boring person","im boring","i have nothing interesting","interesting to talk to"],"n":["ch-interesting","vve-prepare-topics","vve-story-stack","vg-storytelling","vve-confetti","vve-at-ease"],"b":"The quickest way to be interesting is to be interested — Chase's aim is that the other person leaves feeling interesting. But it helps to have a few things ready to share too. Vinh suggests a \"Story Bank\" on your phone: whenever something moves you — frustration, surprise, pride — jot it down. Vanessa says arrive prepared with a couple of fresh stories and questions, and tease a good one (\"Can I tell you something weird that happened?\"). Drop the urge to impress; being relaxed and curious is more magnetic than being clever.","say":"\"Can I tell you the strangest thing that happened to me this week?\"","tip":"Lean in slightly and drop your volume a touch on the tease — it pulls people closer.","short":"Be interested first, and keep a small bank of real stories ready to share.","f":["How do I tell a good story?","How do I make small talk less boring?","How do I be more likeable?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual p. 91"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 \"How to Be Interesting\""},{"l":"Captivate ch. 10"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 15"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 \"A Trick from Master Conversationalists\""},{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 \"Stop Trying to Impress People\""}],"w":["ch","vve","vg"]},{"i":"t-listening","c":"listening","t":"How do I become a better listener?","q":["better listener","listening","listen better","how to listen","active listening","bad listener","not listening","zoning out while listening"],"n":["vg-listening","vg-traps","vg-last-word","vg-reflection","ch-smalltalk","vve-triple-nod","vve-vocal-invitations"],"b":"Good listening is mostly catching your own habits. Vinh lists five traps — judging, advising, one-upping, mind reading and daydreaming — and says spend a week just noticing which one is yours. To stop planning your reply while they talk, try his theatre game Last Word, Last Letter. Then show you're listening: Vanessa's slow nods and little \"mm-hm… and then?\" sounds invite more, and Chase says let your face react to the good bits. Finish with reflection: give back a key word they used, and let them go further.","say":"\"Hang on — 'stuck'? Tell me more about that.\"","tip":"Repeat their word softly with a slight rise, then nod slowly and say nothing until they carry on.","short":"Better listening means catching your trap, showing you're with them, and reflecting their words back.","f":["How do I make someone feel heard?","I talk too much","How do I show I'm listening with my body?"],"src":[{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 24"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 25"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 29 (p. 274)"},{"l":"On Purpose with Jay Shetty"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 26"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 91–93, 448–450"},{"l":"Cues ch. 4"}],"w":["vg","ch","vve"]},{"i":"t-heard","c":"listening","t":"How do I make someone feel heard?","q":["feel heard","make them feel heard","feel understood","validate","validation","empathy","empathise","show i understand","be supportive"],"n":["ch-heard-seen","vg-reflection","vg-undercurrent","all-label-feeling","ch-verbal-mirror","ch-reaction"],"b":"People feel heard when you show them what you heard, not when you say \"I understand\". Chase calls making people feel heard and seen the most important people skill there is. Vinh's method: notice a strong word, reflect it back, pause, then gently name the feeling as a question. Labelling a feeling, research suggests, can actually take the edge off it. Chase's verbal mirror — repeating their last few words — does the same thing. Hold off on advice; fixing too early tells them you've stopped listening.","say":"\"That sounds exhausting. Did it feel like nobody noticed?\"","tip":"Lower your volume, slow down, and let your face show a little of the feeling — then wait.","short":"Make people feel heard by reflecting their words and gently naming the feeling, before any advice.","f":["Someone is upset — what do I say?","How do I become a better listener?","How do I support someone going through something hard?"],"src":[{"l":"DOAC 2026 https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9uSXOr-AdAU"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 26"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 29"},{"l":"Read Anyone handbook §4"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 354–356"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 348–349"},{"l":"DOAC 2024 https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RvjR9GM2kX8"}],"w":["ch","vg","vve"]},{"i":"t-talk-too-much","c":"listening","t":"I talk too much","q":["talk too much","i talk too much","monologue","ramble on","dominate conversations","over share","oversharing","dont let others talk","one upping","make it about me"],"n":["vve-verbal-catch","vg-traps","vg-three-followups","vg-boring","all-follow-ups"],"b":"Treat conversation like a game of catch. Vanessa's fix is simple: after your bit, toss it back with \"How about you?\" A good rule of thumb is to check in once you've talked for a minute or so. Vinh adds that the hidden culprit is often one-upping — hearing their story and reaching for a bigger one. When someone shares something with feeling, ask three follow-ups before you say anything about yourself. And when you do tell a story, cut the detours; know your point before you start.","say":"\"Anyway — that's enough about me. What about you?\"","tip":"Say it with a smile and a slight drop in pace, then genuinely turn your body towards them.","short":"Hand the ball back after your turn, and ask follow-ups before sharing your own story.","f":["How do I keep a conversation going?","How do I become a better listener?","How do I tell a good story?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 2 \"Make Sure You Don't Talk Too Much\""},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 25"},{"l":"On Purpose with Jay Shetty https://podcasts.happyscribe.com/on-purpose-with-jay-shetty/vinh-giang-how-to-communicate-so-people-actually-listen-to-you"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 19–20"},{"l":"Read Anyone handbook §4"},{"l":"Huang et al. 2017 https://www.hbs.edu/faculty/Pages/item.aspx?num=52115"}],"w":["vve","vg"]},{"i":"t-body-listen","c":"body","t":"How do I show I'm listening with my body?","q":["triple nod","nodding","nod","head tilt","tilt","show interest body language","look interested","listening face","eyebrow raise","lean in"],"n":["vve-triple-nod","vve-head-tilt","vve-warmth-cues","ch-display","vve-charisma-cues","vg-expression","vve-mirror"],"b":"Your face and body can say \"keep going\" without a word. Vanessa's slow triple nod — three slow nods while they talk — encourages people to say more; fast nods look like \"hurry up\". Tilt your head slightly when they reach the important part, and turn your whole body towards them with nothing blocking you. Chase says let your face show their story as it happens: brows up at the surprise, a wince at the hard bit. Vinh warns that a blank face reads as \"doesn't care\", even when you do.","say":"\"Mm-hm… and then what happened?\"","tip":"Nod slowly — about one nod a second — and keep your face moving with their story.","short":"Show you're listening with slow nods, a head tilt, facing them, and a face that reacts.","f":["How do I become a better listener?","What should my body be doing?","What does mirroring mean?"],"src":[{"l":"Cues ch. 4"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 1"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 92, 129"},{"l":"Cues ch. 3"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 11"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 \"Be a Mirror\""},{"l":"Chartrand & Bargh 1999, as cited in the handbook"}],"w":["vve","ch","vg"]},{"i":"t-read-people","c":"body","t":"How do I read people and body language?","q":["read people","read body language","body language meaning","what does it mean when","crossed arms","signs","cues","baseline","read anyone","read someone"],"n":["ch-baseline","vg-visual-radar","vg-vocal-radar","vve-decoder","vg-what-they-want"],"b":"Read changes, not single gestures — and then ask rather than conclude. Chase's approach is to notice someone's normal (their baseline), then watch for shifts and clusters in context; a change usually means stress or discomfort, not a hidden truth. Vinh calls it radar: notice when their voice speeds up, slows down or goes cold, or their body changes, and ask about it gently. Vanessa's microexpressions help you spot a flash of feeling so you can respond kindly. And Vinh's simplest read: is this person after information, connection, harmony or fun?","say":"\"You went a bit quiet there — is this an OK thing to talk about?\"","tip":"Ask it softly, at a slower pace, with an open palm. It's an invitation, not a test.","short":"Read changes from someone's normal, then ask gently instead of drawing conclusions.","f":["How can I tell if someone is lying?","They seem bored","How do I adapt to different people?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 216–217, 302"},{"l":"Read Anyone handbook §5.3"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 28"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 27 (Thread Pulling, pp. 260–262)"},{"l":"Captivate ch. 6"},{"l":"Vinh's blog https://vinhgiang.com/blog/how-to-build-rapport-with-absolutely-anyone"},{"l":"DOAC 2025 https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oIiv_335yus"}],"w":["ch","vg","vve"]},{"i":"t-lying","c":"leftout","t":"How can I tell if someone is lying?","q":["lying","lie","liar","tell if someone is lying","detect lies","spot a liar","deception","are they lying","honest or not","microexpressions lie detection"],"n":["lo-lie-spotting","ch-baseline","vg-visual-radar"],"b":"Honestly, you can't reliably — and neither can anyone else from body language alone. Large reviews of the research put people at about 54% accuracy, close to a coin flip. Chase and Vanessa both teach lie-spotting from the body, but Read Anyone leaves it out for that reason. What does work: notice a change from their normal, remember it usually means stress or discomfort, and ask an open, kind question. If trust really matters, look at facts and patterns over time, not a twitch or a glance.","say":"\"Something feels off to me — can we talk it through?\"","tip":"Keep your voice level and curious rather than accusing. Palms open, slower pace.","short":"You can't reliably spot lies from body language, so notice changes and ask openly instead.","f":["How do I read people and body language?","How do I make someone feel heard?","What does Read Anyone leave out?"],"src":[{"u":"https://doi.org/10.1207/s15327957pspr1003_2","l":"Bond & DePaulo 2006"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 205–278 (excluded, H48)"},{"l":"Captivate ch. 6 (excluded, V91)"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 216–217, 302"},{"l":"Read Anyone handbook §5.3"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 28"}],"w":["ch","vve","vg"]},{"i":"t-bored","c":"listening","t":"They seem bored","q":["bored","they seem bored","seem bored","disengaged","not interested","losing them","looking at phone","looking around","zoning out","short answers","uninterested"],"n":["vg-vocal-radar","vg-visual-radar","vg-boring","vve-verbal-catch","vve-transition","vg-what-they-want","vve-exit"],"b":"First, check it's not you holding the floor too long — that's the most common cause, and the easiest fix. Hand it back with a question about them. If they're still flat, Vinh's advice is to read what they want from the chat: some people want quick information, some connection, some just fun, so shift gears. Vanessa's open topic switch (\"Totally shifting gears…\") gives you a graceful reset. And if the energy really isn't there, end warmly rather than dragging it out; a good ending beats a long fade.","say":"\"I've been rambling — what's been keeping you busy lately?\"","tip":"Say it lightly with a smile and a slightly faster pace, so it feels playful, not apologetic.","short":"If they seem bored, hand the floor back, shift gears, or end warmly.","f":["How do I end a conversation?","I talk too much","What do I do in an awkward silence?"],"src":[{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 27 (Thread Pulling, pp. 260–262)"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 28"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 19–20"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 2 \"Make Sure You Don't Talk Too Much\""},{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 \"Talk About Shark Attacks\""},{"l":"Vinh's blog https://vinhgiang.com/blog/how-to-build-rapport-with-absolutely-anyone"},{"l":"DOAC 2025 https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oIiv_335yus"}],"w":["vg","vve"]},{"i":"t-upset","c":"tough","t":"Someone is upset — what do I say?","q":["upset","someone is upset","crying","sad friend","emotional","they are stressed","comfort someone","what to say to someone upset","console"],"n":["vg-undercurrent","vve-calm","all-label-feeling","vg-traps","vg-three-followups","vg-calm-pace"],"b":"Lead with presence, not solutions. Vinh says ask yourself \"what might they be feeling or needing?\", then name it gently as a question. Vanessa's research-backed point is to acknowledge the feeling rather than smooth it over — never \"calm down\" or \"it's not that bad\". Ask before fixing: do they want ideas, or just someone to listen? And watch Vinh's listening traps — advice, one-upping, or \"that happened to me too\" can all wait. Three follow-up questions before anything about you is a good guide.","say":"\"That sounds really hard. Do you want ideas, or do you just want me to listen?\"","tip":"Drop your volume, slow your pace, and sit with the silence after you ask. Don't rush to fill it.","short":"When someone's upset, name the feeling gently and ask whether they want ideas or just an ear.","f":["How do I support someone going through something hard?","How do I calm down someone who's angry?","How do I make someone feel heard?"],"src":[{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 29"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 2 \"How to Calm Someone Down\""},{"l":"Lieberman et al. 2007, as cited in the handbook"},{"l":"Read Anyone handbook §4"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 25"},{"l":"On Purpose with Jay Shetty https://podcasts.happyscribe.com/on-purpose-with-jay-shetty/vinh-giang-how-to-communicate-so-people-actually-listen-to-you"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 4"}],"w":["vg","vve"]},{"i":"t-angry","c":"tough","t":"How do I calm down someone who's angry?","q":["angry","calm someone down","furious","shouting","yelling","heated","de escalate","deescalate","temper","mad at me","customer is angry"],"n":["vve-calm","vg-calm-pace","vg-match-mirror-lead","ch-composure","ch-golden-bridge","all-label-feeling"],"b":"Match first, then slow down — and never say \"calm down\". Vinh's technique: meet their pace and intensity for a moment so they feel met, then gradually lower your speed and volume; people tend to follow you down. Vanessa says name the feeling and invite more (\"Can you tell me more about that?\"), because suppressing anger tends to backfire. Chase adds composure: don't shrink and don't puff up — stay steady. When it's time to resolve things, give them a golden bridge, a way to step back without losing face.","say":"\"I can hear how frustrating this is. Tell me what happened.\"","tip":"Start at their pace, then bring your voice down a notch with every sentence. Keep your hands open and still.","short":"Calm anger by matching, then slowing down, naming the feeling and staying steady.","f":["Someone is being rude to me","How do I disagree without a fight?","Someone is upset — what do I say?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 2 \"How to Calm Someone Down\""},{"l":"Lieberman et al. 2007, as cited in the handbook"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 4"},{"l":"Vinh's blog https://vinhgiang.com/blog/how-to-build-rapport-with-absolutely-anyone"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 154–158, 164"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual p. 51"},{"l":"Read Anyone handbook §4"}],"w":["vve","vg","ch"]},{"i":"t-support","c":"tough","t":"How do I support someone going through something hard?","q":["support someone","grief","grieving","hard time","struggling friend","bad news","diagnosis","lost their job","break up","hardship","going through a lot"],"n":["all-six-needs","vve-fears","vve-believe","vg-undercurrent","vg-traps"],"b":"Acknowledge the weight before anything else. Chase's \"hardship\" need is a listening prompt here: people going through something often quietly wonder whether anyone sees how hard it's been. So say that you see it, then pause. Vanessa says make it safe to share fears without being judged, and later, if they're facing a scary step, four words help: \"I believe in you.\" Vinh's listening traps matter most now — skip the advice, the comparisons and the silver linings unless they ask. Practical help, offered specifically, beats \"let me know if you need anything\".","say":"\"That's a lot to carry. I'm here — can I bring dinner round on Thursday?\"","tip":"Speak slowly and softly, keep the same warm tone through the hard part, and let silence be okay.","short":"Support someone by acknowledging how hard it is, listening without fixing, and offering specific help.","f":["Someone is upset — what do I say?","How do I make someone feel heard?","How do I keep in touch with people?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 366–372"},{"u":"https://www.youtube.com/shorts/YzJI8GTHteQ","l":"Chase's needs Short"},{"l":"Read Anyone handbook §6"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 3 \"Hidden Fears and Secret Desires\""},{"l":"Conversation, Level 3 \"Four Words That Fuel Courage\""},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 29"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 25"}],"w":["ch","vve","vg"]},{"i":"t-likeable","c":"likeability","t":"How do I be more likeable?","q":["likeable","likable","more likeable","people like me","be liked","liked more","why dont people like me","make people like me","win people over","attractive personality"],"n":["all-warmth-first","all-assume-liked","all-liking-gap","vve-signal-liking","ch-interesting","all-follow-ups","vve-highlighter"],"b":"Be warm first, be curious, and let people know you like them. Vanessa's research says people judge warmth before competence, and the \"liking gap\" means they already like you more than you think — so walk in assuming you're liked and you'll relax. Signal it back: a real smile, using their name, and saying what you enjoyed. Ask follow-up questions; studies show people who ask more of them are liked more. Chase's aim is to leave people feeling interesting. Vinh's angle is generosity: give a bit more energy than feels necessary.","say":"\"I really enjoyed hearing about that — you explain it so well.\"","tip":"Mean it, smile with your eyes, and say it slightly slower than your normal pace so it lands.","short":"Likeability comes from warmth first, real curiosity, and letting people know you like them.","f":["How do I make a good first impression?","How do I give a good compliment?","How do I keep a conversation going?"],"src":[{"u":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.tics.2006.11.005","l":"Fiske, Cuddy & Glick 2007"},{"u":"https://doi.org/10.1177/0956797618783714","l":"Boothby et al. 2018"},{"u":"https://doi.org/10.1037/pspi0000097","l":"Huang et al. 2017"},{"l":"Read Anyone handbook §4"},{"l":"Read Anyone handbook §5.1"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 1"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 \"How to Create a Magnetic Presence\""}],"w":["vve","ch"]},{"i":"t-charisma","c":"likeability","t":"How do I become more charismatic?","q":["charisma","charismatic","magnetic","presence","aura","warmth and competence","warm and competent","how to be magnetic","stand out"],"n":["vve-charisma","vve-cue-cycle","vg-presence","vve-charisma-cues","vve-power-cues","all-voice-competence","ch-display"],"b":"Charisma is warmth plus competence, shown at the same time. Vanessa's research is that the most charismatic people balance the two — they seem both trustworthy and capable. Warmth cues: a real smile, a head tilt, nodding, open palms. Competence cues: steady eye contact, standing tall, a calm voice that drops at the end of statements. Vinh adds presence, which for him means stillness — no swaying or fiddling — plus a face that shows what you feel. Chase's point is that calm, unhurried movement reads as confidence on its own.","say":"\"Tell me more — I want to understand how you did that.\"","tip":"Steady eye contact, a warm smile, and land your statements with a downward tone.","short":"Charisma is warmth and competence together — friendly cues plus calm, capable ones.","f":["How do I sound more confident?","What should my body be doing?","How do I be more likeable?"],"src":[{"u":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.tics.2006.11.005","l":"Fiske, Cuddy & Glick 2007"},{"l":"Cues ch. 1"},{"l":"Cues ch. 2"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 8"},{"l":"Cues ch. 3"},{"l":"Cues ch. 5"},{"l":"Read Anyone handbook §4"}],"w":["vve","vg","ch"]},{"i":"t-first-impression","c":"likeability","t":"How do I make a good first impression?","q":["first impression","first impressions","good impression","walking in","enter a room","meet new people","impress","when i arrive"],"n":["vve-triple-threat","vg-posture-doorway","vve-priming","vve-permission-warmth","all-warmth-first","all-assume-liked"],"b":"First impressions happen in seconds, mostly through your body and voice. Vinh's habit is a doorway reset: every time you walk through a door, drop your shoulders, lift your chest and ground yourself. Vanessa's Triple Threat covers the first moments: hands visible, her Launch Stance (shoulders down and back, chin up) and good eye contact. Then open with warmth in your voice — \"So good to meet you!\" rather than a flat \"Nice to meet you\" — because people judge warmth before competence. Walk in assuming they'll like you; they usually do.","say":"\"Hi — so good to meet you. How do you know everyone here?\"","tip":"Breathe out at the door, keep your hands where they can be seen, and smile before your first word.","short":"Make a good first impression with a doorway reset, visible hands, eye contact and a warm hello.","f":["How do I be more likeable?","What should my body be doing?","How do I remember names?"],"src":[{"l":"Captivate ch. 2"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 9"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 \"Ten Seconds to a Memorable First Impression\""},{"l":"Cues ch. 8"},{"l":"Read Anyone handbook §4"},{"l":"Fiske, Cuddy & Glick 2007 https://doi.org/10.1016/j.tics.2006.11.005"},{"l":"Read Anyone handbook §5.1"}],"w":["vve","vg"]},{"i":"t-names","c":"likeability","t":"How do I remember names?","q":["remember names","forget names","forgot their name","names","name game","bad with names","name","use their name"],"n":["vve-name-game","ch-use-names","ch-names-stories"],"b":"Make the name stick in the first ten seconds, and decide that it matters. Vanessa's Name Game has three steps: really hear it, repeat it straight back (\"Priya, lovely to meet you\"), then anchor it — picture them next to someone you already know with the same name. Chase's view is that remembering names is about motivation more than memory, so care about it on purpose and use the name naturally a couple of times — not every sentence. And if you've forgotten, admit it early and warmly; it's far less awkward than dodging it for an hour.","say":"\"I'm so sorry — tell me your name one more time? I want to get it right.\"","tip":"Say their name back with a smile and full eye contact the moment you hear it.","short":"Hear the name, say it straight back, and anchor it to someone you know with the same name.","f":["How do I make a good first impression?","How do I be more likeable?","How do I introduce people?"],"src":[{"l":"Captivate ch. 3 (pp. 68–69)"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 887–889"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 92, 448"}],"w":["vve","ch"]},{"i":"t-compliment","c":"likeability","t":"How do I give a good compliment?","q":["compliment","compliments","praise","flattery","appreciation","appreciate someone","say something nice","acknowledge someone"],"n":["vve-compliments","ch-compliments","vve-so-x","vve-highlighter","vve-appreciation"],"b":"Compliment something they chose or worked on, make it specific, and make sure it's true. Vanessa's rule: if you think something nice, say it — but skip the obvious and praise effort, taste or skill rather than looks. Her \"You're so ___!\" is the quick version: \"You're so organised!\" Chase's line is compliment freely but never suck up; a compliment must be true and never tied to a request. Then let them enjoy it, or follow with a question so they can talk about the thing you admired.","say":"\"The way you handled that question was brilliant — how did you stay so calm?\"","tip":"Say it plainly with eye contact, then stop. Don't water it down with a joke.","short":"Compliment something specific they chose or worked on — true, and never tied to a request.","f":["How do I thank someone properly?","How do I be more likeable?","How do I start a conversation?"],"src":[{"l":"Vanessa on X, Sep 2026 https://x.com/vvanedwards/status/2096268031156478239"},{"l":"Read Anyone handbook §5.1"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual p. 131"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 magic phrase 5"},{"l":"Captivate ch. 4"},{"l":"Captivate ch. 8"}],"w":["vve","ch"]},{"i":"t-humour","c":"likeability","t":"How do I be funnier?","q":["funny","funnier","humour","humor","jokes","be witty","make people laugh","banter","playful","sense of humour"],"n":["vg-humour","vve-humour","vg-yes-and","vve-anti-perfect"],"b":"You don't need jokes — you need honest little moments, told with timing. Vinh says the easiest humour comes from small, true, self-aware stories, with a pause before the punchline. Vanessa agrees: the gentlest way to be funny is to be your own butt, and shared laughs become glue when you call back an inside joke later. Borrow Vinh's improv habit too — \"Yes, and…\" builds on what someone says, where \"yes, but…\" kills the fun. Keep it warm; teasing only works when everyone's in on it.","say":"\"Okay, so I confidently walked into the wrong meeting today — and stayed ten minutes.\"","tip":"Pause for a beat just before the funny bit, and let your face give away that you're enjoying it.","short":"Humour comes from small, true, self-aware moments, good timing and building on what others say.","f":["How do I be more interesting?","How do I tell a good story?","How do I be more likeable?"],"src":[{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 18"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 2 \"The Easiest Way to Be Funny\", \"Be Your Own Butt\""},{"l":"Vinh on Instagram, Jun 2025, via the Read Anyone handbook"},{"l":"Captivate ch. 12"},{"l":"Read Anyone handbook §5.1"}],"w":["vg","vve"]},{"i":"t-mirroring","c":"likeability","t":"What does mirroring mean, and does it work?","q":["mirroring","mirror","matching","rapport","build rapport","mimic","copy body language","chameleon","connect quickly"],"n":["all-rapport","vve-mirror","vg-match-mirror-lead","ch-verbal-mirror","ch-resonate"],"b":"Mirroring means gently matching someone's energy, pace or words, and it does help — when it's subtle. Research on the \"chameleon effect\" found light mimicry raises liking, while obvious copying backfires. Vanessa's rule is to mirror the positive (a smile, an open posture), never the negative. Vinh's version is Match, Mirror, Lead: get in sync with their pace and volume first — quiet and slow if they are — then lead gently, for example from tense to calm. Chase's verbal mirror is the simplest: repeat their last few words, and they'll tell you more.","say":"\"Crazy busy? What's been the craziest part?\"","tip":"Match their speed and volume for the first minute, then slow down slightly and see if they follow.","short":"Mirroring means lightly matching someone's pace, energy and words — subtle, and only the positive.","f":["How do I calm down someone who's angry?","How do I read people and body language?","How do I be more likeable?"],"src":[{"u":"https://doi.org/10.1037/0022-3514.76.6.893","l":"Chartrand & Bargh 1999"},{"u":"https://vinhgiang.com/blog/how-to-build-rapport-with-absolutely-anyone","l":"Vinh's blog"},{"l":"Captivate ch. 5"},{"l":"DOAC 2026"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 \"Be a Mirror\""},{"l":"Cues ch. 4"},{"l":"Vinh's blog https://vinhgiang.com/blog/how-to-build-rapport-with-absolutely-anyone"}],"w":["vve","vg","ch"]},{"i":"t-body","c":"body","t":"What should my body be doing?","q":["body language","posture","stand","how to stand","what to do with my body","open body language","closed body language","power pose","confident body language"],"n":["all-body-language","vg-posture-doorway","vg-presence","vg-power-sphere","vve-triple-threat","vve-charisma-cues","ch-move-slower","all-myth-power-pose"],"b":"Keep it open, upright and unhurried. Vinh's three habits: reset your posture at every doorway, stay still rather than swaying or fiddling (stillness reads as presence), and keep gestures in his Power Sphere, roughly between your navel and your eyes. Vanessa says face people with your toes, torso and head, keep your hands visible, and remove barriers like crossed arms or a bag held in front. Chase's rule: never move faster than you would in a swimming pool. One myth to drop: power poses don't reliably change your hormones, though open posture may still help you feel steadier.","say":"\"Hey — great to see you.\"","tip":"Shoulders down, chin level, hands visible, and one slow breath before you speak.","short":"Keep your body open, still and unhurried, with your hands visible and facing them.","f":["What do I do with my hands?","How do I show I'm listening with my body?","How do I sound more confident?"],"src":[{"u":"https://doi.org/10.1177/0956797614553946","l":"Ranehill et al. 2015"},{"l":"Captivate ch. 2"},{"l":"Cues ch. 3–5"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 8–13"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual p. 144"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 9"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 8"}],"w":["vg","vve","ch"]},{"i":"t-hands","c":"body","t":"What do I do with my hands?","q":["hands","what to do with my hands","gestures","gesturing","fidgeting","hands in pockets","arms","talking with hands"],"n":["vg-power-sphere","vve-triple-threat","vve-power-cues","vve-danger-cues","vg-movement"],"b":"Keep them visible and let them help you explain. Vinh's Power Sphere is the zone between your navel and your eyes, elbows off your sides — gestures there look purposeful. Palms up invite people in; palms down sound firm and certain. Vanessa says visible hands build trust, explanatory gestures help people follow your point, and fidgeting or self-touching reads as nerves. When you're not gesturing, let your hands rest loosely. Vinh's simplest rule: move to mark a point, then go still.","say":"\"There are really three things here — one… two… three.\"","tip":"Count the points on your fingers at chest height, then let your hands settle while you pause.","short":"Keep your hands visible, gesture between navel and eyes, and go still between points.","f":["What should my body be doing?","How do I speak in front of a group?","How do I sound more confident?"],"src":[{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 10"},{"l":"Captivate ch. 2"},{"l":"Cues ch. 5"},{"l":"Cues ch. 6"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 12"}],"w":["vg","vve"]},{"i":"t-eye-contact","c":"body","t":"How much eye contact should I make?","q":["eye contact","look people in the eye","staring","avoid eye contact","cant look at people","eyes","where to look"],"n":["vg-eye-contact","vve-triple-threat","vve-charisma-cues"],"b":"Aim for steady but relaxed — enough that they feel seen, not stared at. If it feels intense, Vinh's triangle helps: look at their left eye, then the right, then the bridge of the nose, and repeat. In a group, finish a whole thought with one person before moving to the next, and avoid a robotic left-to-right sweep. Vanessa lists good eye contact as one of the three big first-impression cues, and says purposeful eye contact shows you're interested. Glancing away to think is fine — it's darting eyes that look nervous.","say":"\"I'm really glad you said that.\"","tip":"Hold their gaze to the end of your sentence, then look away slowly, not suddenly.","short":"Hold eye contact for a full thought, use the triangle if it feels intense, and look away slowly.","f":["What should my body be doing?","How do I speak in front of a group?","How do I make a good first impression?"],"src":[{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 13 (p. 122)"},{"l":"Captivate ch. 2"},{"l":"Cues ch. 3"}],"w":["vg","vve"]},{"i":"t-sound-confident","c":"voice","t":"How do I sound more confident?","q":["sound confident","confident voice","voice","sound more confident","authority","sound authoritative","weak voice","upspeak","uptalk","question voice","sound smart","sound smarter","sound smart","sound intelligent","sound professional"],"n":["all-voice-competence","vve-vocal-power","vve-confident-language","ch-plain-speech","ch-tonality","vg-melody","vg-pause"],"b":"Slow down, let your pitch drop at the end of statements, and stop shrinking your words. Vanessa and Vinh both say end statements going down — rising at the end makes them sound like questions. Vanessa adds: speak at your natural low pitch and use short, simple words. Chase's fix is to cut hedges like \"just\", \"maybe\" and \"I'll try\", then say it once and stop. He also says choose which word to stress and pause around it. Research backs the point: people who hear a pitch judge the speaker more competent than people who read it.","say":"\"Here's what I'd recommend.\"","tip":"Breathe low, keep an unhurried pace, and let your pitch fall on the last word.","short":"Sound confident by slowing down, dropping your pitch at the end, and cutting hedge words.","f":["How do I stop saying um?","I talk too fast or ramble","My voice is monotone"],"src":[{"u":"https://doi.org/10.1177/0956797615572906","l":"Schroeder & Epley 2015"},{"l":"Read Anyone handbook §4"},{"l":"Cues ch. 7"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 2 \"Speak with Confidence\""},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 453, 462–463"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 498–499"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 5"}],"w":["vg","vve","ch"]},{"i":"t-um","c":"voice","t":"How do I stop saying \"um\"?","q":["um","uh","filler words","fillers","like","you know","stop saying um","ums and ahs","stammering"],"n":["vg-pause","vve-vocal-power","vg-practice","vg-instrument"],"b":"Don't fight the \"um\" — replace it with a pause. Vinh, who calls himself the Pause Guy, says when you feel a filler coming, stop, breathe, find your next thought and carry on. A pause sounds composed and gives people time to take in what you said; Vanessa calls it a power pause. To find your pattern, record yourself telling a two-minute story and listen back. Then practise in tiny daily reps, because you can't just delete a habit — you have to replace it.","say":"\"That's a good question. [pause] Here's how I'd look at it.\"","tip":"When you feel an \"um\" coming, close your mouth, breathe in through your nose, then carry on.","short":"Swap fillers for a short pause — it sounds composed and gives you time to think.","f":["How do I sound more confident?","I talk too fast or ramble","How do I get better at talking to people?"],"src":[{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 7"},{"l":"Cues ch. 7"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 14"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 2"}],"w":["vg","vve"]},{"i":"t-too-fast","c":"voice","t":"I talk too fast or ramble","q":["talk too fast","speak too fast","rush","rambling","ramble","i ramble","nervous talking","slow down","get to the point"],"n":["vg-rate","vg-pause","vg-spark-point-link","ch-move-slower","vg-calm-pace"],"b":"Slow down in the places that matter, and know your point before you start. Vinh's rule is to vary your speed: quicker for excitement, slower for the important bit — then pause after it. Chase says slower speech and movement come across as calm and in control. For rambling, Vinh's Spark, Point, Link keeps you on track: a spark that grabs attention, the single point you want to make, then a link back to them. If you notice you've drifted, it's fine to say so and land it.","say":"\"Let me get to the point — the main thing is this.\"","tip":"Breathe at every full stop, and slow right down on the one sentence you most want remembered.","short":"Slow down on what matters, pause after it, and know your one point before you begin.","f":["How do I stop saying um?","How do I tell a good story?","How do I sound more confident?"],"src":[{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 4"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 7"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 20"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 144, 880"}],"w":["vg","ch"]},{"i":"t-quiet","c":"voice","t":"My voice is too quiet","q":["quiet voice","too quiet","soft spoken","people cant hear me","speak up","mumble","mumbling","louder","project my voice"],"n":["vg-volume","vg-instrument","vve-vocal-power","vg-practice"],"b":"Volume is a dial, so learn where yours sits and practise turning it. Vinh maps volume from 1 to 10: Whisper (1–2), Close Conversation (3–4), Everyday Leader (5–6), Projection (7–8) and Emergency (9–10). Ask three people where your normal volume sits — quiet speakers are often a 3 when the moment needs a 5. Then practise moving between zones. Breathe low so the sound comes from your body, not a strained throat. Vanessa adds control: a fuller, steady volume reads as capable.","say":"\"Can everyone hear me okay at the back?\"","tip":"Breathe low, open your mouth a little wider, and send your last word to the furthest person.","short":"Treat volume like a dial — find your usual zone and practise moving up to \"Everyday Leader\".","f":["How do I sound more confident?","My voice is monotone","How do I speak in front of a group?"],"src":[{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 6"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 2"},{"l":"Cues ch. 7"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 14"}],"w":["vg","vve"]},{"i":"t-monotone","c":"voice","t":"My voice is monotone","q":["monotone","boring voice","flat voice","voice sounds dull","expressive voice","vocal variety","intonation","melody"],"n":["vg-melody","vg-instrument","vg-tonality","vg-rate","vve-vocal-variety"],"b":"Your voice is an instrument, and monotone just means playing a few keys. Vinh's five foundations are tonality, rate, melody, volume and pause, and he says most people use only about a fifth of their pitch range. Try his siren: read a passage starting at your lowest note, climb slowly to your highest, then back down. His other trick: your face drives your tone, so let the feeling show on your face and your voice will follow. Vanessa suggests marking important scripts with where to pause, smile and emphasise.","say":"\"And then — you won't believe this — it worked.\"","tip":"Pause before the surprise, then lift your pitch on \"worked\". Exaggerate in practice so normal feels lively.","short":"Fix a flat voice by using more of your range — sirens, pauses, and letting your face lead your tone.","f":["How do I sound more confident?","How do I tell a good story?","My voice is too quiet"],"src":[{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 5"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 2"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 3"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 4"},{"l":"Cues ch. 8"}],"w":["vg","vve"]},{"i":"t-video","c":"voice","t":"How do I come across well on video calls?","q":["video call","zoom","teams call","online meeting","on camera","webcam","virtual meeting","video"],"n":["vg-video","vve-imagery","vg-expression","vve-triple-nod"],"b":"Bring a bit more of everything than you would in person. Vinh's checklist: camera on at hello, torso and hands in shot, good light and sound, and a little more energy than feels natural, since the screen flattens you. Let your face move — a blank face reads as \"doesn't care\". Vanessa notes your background and picture send warmth or competence signals too, so check what's behind you. Nod visibly while others talk. And for your key point, looking at the lens rather than the faces makes it feel like eye contact.","say":"\"Can I jump in with one thought here?\"","tip":"Look at the lens on your key sentence, smile, and speak about 10% more energetically than in person.","short":"On video: camera on, hands in shot, good light, and a bit more energy than in person.","f":["How do I come across well in work meetings?","What do I do with my hands?","How do I sound more confident?"],"src":[{"l":"@askvinh on YouTube"},{"l":"On Purpose with Jay Shetty"},{"l":"Cues ch. 10"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 11"},{"l":"Cues ch. 4"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 1"}],"w":["vg","vve"]},{"i":"t-story","c":"story","t":"How do I tell a good story?","q":["tell a story","storytelling","good story","stories","anecdote","tell stories better","story structure","my stories fall flat"],"n":["vg-spark-point-link","vg-storytelling","vg-story-bank","vg-stakes","vve-story-stack","vg-boring","ch-mammal","vg-vulnerability"],"b":"Start at the peak, make the stakes clear, and land one point. Vinh's Spark, Point, Link: open at a moment of emotion or action (\"So I'm standing at the gate and my passport's gone…\"), fill in who, what, where and when, make one clear point, then link it to the listener. Say what could be lost so people lean in, and cut detours and \"no wait, it was Tuesday\" corrections. Vanessa's Story Stack adds a struggle and vivid words, then a boomerang back: \"Has that ever happened to you?\" Chase says save the emotional reveal for the end.","say":"\"So I'm standing at the gate, and I realise my passport is still at home.\"","tip":"Relive the moment as you tell it, slow down at the tension, pause, then deliver the turn.","short":"Start at the peak, make the stakes clear, land one point, and link it back to them.","f":["How do I be more interesting?","I talk too fast or ramble","How do I speak in front of a group?"],"src":[{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 20"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 15"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 15–18"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 16"},{"l":"Captivate ch. 10"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 19–20"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 452, 456–461, 473–484"}],"w":["vg","vve","ch"]},{"i":"t-on-spot","c":"speaking","t":"What do I do when I'm put on the spot?","q":["put on the spot","on the spot","impromptu","off the cuff","asked a question suddenly","unexpected question","think on my feet","caught off guard","called on"],"n":["vg-321","vg-pause","ch-composure","ch-plain-speech","vve-own-it"],"b":"Pause, give it a shape, and say it once. Vinh's first move is a breath — a calm pause looks thoughtful, not stuck. Then his 3-2-1 gives instant structure: three steps, two types, or one thing (\"The one thing I'd say is…\"). Picking a shape stops you rambling. Chase's composure rule helps too: don't collapse into apologies or puff up — say your point plainly, then stop talking. If you really don't know, say so and offer to follow up; that sounds far more confident than bluffing.","say":"\"Good question. [pause] The one thing I'd say is…\"","tip":"Breathe out before you start, keep your hands still, and let the last word fall.","short":"When you're put on the spot, pause, pick a shape (three, two or one), and stop when you've said it.","f":["How do I stop saying um?","How do I speak in front of a group?","How do I calm my nerves before a big moment?"],"src":[{"l":"@askvinh on YouTube https://www.youtube.com/@askvinh"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 7"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 154–158, 164"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 453, 462–463"},{"l":"Captivate ch. 11"}],"w":["vg","ch","vve"]},{"i":"t-group-speak","c":"speaking","t":"How do I speak in front of a group?","q":["speak in front of a group","public speaking","presentation","present","speech","give a talk","speak to a crowd","stage fright","toast","best man speech"],"n":["vg-eye-contact","vg-presence","vg-delivery","vg-instrument","vg-pause","ch-performance-mode","ch-nervous-excited","vg-competence-stages"],"b":"Talk to one person at a time, and call your nerves excitement. Vinh's approach is to finish a whole thought with one person, then move to someone else, so it feels like many small conversations. Stand still between points, plan your first line word for word, and pause after anything important. Chase suggests saying \"I'm excited\" rather than \"calm down\" — Alison Wood Brooks's research found that reframing works better — and treating it as connection, not performance. Vinh's reminder: if it feels unnatural, it's just unfamiliar; it gets easier with reps.","say":"\"I want to start with a quick question — hands up if you've ever…\"","tip":"Find one friendly face, deliver your first sentence slowly to them, then pause before moving on.","short":"In front of a group, speak to one person per thought and call your nerves excitement.","f":["How do I calm my nerves before a big moment?","What do I do with my hands?","How do I tell a good story?"],"src":[{"u":"https://doi.org/10.1037/a0035325","l":"Brooks 2014"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 13 (p. 122)"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 8"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 1"},{"l":"Schroeder & Epley 2015, as cited in the handbook"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 2"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 7"}],"w":["vg","ch"]},{"i":"t-disagree","c":"tough","t":"How do I disagree without a fight?","q":["disagree","disagreement","argue","argument","conflict","push back","different opinion","debate","challenge someone","tell them they are wrong"],"n":["all-disagree","ch-resonate","vg-situation-not-person","vg-yes-and","ch-golden-bridge","vve-high-stakes"],"b":"Show you understand their view first, then add yours. Chase calls it getting into their river before you steer the boat: say back the best version of their point so they feel heard. Then use Vinh's \"Yes, and…\" instead of \"yes, but…\", and aim at the situation, not the person — \"this plan\", not \"you\". Vanessa's high-stakes approach is to name the goal you share, which turns it from a fight into a problem to solve together. Finish with Chase's golden bridge: give them a way to change their mind without losing face.","say":"\"I can see why you'd go that way — and I'm worried about one thing. We both want this to land, so can I share it?\"","tip":"Keep your voice low and steady, slow down on \"and\", and keep your hands open.","short":"Disagree by restating their view fairly first, then adding yours with \"and\", aimed at the situation.","f":["Someone is being rude to me","How do I give feedback?","How do I persuade someone honestly?"],"src":[{"l":"DOAC 2026"},{"l":"Instagram Jun 2025"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 1"},{"l":"DOAC 2026 https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9uSXOr-AdAU"},{"l":"On Purpose with Jay Shetty"},{"l":"Vinh on Instagram, Jun 2025, via the Read Anyone handbook"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual p. 51"}],"w":["ch","vg","vve"]},{"i":"t-rude","c":"tough","t":"Someone is being rude to me","q":["rude","someone is rude","disrespect","disrespectful","insult","snide comment","dismissive","condescending","patronising","mean comment","put down"],"n":["ch-composure","vg-situation-not-person","ch-plain-speech","vve-curious-critical","vg-low-status"],"b":"Stay steady and name it plainly — no shrinking, no snapping back. Chase's composure idea is that both collapsing (going small, over-apologising) and posturing (puffing up) give your power away; calm is what looks strong. Vinh's conflict move: acknowledge their emotion first, then say what bothered you about the situation, not what's wrong with them. Vanessa suggests a quick check before reacting: what explanation would you give if you'd done the same? Sometimes it's a bad day, not an attack. If it does need saying, say it once and stop.","say":"\"I can tell you're frustrated, but that came across as dismissive. Can we keep this respectful?\"","tip":"Pause first, keep eye contact, use a level tone, then stop talking — don't soften it with a laugh.","short":"Stay calm and name rude behaviour once, plainly, aimed at what happened, not who they are.","f":["How do I disagree without a fight?","How do I deal with a difficult or annoying person?","I keep getting interrupted"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 154–158, 164"},{"l":"On Purpose with Jay Shetty"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 453, 462–463"},{"l":"Vanessa on TikTok, via Read Anyone handbook §5.1"},{"l":"@askvinh on YouTube"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 14"}],"w":["ch","vg","vve"]},{"i":"t-interrupted","c":"tough","t":"I keep getting interrupted","q":["interrupted","interrupt","interrupting","talked over","cut off","cant get a word in","people talk over me","hold the floor"],"n":["vg-hard-to-interrupt","vve-interruptions","ch-interruptions","vve-anchor-touch"],"b":"Make yourself harder to interrupt, then hold your place calmly. Vinh's take: finish your sentences, keep your volume steady, and keep your body still — people interrupt less when you're clearly mid-thought. Vanessa's toolkit helps: preview up front (\"I've got three points\"), and if someone cuts in, raise a hand slightly, palm out, to bookmark your place. Chase's line is calm and simple: use their name and finish your thought. If you're about to make a longer point, ask for a few minutes up front and promise to hear their input after.","say":"\"One sec, James — let me finish this thought, then I'd love your take.\"","tip":"Keep your pace steady and raise a palm slightly. Speeding up only invites more interruptions.","short":"Signal there's more coming, keep your volume steady, and use their name to finish your thought.","f":["How do I come across well in work meetings?","How do I sound more confident?","Someone is being rude to me"],"src":[{"l":"On Purpose with Jay Shetty"},{"l":"Cues ch. 5"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 454–455"}],"w":["vg","vve","ch"]},{"i":"t-feedback","c":"tough","t":"How do I give feedback?","q":["give feedback","feedback","criticism","criticise","tell someone they did badly","performance conversation","constructive criticism","correct someone"],"n":["vve-feedback","vg-situation-not-person","vve-believe","vve-pygmalion","ch-plain-speech"],"b":"Lead with belief, be specific, and aim at the work. Vanessa's approach: start by saying you believe in them, describe exactly what happened, then ask how you can help it not happen again. Research on \"wise feedback\" found notes framed around high standards and belief in the person were taken up far more. Vinh says keep it about the situation — \"this report\", not \"you\". Chase's plain-speech habit matters here: no hedging or burying the point; say it once, kindly and clearly. Expecting the best from people tends to bring it out.","say":"\"I'm telling you this because I know you can hit a high bar — the intro needs to be shorter. How can I help?\"","tip":"Keep your voice warm and level, say the main point once, then pause for their reply.","short":"Give feedback that leads with belief, names one specific thing, and asks how you can help.","f":["How do I disagree without a fight?","How do I say no?","How do I come across well in work meetings?"],"src":[{"u":"https://doi.org/10.1037/a0033906","l":"Yeager et al. 2014"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 2 \"How to Deliver Tough Feedback\""},{"l":"On Purpose with Jay Shetty"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 3 \"Four Words That Fuel Courage\""},{"l":"Captivate ch. 4"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 453, 462–463"}],"w":["vve","vg","ch"]},{"i":"t-say-no","c":"tough","t":"How do I say no?","q":["say no","saying no","decline","turn down","boundaries","set boundaries","people pleaser","people pleasing","refuse","overcommitted"],"n":["vve-say-no","ch-plain-speech","ch-composure","vg-low-status"],"b":"Say no to the thing, yes to the person. That's Vanessa's approach: decline clearly, then offer something smaller you're genuinely happy to do (\"I can't do dinner, but let's grab a coffee\"). Chase's plain-speech rule keeps it short — no pile of excuses, no \"I'll try\", just say it once and stop. Over-explaining invites negotiation. Vinh's point about low-status habits applies too: skip the over-apologising. You can be warm and still mean no.","say":"\"I can't take that on this month — but I'm happy to look over the plan on Friday.\"","tip":"Keep a friendly tone, a level pitch on \"can't\", and don't rush to fill the pause afterwards.","short":"Say no to the thing and yes to the person, briefly and without a pile of excuses.","f":["How do I ask for what I want?","How do I disagree without a fight?","Someone is being rude to me"],"src":[{"l":"Captivate ch. 13"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 453, 462–463"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 154–158, 164"},{"l":"@askvinh on YouTube"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 14"}],"w":["vve","ch","vg"]},{"i":"t-ask-for","c":"tough","t":"How do I ask for what I want?","q":["ask for what i want","be assertive","assertive","assertiveness","ask for a raise","ask for help","stand up for myself","request","speak up for myself","negotiate","get a raise","raise","pay rise","salary","promotion"],"n":["vve-assertive","vve-own-it","ch-plain-speech","ch-composure","vve-vocal-power","vve-franklin"],"b":"Get clear, give a real reason, then ask plainly and stop. Vanessa's assertive approach starts with curiosity — ask what, who and why so you understand the situation — then claim it: \"I need X. Can you do Y?\" She also notes the word \"because\", with a genuine reason, makes requests feel fair. Chase's plain-speech rule: drop \"just\", \"maybe\" and \"sorry to bother you\", say it once, then let the silence work. And asking for advice or help often makes people like you more, not less.","say":"\"I'd like to talk about my pay, because I've taken on the team lead role. Can we review it this month?\"","tip":"Drop your pitch at the end, keep eye contact, and wait through the silence that follows.","short":"Ask clearly with a real reason, drop the hedges, then stop and wait.","f":["How do I say no?","How do I sound more confident?","How do I handle sceptical pushback?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 2 \"Be More Assertive\""},{"l":"Captivate ch. 11"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 453, 462–463"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 154–158, 164"},{"l":"Cues ch. 7"},{"l":"Captivate ch. 12"}],"w":["vve","ch"]},{"i":"t-sceptic","c":"tough","t":"How do I handle sceptical pushback?","q":["sceptical","skeptical","pushback","objections","they doubt me","dont believe me","resistance","hard sell","convince a sceptic","objection"],"n":["ch-objections","ch-resonate","vve-nut-job","ch-golden-bridge","ch-plain-speech"],"b":"Get curious about the doubt instead of fighting it. Chase's five S's, used honestly: Sympathise (\"I felt the same\"), Socialise (\"lots of people ask that\"), Select (\"Which part worries you most?\"), Show the real picture, and Secure (\"Does that help?\"). Vanessa's NUT Job is similar for worried people: Name the fear, Understand it, then Transform it into a solution together. Speak plainly — jargon makes sceptics more sceptical. And leave them a golden bridge: they can change their mind, or say no, without losing face.","say":"\"That's a fair concern. Which part worries you most?\"","tip":"Relax your shoulders, keep your voice curious rather than defensive, and pause after asking.","short":"Meet doubt with curiosity — ask which part worries them, show the real picture, and respect a no.","f":["How do I persuade someone honestly?","How do I disagree without a fight?","How do I talk to my boss or someone important?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 538–539"},{"l":"DOAC 2026 https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9uSXOr-AdAU"},{"l":"Captivate ch. 13"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual p. 51"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 453, 462–463"}],"w":["ch","vve"]},{"i":"t-boss","c":"work","t":"How do I talk to my boss or someone important?","q":["boss","manager","senior people","important people","vip","ceo","executive","intimidating person","leadership","impress my boss"],"n":["vve-vips","vg-low-status","ch-authority","ch-acss","vve-high-stakes","all-voice-competence"],"b":"Be calm, brief and genuinely curious. Vanessa's advice for VIPs is to skip the fan compliment and ask for their take on something real. Vinh says watch the low-status habits — over-apologising, shrinking your body, rushing — and start with a pause instead of \"sorry\". Chase sees authority as self-work: comfort first, then calm, gratitude and visible enjoyment of the moment. If you have an update, lead with the headline. And asking a senior person for advice tends to make you look more competent, not less.","say":"\"Quick headline: the launch is on track. I'd love your take — what's the biggest risk you see?\"","tip":"Lead with your conclusion, keep an unhurried pace, and hold eye contact as you finish.","short":"With important people, skip the flattery, lead with the headline, and ask for their real take.","f":["How do I come across well in work meetings?","How do I sound more confident?","How do I ask for what I want?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 2 \"How to Talk to Your Boss (or VIPs)\""},{"l":"@askvinh on YouTube"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 14"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 32, 122–198"},{"l":"DOAC 2024 https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RvjR9GM2kX8"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 938–943"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 \"How to Handle High-Stakes Conversations\""}],"w":["vve","vg","ch"]},{"i":"t-difficult","c":"tough","t":"How do I deal with a difficult or annoying person?","q":["difficult person","annoying person","deal with difficult people","toxic person","hard to get along with","irritating","frustrating colleague","pet peeve"],"n":["ch-people-are-reasons","vve-curious-critical","vve-nut-job","vve-disliked","vg-situation-not-person","all-six-needs"],"b":"Get curious about what's driving them, then set limits where needed. Chase's view is that behaviour has reasons — people carry things and wear masks, so they aren't broken; something is driving them. His six needs make good listening prompts: are they after recognition, control, or acknowledgement of how hard it's been? Treat that as a guess, never a label. Vanessa's check: what explanation would you give if you'd done the same thing? If they're worried, name the fear and solve it together. And accept that not everyone will click with you.","say":"\"I want this to work for both of us. What matters most to you here?\"","tip":"Use a calm, slower voice and a neutral face, and let them finish before you respond.","short":"With difficult people, get curious about what's driving them and set clear limits.","f":["Someone is being rude to me","How do I disagree without a fight?","What are the six needs?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 297–302"},{"l":"Vanessa on TikTok, via Read Anyone handbook §5.1"},{"l":"Captivate ch. 13"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 2"},{"l":"On Purpose with Jay Shetty"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 366–372"},{"u":"https://www.youtube.com/shorts/YzJI8GTHteQ","l":"Chase's needs Short"}],"w":["ch","vve","vg"]},{"i":"t-apologise","c":"tough","t":"How do I apologise well (and stop over-apologising)?","q":["apologise","apologize","apology","say sorry","sorry","over apologising","i say sorry too much","made a mistake","messed up","my fault"],"n":["vg-low-status","vg-practice","ch-composure","ch-plain-speech","vve-anti-perfect","vve-franklin"],"b":"Apologise once, properly, for real mistakes — and drop the reflex \"sorry\" everywhere else. Vinh lists over-apologising as a low-status habit that makes people take you less seriously; his swap is to start with a pause, not an apology, or to say \"thank you\" instead (\"Thanks for waiting\" rather than \"Sorry I'm late\"). Chase's composure point fits: grovelling is collapse. When you do need to apologise, name exactly what you did, how it affected them, and what you'll do now — then stop. Vanessa notes that owning a small flaw makes you easier to warm to, not weaker.","say":"\"I got that wrong, and it cost you an evening. I've fixed it, and it won't happen again.\"","tip":"Say it slowly and plainly, with eye contact. One clear apology lands; three nervous ones don't.","short":"Apologise once, specifically, for real mistakes — and swap reflex sorries for a pause or a thank-you.","f":["How do I sound more confident?","How do I ask for what I want?","Someone is being rude to me"],"src":[{"l":"@askvinh on YouTube"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 14"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 154–158, 164"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 453, 462–463"},{"l":"Captivate ch. 12"},{"l":"Read Anyone handbook §5.1"}],"w":["vg","ch","vve"]},{"i":"t-end","c":"endings","t":"How do I end a conversation?","q":["end a conversation","end conversation","exit","leave a conversation","wrap up","wrap it up","get away politely","say goodbye","finish talking","walk away","leave politely","leave a party","leave the party","leave early","how to leave"],"n":["vve-exit","all-end-on-purpose","vve-thinking-of-you","ch-gratitude","vve-connector"],"b":"End on purpose, while it's still good — that's kinder than waiting for it to fizzle. Research by Mastroianni found most conversations don't end when either person wants them to, so you're probably doing them a favour. Vanessa's three-step exit: mention something in their future, give a specific compliment, then say what you're off to do. Chase counts gratitude among his core traits, and a quick thank-you leaves a warm last impression. If you can, introduce them to someone else on your way out, so nobody's left standing alone.","say":"\"Good luck with the launch next week — I loved hearing about it. I'm going to grab a drink. Talk soon!\"","tip":"Smile, keep the energy up through the last line, and actually move — don't hover after you've said goodbye.","short":"End on a high: mention their future, give a specific compliment, and say what you're off to do.","f":["How do I get away from someone who won't stop talking?","How do I network without feeling fake?","How do I keep in touch with people?"],"src":[{"u":"https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2011809118","l":"Mastroianni et al. 2021"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 \"How to Gracefully End a Conversation\""},{"l":"Read Anyone handbook §4"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 magic phrase 1"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 148–153"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 2 \"How to Be a Connector\""},{"l":"Captivate ch. 4"}],"w":["vve","ch"]},{"i":"t-escape","c":"endings","t":"How do I get away from someone who won't stop talking?","q":["wont stop talking","cant get away","trapped in conversation","stuck talking","escape a conversation","talker","chatterbox","monopolise","get out of a conversation"],"n":["vve-exit","vve-connector","all-end-on-purpose","ch-gratitude"],"b":"Wait for a breath, then end kindly and firmly. Vanessa's gentle sequence for a monologue: the Fish (open your mouth slightly, as if about to speak), then the Bookmark (raise a hand, palm out), and use the pause to say a warm goodbye. Mention something they said so they know you listened. An introduction is the easiest way out: bring someone in, then step back. A quick thank-you keeps it warm, not abrupt. Don't invent elaborate excuses; one clear line and moving your feet does it.","say":"\"I've loved hearing about your trip — I promised I'd catch up with Sam, so I'll let you go.\"","tip":"Touch your glass or bag as you speak, turn your feet towards your exit, and keep smiling as you go.","short":"Wait for a breath, sum up something they said, then name your next move and go.","f":["How do I end a conversation?","How do I introduce people?","How do I join a group conversation?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 \"How to Gracefully End a Conversation\""},{"l":"Mastroianni et al. 2021, as cited in the handbook"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 2 \"How to Be a Connector\""},{"l":"Captivate ch. 4"},{"l":"Read Anyone handbook §4"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 148–153"}],"w":["vve","ch"]},{"i":"t-join-group","c":"social","t":"How do I join a group conversation?","q":["join a group","join a conversation","group conversation","group of people","break into a group","walk up to a group","circle of people"],"n":["vg-entering-group","vve-context-cues","vve-break-script","vg-generosity-energy"],"b":"Approach a group that's open, listen first, then add to what's already being said. Vinh's way in: hover near the group, catch someone's eye, wait for a natural gap, then add to the topic they're already on rather than changing it. Vanessa suggests a short, warm opener that connects to the setting. Once you're in, ask someone a question so the group naturally opens up.","say":"\"Mind if I join you? This sounded too good to miss.\"","tip":"Approach from the side, smile at whoever's speaking, and keep your first line short and light.","short":"Join a group at the edge, listen first, then add to their topic.","f":["How do I start a conversation?","I'm shy at parties","How do I network without feeling fake?"],"src":[{"l":"@askvinh \"How to enter conversations\" https://www.youtube.com/@askvinh"},{"l":"On Purpose with Jay Shetty"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 \"Never Run Out of Things to Say\""},{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 \"The Weirdest, Best Party\""},{"l":"Read Anyone handbook §5.1"},{"l":"Audible interview and On Purpose, via the Read Anyone inventory (G15)"}],"w":["vg","vve"]},{"i":"t-networking","c":"social","t":"How do I network without feeling fake?","q":["networking","network","networking event","conference","meet people professionally","mixer","business event","schmooze","professional contacts"],"n":["vve-social-game-plan","vve-pq-exciting","vve-connector","vve-prepare-topics","ch-people-are-reasons","vve-exit","all-sincerity"],"b":"Treat it as meeting interesting people, not collecting contacts. Vanessa's Social Game Plan: go to events where you thrive, skip the ones you dread, plan where you'll stand, and bring a friend if you can. Prepare a couple of questions that skip \"What do you do?\" — her favourite is \"Working on anything exciting these days?\" The quickest way to feel less fake is to be useful: introduce people who should meet. Chase's reminder is that people are the point, not the business card. And end conversations on purpose so you're not stuck.","say":"\"Working on anything exciting at the moment?\"","tip":"Keep one hand free, smile before you approach, and follow up within a day with something specific.","short":"Network by picking your events, asking better questions and introducing people — useful, not salesy.","f":["How do I start a conversation?","How do I end a conversation?","How do I introduce people?"],"src":[{"l":"Captivate ch. 1"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 power question 1"},{"l":"Read Anyone handbook §5.1"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 2 \"How to Be a Connector\""},{"l":"Captivate ch. 4"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 \"How to Be Interesting\""},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 297–302"}],"w":["vve","ch"]},{"i":"t-introduce","c":"social","t":"How do I introduce people?","q":["introduce people","introductions","introduce someone","connect two people","how to introduce","mutual friend"],"n":["vve-highlighter","vve-connector","ch-barkeep","vve-name-game"],"b":"Introduce people with a hook, not just names. Vanessa says be a highlighter: introduce people by their best qualities, then give them a shared keyword to talk about — \"You both love climbing!\" It makes them feel looked after and makes you memorable. Chase's Barkeep challenge turns this into a habit: meet a small group and learn their names, then meet a second group, then introduce the two. Soon you're the thing everyone has in common. Once they're talking, step back and let it run.","say":"\"Sam, this is Priya — she just ran her first marathon. Priya, Sam's training for his.\"","tip":"Use an upbeat tone, gesture to each person with an open palm as you say their name, then step back.","short":"Introduce people by their best qualities and give them a shared hook to talk about.","f":["How do I remember names?","How do I network without feeling fake?","How do I end a conversation?"],"src":[{"l":"Captivate ch. 4"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 2 \"How to Be a Connector\""},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 451–452"},{"l":"Captivate ch. 3 (pp. 68–69)"}],"w":["vve","ch"]},{"i":"t-friends","c":"social","t":"How do I make friends?","q":["make friends","making friends","friendship","new friends","lonely","loneliness","no friends","adult friends","find my people"],"n":["vve-friends","all-liking-gap","ch-go-first","ch-insecurities","vg-vulnerability","vve-thinking-of-you"],"b":"Show up repeatedly, go first, and assume people like you more than you think. Vanessa's approach: schedule regular social time, build shared contexts — a class, a club, a regular spot — and try small, low-key invitations. The liking gap research says people usually liked you more than you guessed, so ask again. Chase's go-first principle helps: share something real, and they'll find it easier to share back; people like imperfect people. Vinh adds that real struggles connect more than polished highlights. And reaching out is appreciated more than we expect.","say":"\"I really enjoyed chatting last time — want to grab a coffee next week?\"","tip":"Keep the invite specific and light, with a smile, and don't over-explain it.","short":"Make friends by showing up regularly, going first and reaching out more than feels natural.","f":["How do I keep in touch or reconnect with people?","How do I go deeper?","I'm shy at parties"],"src":[{"u":"https://doi.org/10.1177/0956797618783714","l":"Boothby et al. 2018"},{"u":"https://doi.org/10.1037/pspi0000402","l":"Liu et al. 2023"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 2 \"How to Make Friends\""},{"l":"Read Anyone handbook §4"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 1"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual p. 350"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual p. 132"}],"w":["vve","ch","vg"]},{"i":"t-keep-touch","c":"social","t":"How do I keep in touch or reconnect with people?","q":["keep in touch","reconnect","reach out","rekindle","lost touch","old friend","follow up with someone","stay in touch","catch up"],"n":["vve-thinking-of-you","vve-last-time","vve-friends","ch-go-first","ch-gratitude"],"b":"Reach out first with something specific. Vanessa's \"thinking of you\" message works because it shows you remembered — mention something they told you last time. Research by Liu and colleagues found people underestimate how much others appreciate being reached out to. Chase's go-first principle applies here too: suggest a concrete time rather than \"we should catch up\". If it's been a while, you can simply say you miss them. Small, regular contact matters more than big gestures.","say":"\"I saw this and thought of you — how did the new job turn out?\"","tip":"Send it warmly and briefly, with a question they can answer easily.","short":"Reach out first with something specific, and suggest a concrete time.","f":["How do I make friends?","How do I text better?","How do I thank someone properly?"],"src":[{"u":"https://doi.org/10.1037/pspi0000402","l":"Liu et al. 2023"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 magic phrase 1"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 magic phrase 3"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 2 \"How to Make Friends\""},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual p. 350"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 148–153"}],"w":["vve","ch"]},{"i":"t-texting","c":"social","t":"How do I text better?","q":["texting","text","texts","messaging","dms","reply to a text","text conversation","whatsapp","what to text","dry texter"],"n":["vve-thinking-of-you","vve-last-time","vve-magic-phrases","vg-threads","vve-pq-plans"],"b":"Make your texts specific and easy to reply to. Vanessa's magic phrases work brilliantly in text: \"I was just thinking of you\" (when it's true) and \"Last time you mentioned…\" both show you remember. Ask a question that invites a story, not a yes or no. Vinh's thread idea works in text too — pick up the detail with the most energy in their last message. Keep messages short and warm, not walls of text. And if you want to meet, suggest a time rather than chatting forever.","say":"\"How did the interview go? I've been wondering since you mentioned it!\"","tip":"Read it aloud before sending — if it sounds flat, add one warm word or a question.","short":"Text with something specific and an easy question, then move towards a plan.","f":["How do I keep in touch or reconnect with people?","How do I make friends?","What do I say on a date?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 magic phrase 1"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 magic phrase 3"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 \"Five Magic Phrases\""},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 23, 26"},{"l":"Vinh on TikTok"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 power question 2"}],"w":["vve","vg"]},{"i":"t-date","c":"social","t":"What do I say on a date?","q":["date","first date","dating","romantic","flirting","flirt","crush","date conversation","second date","how to flirt","talk to girls","talk to guys","talk to women","talk to men","someone i fancy","attractive"],"n":["vve-blueprint","vve-signal-liking","vve-pq-exciting","vg-high-low-buffalo","vve-warmth-cues","vve-yums","all-sincerity"],"b":"Be curious, show you're enjoying it, and go a little deeper as you go. Vanessa says your signals of liking are weaker than you think, so turn your warmth up: smile, lean in, react. Skip interview-style questions; start light, then move to goals and what matters to them, at their pace — her Conversation Blueprint. Vinh's High, Low, Buffalo is a playful way in: a high, a low and something random, and you go first. Meet their passions with curiosity, even the odd ones. If you'd like to see them again, say so — warmth plus directness is attractive.","say":"\"I've really enjoyed this. What's something you're looking forward to right now?\"","tip":"Lean in slightly, keep your voice warm and unhurried, and let the pauses breathe.","short":"On a date, be curious, show you're enjoying it, and go deeper at their pace.","f":["How do I go deeper?","How do I be more likeable?","How do I text better?"],"src":[{"l":"Conversation, \"The Conversation Blueprint\""},{"u":"https://www.scienceofpeople.com/conversation/","l":"Science of People: Conversation"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 \"How to Create a Magnetic Presence\""},{"l":"Boothby et al. 2018, as cited in the handbook"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 power question 1"},{"l":"Read Anyone handbook §5.1"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 23"}],"w":["vve","vg"]},{"i":"t-meetings","c":"work","t":"How do I come across well in work meetings?","q":["work meetings","meetings","meeting","speak up in meetings","contribute in meetings","team meeting","standup","workplace","colleagues","at work"],"n":["vg-spark-point-link","vg-hard-to-interrupt","vve-power-cues","ch-plain-speech","vg-wins","vve-ask-good"],"b":"Speak early, keep it short, and land your point. Vinh's Spark, Point, Link keeps you tight: a quick hook, one clear point, then link it to what the meeting needs. Speaking in the first few minutes makes it easier to contribute later. Vanessa's competence cues help: sit up, hands visible, explanatory gestures. Chase says drop the hedges and say it once. When you share a win, Vinh suggests crediting the people who helped. And Vanessa's trick for opening your own meeting: \"What's something good?\" lifts the room.","say":"\"Building on Sam's point — I think the key is the deadline. Can we decide by Friday?\"","tip":"Lean in slightly, keep a steady pace, and let your pitch drop at the end of your point.","short":"In meetings, speak early, land one clear point, and give credit to others.","f":["I keep getting interrupted","How do I sound more confident?","How do I talk to my boss or someone important?"],"src":[{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 20"},{"l":"On Purpose with Jay Shetty"},{"l":"Cues ch. 5"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 453, 462–463"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 \"Use This Icebreaker…\""}],"w":["vg","vve","ch"]},{"i":"t-interview","c":"work","t":"How do I do well in a job interview?","q":["job interview","interview","interviewing","hiring","recruiter","job application","tell me about yourself interview"],"n":["vve-logline","vve-triple-threat","vg-spark-point-link","vg-story-bank","vve-story-stack","all-voice-competence","ch-nervous-excited"],"b":"Prepare stories, not just answers. Pick three or four from your work with real stakes, and shape them Vinh's way: start at the moment of tension, give one clear point, then link it to the job. Vanessa suggests a short, hooky logline for \"Tell me about yourself\" and her Triple Threat for the first seconds: hands visible, Launch Stance and good eye contact. Nervous? Chase's reframe is \"I'm excited\", not \"calm down\". End by asking a real question about the team or the work.","say":"\"One example: we were three weeks behind, so I split the project into two releases — and we shipped on time.\"","tip":"Practise your top stories aloud, and pause after the result so it lands.","short":"In interviews, prepare short stories with clear stakes and a result, and open with a strong first impression.","f":["How do I tell a good story?","How do I sound more confident?","How do I calm my nerves before a big moment?"],"src":[{"u":"https://doi.org/10.1037/a0035325","l":"Brooks 2014"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 \"How to Answer 'What Do You Do?'\""},{"l":"Captivate ch. 2"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 20"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 15–18"},{"l":"Captivate ch. 10"},{"l":"Read Anyone handbook §4"}],"w":["vve","vg","ch"]},{"i":"t-family","c":"social","t":"How do I talk better with my partner or family?","q":["partner","family","spouse","parents","kids","children","relationship","boyfriend","girlfriend","husband","wife","close people","people closest to me"],"n":["vg-listening","vg-traps","vve-appreciation","vve-dreambuilding","ch-heard-seen","ch-one-new-thing"],"b":"Listen to them like someone new, and say the appreciation out loud. Vinh says listening is the bigger half of connection; ask yourself afterwards what seemed to matter most to them. Watch his traps — judging, advising, mind reading — which creep in most with people we know well. Chase's habit is to learn one new thing in every conversation, even with people you've known for years. Vanessa says thank people their way (words, time, help), and when they share a goal, get excited and help them dream it up.","say":"\"I noticed how much effort you put into today — thank you.\"","tip":"Put your phone away, face them fully, and keep your voice soft and warm.","short":"With close people, listen without fixing, learn something new, and say appreciation out loud.","f":["How do I make someone feel heard?","How do I keep in touch or reconnect with people?","How do I thank someone properly?"],"src":[{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 24"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 25"},{"l":"Captivate ch. 8"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 2 \"Uncovering Secret Dreams\""},{"l":"DOAC 2026 https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9uSXOr-AdAU"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 92, 449"},{"l":"Vanessa on X https://x.com/vvanedwards/status/2095555287851913413"}],"w":["vg","vve","ch"]},{"i":"t-get-better","c":"growth","t":"How do I get better at talking to people?","q":["get better at talking","better at talking","improve social skills","social skills","communication skills","better communicator","improve communication","people skills","awkward","socially awkward"],"n":["vg-practice","vg-competence-stages","vg-practical-magic","ch-one-new-thing","ch-acss","vve-social-game-plan","all-follow-ups"],"b":"Practise one small skill at a time, in low-stakes moments. Vinh's method: record a short \"before\" video, stretch by exaggerating one skill, then compare, and finish with \"The one thing I need to work on is…\". Expect the clunky stage — it feels unnatural before it feels normal. Chase says fix comfort before scripts, and set a tiny goal like learning one new thing in every conversation. Vanessa's game plan is to practise where you thrive first. Start with low-stakes people like a barista before conversations that matter.","say":"\"Tell me more about that.\"","tip":"Pick one skill for the week, use it three times a day, and notice what changes.","short":"Get better by practising one small skill at a time in low-stakes moments.","f":["What should I practise every day?","How do I stop saying um?","How do I keep a conversation going?"],"src":[{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 14"},{"l":"@askvinh on YouTube https://www.youtube.com/@askvinh"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect, all chapters"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 92, 449"},{"l":"Vanessa on X https://x.com/vvanedwards/status/2095555287851913413"},{"l":"DOAC 2024 https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RvjR9GM2kX8"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 938–943"}],"w":["vg","ch","vve"]},{"i":"t-daily","c":"growth","t":"What should I practise every day?","q":["practise every day","practice every day","daily practice","exercises","drills","practice routine","voice exercises","warm up","train myself","habit"],"n":["vg-practice","vg-melody","vg-321","vg-story-bank","vg-practical-magic","ch-one-new-thing"],"b":"Five minutes of voice and one small real-world rep. Vinh's daily practice: a siren (slide from your lowest note to your highest and back), a few minutes reading aloud with pauses instead of fillers, and one tiny rep like a \"Good morning!\" with eye contact and a smile. Practise his 3-2-1 for off-the-cuff answers — three steps, two types, or one thing. Add one story a day to your Story Bank. Chase's addition: in one real conversation, learn one new thing about the person.","say":"\"Good morning! How's your day going so far?\"","tip":"Record one practice a week and listen back — it's the fastest way to hear progress.","short":"Spend five minutes on voice drills and do one small real conversation challenge every day.","f":["How do I get better at talking to people?","How do I stop saying um?","My voice is monotone"],"src":[{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 14"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 5"},{"l":"@askvinh on YouTube https://www.youtube.com/@askvinh"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 15–18"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect, all chapters"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 92, 449"},{"l":"Vanessa on X https://x.com/vvanedwards/status/2095555287851913413"}],"w":["vg","ch"]},{"i":"t-six-needs","c":"ethics","t":"What are the six needs?","q":["six needs","6 needs","needs map","human needs","what people need","motivations","significance","approval","acceptance","intelligence","pity","strength"],"n":["all-six-needs","ch-heard-seen","ch-values-map","lo-needs-steering"],"b":"Chase Hughes describes six common social needs: significance, approval, acceptance, intelligence, pity or hardship, and strength or power. In Read Anyone they're listening prompts, never labels. Use them to notice what someone might want acknowledged: their effort, their competence, how hard something was. Then check by asking, rather than assuming. Read Anyone leaves out Chase's methods for using needs to steer people covertly; the honest use is to meet the need openly.","say":"\"That sounds like it took a lot to pull off. How did you do it?\"","tip":"Ask it with real curiosity and a slow pace, then let them tell you what matters to them.","short":"The six needs are prompts for noticing what someone might want acknowledged — never labels.","f":["Is this manipulation?","How do I make someone feel heard?","What does Read Anyone leave out?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 366–372"},{"u":"https://www.youtube.com/shorts/YzJI8GTHteQ","l":"Chase's needs Short"},{"l":"Read Anyone handbook §6"},{"l":"DOAC 2026 https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9uSXOr-AdAU"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 383–386"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 373–377 (excluded, H50)"}],"w":["ch"]},{"i":"t-ethics","c":"ethics","t":"Is this manipulation?","q":["manipulation","manipulative","is this manipulation","ethical","ethics","dark psychology","influence ethically","is it fake","covert","sincerity test"],"n":["all-sincerity","lo-overview","all-six-needs","ch-people-are-reasons"],"b":"It depends on whether you'd be happy for them to know exactly what you're doing. Read Anyone uses a sincerity test: would they agree, knowing your full intent, that it was in their interest? Listening, asking good questions, being warm and speaking clearly pass easily. Covert techniques — hidden commands, pressure, exploiting needs or fears — don't, so they're left out. All three teachers ultimately emphasise connection: making people feel seen, heard and comfortable.","say":"\"I want to be upfront about why I'm asking.\"","tip":"If you'd feel awkward explaining your technique to them, use a more honest approach.","short":"It's not manipulation if they'd agree it was in their interest, knowing your full intent.","f":["What does Read Anyone leave out?","How do I persuade someone honestly?","What are the six needs?"],"src":[{"l":"Read Anyone handbook §2"},{"l":"Read Anyone technique inventory §3c https://github.com/mhccog/read-anyone/blob/main/research/techniques-inventory.md"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 366–372"},{"u":"https://www.youtube.com/shorts/YzJI8GTHteQ","l":"Chase's needs Short"},{"l":"Read Anyone handbook §6"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 297–302"}],"w":["ch"]},{"i":"t-persuade","c":"ethics","t":"How do I persuade someone honestly?","q":["persuade","persuasion","convince","influence","get someone to agree","sell","sales","win an argument","change someones mind","get a yes"],"n":["ch-values-map","ch-their-words","ch-decision-style","vve-primary-value","vve-own-it","ch-objections","all-sincerity"],"b":"Find out what matters to them, then show plainly how your idea serves it. Chase listens for the end goal behind what people want — often heard as \"so I can finally…\" — and speaks in their words so he's understood. Vanessa's Primary Value idea: people value different things (status, information, service, money), so offer what means something to them. Give a real reason; \"because\" makes requests feel fair. Answer doubts with the true picture. And accept no — if they'd feel steered knowing your full intent, it isn't honest.","say":"\"You said you want to finally get your evenings back. This would save about five hours a week — does that help?\"","tip":"Use their exact words, slow down on the benefit, and pause so they can respond.","short":"Persuade honestly by finding what matters to them and showing plainly how your idea serves it.","f":["How do I handle sceptical pushback?","Is this manipulation?","How do I ask for what I want?"],"src":[{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 383–386"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 393–400"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 378–382"},{"l":"Captivate ch. 9"},{"l":"Captivate ch. 11"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 538–539"},{"l":"Read Anyone handbook §2"}],"w":["ch","vve"]},{"i":"t-left-out","c":"ethics","t":"What does Read Anyone leave out?","q":["left out","excluded","what is left out","dark techniques","hypnosis","nlp","embedded commands","covert influence","mind control","behavior ops","interrogation"],"n":["lo-overview","lo-embedded","lo-hypnosis","lo-lie-spotting","lo-needs-steering","lo-confusion","all-sincerity"],"b":"Read Anyone leaves out 41 techniques that fail its sincerity test: 40 from Chase Hughes and one from Vanessa Van Edwards. They include hidden commands, hypnotic language, confusion tactics, exploiting needs or fears, interrogation methods and lie-spotting. For each, there's an honest alternative: say what you want plainly, ask instead of steering, and acknowledge needs openly. What remains is the practical, respectful core of their work.","say":"\"Here's what I'm asking for, and why.\"","tip":"Say it plainly, with steady eye contact and an open palm.","short":"Read Anyone leaves out techniques that fail the sincerity test, and offers honest alternatives.","f":["Is this manipulation?","How can I tell if someone is lying?","What are the six needs?"],"src":[{"l":"Read Anyone technique inventory §3c https://github.com/mhccog/read-anyone/blob/main/research/techniques-inventory.md"},{"l":"Read Anyone handbook §2"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 523–531, 819–823 (excluded, H60)"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 499–522, 566–581 (excluded, H59, H63)"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 205–278 (excluded, H48)"},{"l":"Captivate ch. 6 (excluded, V91)"},{"l":"Bond & DePaulo 2006, as cited in the handbook"}],"w":["ch","vve"]},{"i":"t-gears","c":"growth","t":"How do I adapt to different people?","q":["adapt","different people","read the room","gears","style","introvert","extrovert","personality types","adjust to people","versatile"],"n":["vg-what-they-want","vg-match-mirror-lead","vve-speed-read","vve-verbal-chameleon","ch-decision-style"],"b":"Notice what they want, then shift gears. Vinh says most people want one of four things from a conversation: information (be clear and short), connection (warmth), harmony (keep the peace) or fun. Match their pace first, then lead gently. Vanessa adjusts to personality — more detail for a planner, more novelty for someone open — and balances warm words (\"thanks\", \"together\") with competent ones (\"plan\", \"results\") to suit them. Chase notes people decide for different reasons: practicality, value, novelty or what others do. You don't need one style; you need range.","say":"\"Do you want the quick version or the full story?\"","tip":"Ask with a smile, then match their answer in your pace and how much detail you give.","short":"Adapt by spotting what someone wants — information, connection, harmony or fun — and shifting gears.","f":["How do I read people and body language?","What does mirroring mean, and does it work?","How do I be more likeable?"],"src":[{"l":"Vinh's blog https://vinhgiang.com/blog/how-to-build-rapport-with-absolutely-anyone"},{"l":"DOAC 2025 https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oIiv_335yus"},{"l":"Captivate ch. 7"},{"l":"Cues ch. 9"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 378–382"}],"w":["vg","vve","ch"]},{"i":"t-good-news","c":"listening","t":"How do I respond when someone shares good news?","q":["good news","celebrate","congratulate","someone got promoted","they got engaged","respond to good news","share in excitement","promotion"],"n":["vve-dreambuilding","vg-three-followups","ch-display","vve-so-x","vve-awe"],"b":"Get excited with them and ask for the story. Research by Shelly Gable found that active, enthusiastic responses to good news strengthen relationships, while a flat \"nice\" quietly drains them. Vanessa's Dreambuilding: get excited, bring ideas, and check back later. Vinh's three-follow-ups rule fits perfectly: how did it happen, how did it feel, what's next? Chase's Display step is to let your face show it before you say a word. Then be easily impressed out loud: \"You're so ___!\"","say":"\"That's amazing! How did you find out?\"","tip":"Let your face light up first, then lift your pitch on the question.","short":"Celebrate good news actively — show it on your face and ask for the story.","f":["How do I give a good compliment?","How do I keep a conversation going?","How do I be more likeable?"],"src":[{"u":"https://doi.org/10.1037/0022-3514.87.2.228","l":"Gable et al. 2004"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 2 \"Uncovering Secret Dreams\""},{"l":"On Purpose with Jay Shetty https://podcasts.happyscribe.com/on-purpose-with-jay-shetty/vinh-giang-how-to-communicate-so-people-actually-listen-to-you"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 92, 129"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 1 magic phrase 5"},{"l":"Conversation, Level 3 \"The Single Most Important Connection Ingredient\""}],"w":["vve","vg","ch"]},{"i":"t-thanks","c":"likeability","t":"How do I thank someone properly?","q":["thank someone","thanks","gratitude","say thank you","show appreciation","thank you"],"n":["vve-appreciation","ch-gratitude","vve-highlighter","vve-compliments"],"b":"Make it specific, say what it meant, and thank them their way. Instead of \"Thanks for your help\", name what they did and the difference it made. Vanessa's Appreciation Matrix says people feel appreciated differently — through words, time, help or small gifts — so match theirs. Chase counts gratitude as one of his authority traits: people who show it come across as grounded and warm. And research by Kumar and Epley found people underestimate how much a thank-you note means to the person receiving it.","say":"\"Thank you for staying late yesterday — it meant I could actually sleep before the pitch.\"","tip":"Make eye contact, keep it brief and sincere, and pause after so they can take it in.","short":"Thank people specifically, naming what they did and why it mattered.","f":["How do I give a good compliment?","How do I keep in touch or reconnect with people?","How do I end a conversation?"],"src":[{"u":"https://doi.org/10.1177/0956797618772506","l":"Kumar & Epley 2018"},{"l":"Captivate ch. 8"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 148–153"},{"l":"Captivate ch. 4"},{"l":"Vanessa on X, Sep 2026 https://x.com/vvanedwards/status/2096268031156478239"},{"l":"Read Anyone handbook §5.1"}],"w":["vve","ch"]},{"i":"t-teachers","c":"about","t":"Who are Vanessa, Chase and Vinh?","q":["who are the teachers","vanessa van edwards","chase hughes","vinh giang","who is vanessa","who is chase","who is vinh","authors","teachers","experts"],"n":["vve-about","ch-about","vg-about","all-read-anyone"],"b":"Vanessa Van Edwards runs Science of People and writes about charisma and conversation using research. Chase Hughes is a behaviour expert whose work includes reading people and influence; Read Anyone keeps his practical people skills and leaves out covert tactics. Vinh Giang is a communication coach and former magician who focuses on voice, presence, listening and storytelling. Together: what to say, how to read people, and how to deliver it.","say":"\"Teach me the one skill that would help me most.\"","tip":"Pick one teacher's tool to try today, then try another tomorrow.","short":"Vanessa covers warm conversation, Chase reading people, and Vinh voice and presence.","f":["Who is Vanessa Van Edwards?","Who is Chase Hughes?","Who is Vinh Giang?"],"src":[{"l":"scienceofpeople.com https://www.scienceofpeople.com/"},{"l":"Read Anyone handbook §3"},{"l":"The Behavior Ops Manual"},{"u":"https://nci.university/","l":"NCI University"},{"l":"Read Anyone handbook §3, §5.3"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect, contents"},{"u":"https://vinhgiang.com/before-we-forget-how-to-connect","l":"Vinh's book page"}],"w":["vve","ch","vg"]},{"i":"t-general","c":"general","t":"General advice for any conversation","q":["general advice","any conversation","conversation tips","tips","advice","help","how to talk to people","talk to people"],"n":["all-warmth-first","all-follow-ups","vg-listening","vg-pause","all-sincerity"],"b":"Be warm, curious, clear and calm. Start with a real smile and a question that invites a story. Listen for the detail with energy and ask a follow-up. Let your face show you're listening. When it's your turn, make one point, pause, and hand it back. If things feel tense, slow down and name what's happening. Ask whether your approach would pass the sincerity test.","say":"\"Tell me more about that.\"","tip":"Slow down by about 10%, breathe at full stops and keep your face responsive.","short":"In any conversation: warmth, curiosity, a follow-up, one clear point and calm pacing.","f":["How do I start a conversation?","How do I keep a conversation going?","How do I become a better listener?"],"src":[{"l":"Read Anyone handbook §4"},{"l":"Fiske, Cuddy & Glick 2007 https://doi.org/10.1016/j.tics.2006.11.005"},{"l":"Huang et al. 2017 https://www.hbs.edu/faculty/Pages/item.aspx?num=52115"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 24"},{"l":"Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 7"},{"l":"Read Anyone handbook §2"}],"w":["vve","vg"]}]};
